import type { KbChunk } from './kb.js';

export const KB_DATA: KbChunk[] = [{"id":"kb-care-001","topic":"care","title":"Everyday upholstery care","aliases":["upholstery care","caring for upholstered furniture","maintenance tips","how to keep sofa looking new","furniture care basics"],"text":"Simple habits keep upholstery looking good for longer. Vacuum regularly with an upholstery attachment, including crevices where crumbs and grit collect, since grit wears fibres. Flip and rotate loose cushions every week or two so wear and fading spread evenly. Plump down and fibre cushions often. Keep furniture out of strong direct sun or use blinds, and keep it away from radiators and heat vents, which dry out leather and fabrics. Blot spills right away. Avoid sitting on arms or the front edge of cushions, which stresses seams and foam. Have pieces professionally cleaned periodically, following the fabric's cleaning code."},{"id":"kb-care-002","topic":"care","title":"Handling spills and stains","aliases":["spill cleaning","stain removal upholstery","blotting","coffee spill sofa","wine on couch","emergency stain"],"text":"When something spills on upholstery, act fast. Blot, do not rub, with a clean white cloth or paper towel, working from the outside of the spill toward the centre so it does not spread. Scrape off solids gently. Check the cleaning code before using any cleaner: use water-based cleaners only on W or W/S fabrics, solvent cleaners only on S or W/S fabrics, and nothing but vacuuming on X. Use as little liquid as possible and test in a hidden area first. Do not use household products not designed for upholstery, or heat such as a hair dryer, which can set stains. For tough or set stains, a professional upholstery cleaner is the safest choice."},{"id":"kb-care-003","topic":"care","title":"Leather care","aliases":["leather care","cleaning leather sofa","leather conditioner","how to care for leather","leather cracking","leather fading"],"text":"Leather care depends on the type of leather. For all types, dust or vacuum regularly, blot spills immediately with a clean cloth, keep the piece out of direct sun and away from heat sources, which dry and crack leather, and avoid household cleaners, solvents and wet cloths unless the leather maker allows them. Pigmented leathers are easiest, usually tolerating gentle cleaning with leather-specific products. Aniline and nubuck are more delicate and stain easily, so they need extra care and specialist products. Conditioning can help some leathers stay supple; follow the manufacturer's guidance. Professional leather technicians can repair scratches, cracks and colour loss."},{"id":"kb-care-004","topic":"care","title":"Vinyl and faux leather care","aliases":["vinyl care","cleaning vinyl","faux leather care","PU care","vinyl cracking","cleaning faux leather"],"text":"Vinyl and faux leather are easy to clean but can be damaged by the wrong products. Wipe with a soft cloth and mild soap and water, then wipe with clean water and dry. Avoid harsh solvents, abrasive pads, and some disinfectants unless the material maker approves them, because they can strip protective coatings and lead to cracking. Body oils, sunscreen and hair products can break down vinyl over time, so regular gentle cleaning helps. Keep pieces out of strong sun and away from heat. For commercial or healthcare vinyl, follow the manufacturer's list of approved cleaners and disinfectants."},{"id":"kb-care-005","topic":"care","title":"Cushion care: foam, down and fibre","aliases":["cushion care","plumping cushions","flipping cushions","rotating cushions","down cushion care","cushion maintenance"],"text":"Loose cushions last longer with a little attention. Flip and rotate seat cushions regularly, especially the most-used seat, so wear spreads. Plump down and feather cushions daily or weekly, and fibre-filled back pillows often, by pressing and fluffing them to redistribute fill. Foam cushions benefit from flipping more than plumping. Avoid sitting on the edge of cushions or letting children jump on them. If a cushion cover twists or shifts, unzip it and realign the insert. When cushions stay flat even after plumping, the fill or foam is likely worn out, and new inserts can make a big difference."},{"id":"kb-care-006","topic":"care","title":"Sun and heat damage prevention","aliases":["sun damage","fading prevention","furniture near window","heat damage","UV protection furniture","radiator damage"],"text":"Sunlight is one of the main causes of fabric and leather damage. It fades colours, weakens fibres and threads, and dries out leather and vinyl. To protect furniture, place it out of direct sunlight where possible, use curtains, blinds or UV-filtering window film, and rotate cushions and throws. Solution-dyed fabrics resist fading best for sunny rooms. Heat from radiators, baseboard heaters, fireplaces and heating vents can dry out leather and some fabrics, so keep furniture a little distance away. In Canadian winters, dry indoor air can also affect leather and wooden frames, and a humidifier can help."},{"id":"kb-care-007","topic":"care","title":"Pet hair and pet-related care","aliases":["pet hair removal","pets on furniture","cat scratching sofa","dog on couch","pet odour sofa","pet accidents"],"text":"For pet households, vacuum often with an upholstery or pet-hair tool, or use a rubber glove or lint roller to lift hair. Throws on favourite pet spots protect fabric and are easy to wash. Trim pets' nails to reduce snags, and give cats scratching posts away from the sofa. Clean accidents quickly using a cleaner suited to the fabric's cleaning code; enzymatic pet cleaners can help with odours on water-safe fabrics, but test first. Odours that have soaked into foam and padding may not come out with surface cleaning alone, and replacing the cushion inserts or padding during reupholstery is often the real fix."},{"id":"kb-care-008","topic":"care","title":"How long does reupholstery take?","aliases":["how long does reupholstery take","turnaround time","lead time","when will my sofa be ready","reupholstery timeline","wait time"],"text":"Turnaround time varies by shop and job. The time spent on the piece in the workroom is often a matter of days for a chair or sofa, but the overall wait is usually longer because of the shop's queue and fabric ordering. Fabric from a supplier can take anywhere from days to several weeks, and backorders happen. Many shops quote total timelines in weeks rather than days. Busy seasons, such as before holidays, can extend waits. Stitch should never promise a completion date; instead it should give the shop's current typical lead time if staff have provided one, and explain that fabric availability is the biggest variable."},{"id":"kb-care-009","topic":"care","title":"Reupholster or replace?","aliases":["reupholster or replace","is reupholstery worth it","should I reupholster","buy new or reupholster","worth fixing","value of reupholstery"],"text":"Reupholstery usually makes sense when the frame is solid, especially older hardwood frames, hand-tied springs, quality construction, a design the customer loves, or sentimental value. It also lets customers choose the exact fabric and adjust comfort. Replacing often makes more sense for inexpensive furniture with particle board frames, broken mechanisms, or designs that are hard to take apart, where reupholstery could cost more than the piece is worth. Quality reupholstery is not always cheaper than new furniture, but it is often comparable to buying a similarly well-made new piece. Keeping furniture in use is also less wasteful. A shop inspection is the best way to decide."},{"id":"kb-care-010","topic":"care","title":"What affects the price of reupholstery","aliases":["reupholstery cost factors","what affects price","why is reupholstery expensive","price factors","quote factors","cost of upholstery"],"text":"Reupholstery prices depend on several factors. Labour is usually the biggest part, and it rises with the size and complexity of the piece: tufting, channelling, skirts, curves, show wood, many cushions, welt and nailheads all add time. Fabric cost depends on yardage and price per yard; patterns and velvets need more. Repairs to the frame, springs or webbing add cost, and these may only be found after stripping. New foam, down or other fill adds materials cost. Extras like pick-up and delivery, refinishing wood, or rush service also affect totals. Stitch should explain these factors but leave actual prices to the shop's price list or staff."},{"id":"kb-care-011","topic":"care","title":"Stitch pricing rule: never invent prices","aliases":["price policy","no made up prices","quote policy","how much does it cost","price question","estimate ranges"],"text":"When customers ask how much something costs, Stitch should use only prices or ranges the shop has provided in its own price list or instructions, and clearly label them as typical ranges, not quotes. If the shop has not provided a price, Stitch should not guess or use general internet figures; instead it should explain what affects the price, collect photos and details, and offer to have staff follow up with an estimate. Stitch should always say final prices are confirmed after the shop sees the piece and the fabric is chosen. Sales tax, which in Ontario is 13 percent HST, is generally added to taxable goods and services, but Stitch should follow how the shop presents tax on quotes."},{"id":"kb-care-012","topic":"care","title":"Why reupholstery can cost as much as new furniture","aliases":["why is reupholstery so expensive","cost compared to new","cheaper to buy new","reupholstery value"],"text":"Customers are sometimes surprised that reupholstery can cost as much as, or more than, a new sofa from a big-box store. The reason is that reupholstery is skilled handwork: stripping, repairing, rebuilding and covering one piece at a time, using materials chosen for that job. Mass-produced furniture uses factory methods and bulk materials to keep prices low, and is often not built to last as long. A fairer comparison is with new furniture of similar construction quality. Stitch can explain this kindly without pressuring the customer, and let them know the shop can suggest ways to manage cost, such as choosing a less expensive fabric or simplifying details."},{"id":"kb-care-013","topic":"care","title":"Ways to reduce reupholstery cost","aliases":["save money reupholstery","cheaper options","budget reupholstery","lower the cost","affordable upholstery"],"text":"Customers on a budget have options. Choose a plain or small-pattern fabric that uses less yardage and needs no matching. Skip or simplify extras like skirts, welt, tufting or nailheads. Replace only cushion foam or inserts if the cover is still good. Recover only, if the shop confirms the inside is sound. Bring a piece in rather than paying for pick-up, if the shop charges for it. Choose a mid-range fabric instead of a premium one. Ask about fabric remnants for small pieces like dining seats and ottomans. Stitch should present these as options to discuss with staff, not promises of a specific saving."},{"id":"kb-care-014","topic":"care","title":"Can I bring my own fabric?","aliases":["own fabric","bring my own fabric","COM policy","customer fabric","buying fabric online","supply my own material"],"text":"Many shops accept customer's own material, often called COM. Before buying, customers should ask the shop how much fabric is needed for their piece and whether the fabric they like is suitable for upholstery. Useful details to share are the fabric's width, content, pattern repeat, abrasion rating and cleaning code. Buy all the fabric at once from the same dye lot, plus a little extra. Shops may charge a different labour rate for COM, may need to inspect the fabric before starting, and may not guarantee results with fabrics that are not designed for upholstery. Stitch should check the shop's own COM policy before answering specifics."},{"id":"kb-care-015","topic":"care","title":"Choosing a fabric: questions to ask","aliases":["choosing fabric","how to choose upholstery fabric","fabric advice","what fabric should I get","fabric selection"],"text":"Helping a customer choose fabric starts with a few questions. Who uses the piece and how often: children, pets, heavy daily use or occasional? Is it in a sunny spot? What look do they want: smooth, textured, patterned, velvet, leather? Do they prefer easy cleaning? Any allergies? What is their budget? From there, the shop can suggest suitable fabric types, such as performance wovens for families, solution-dyed fabrics for sunny rooms, velvets for tufted pieces, or leather for durability with a classic look. Seeing and touching samples in the room's light helps before ordering. Stitch should collect these answers and pass them to staff."},{"id":"kb-care-016","topic":"care","title":"Fabric samples and swatches","aliases":["fabric samples","swatches","borrow samples","sample books","see fabric","memo samples"],"text":"Customers should always see fabric in person before ordering, since colours on screens and in photos are often misleading. Shops typically have sample books or swatches customers can view in the showroom, and some let customers borrow samples to see them at home in their own light. Larger cuttings show pattern scale better than small swatches. When choosing, customers can test the fabric by rubbing it, checking how pet hair releases, and comparing it with nearby furniture and flooring. Stitch should check the shop's sample policy before telling customers what is available, and offer to arrange a showroom visit."},{"id":"kb-care-017","topic":"care","title":"Dye lots and ordering enough fabric","aliases":["dye lot","colour variation","ordering extra fabric","fabric matching batches","backorder fabric","discontinued fabric"],"text":"Fabric is dyed in batches called dye lots, and colours can differ slightly between lots. This is why all fabric for one piece, or a matching set, should be ordered together, plus a little extra. If a shop runs short and has to order more later, the new lot may not match. Fabrics can also be discontinued or backordered, which can delay a job. Some customers keep leftover fabric for future repairs or matching pillows. Stitch can explain dye lots to customers who want to buy fabric gradually, and advise them to confirm the full yardage with the shop first."},{"id":"kb-care-018","topic":"care","title":"Pick-up, delivery and drop-off","aliases":["pick up and delivery","delivery service","drop off furniture","bring furniture in","moving sofa to shop","transport"],"text":"Most shops either offer pick-up and delivery, often for a fee based on distance and size, or ask customers to bring pieces in. Large sofas and sectionals may need two people and enough clearance through doors, stairs and elevators; condo buildings in Toronto often require elevator booking and sometimes a certificate of insurance from the moving party. Customers should remove cushions' loose items, pet beds and throws before pick-up. Stitch should follow the shop's own delivery policy, collect the address, access details such as stairs or elevators, and preferred times, and never promise a delivery date without staff confirmation."},{"id":"kb-care-019","topic":"care","title":"Deposits and payments","aliases":["deposit","payment terms","pay upfront","payment policy","balance due","how do I pay"],"text":"Payment terms are set by each shop. It is common in upholstery for shops to ask for a deposit before ordering fabric or starting work, because fabric is often cut specifically for the job and cannot be returned, with the balance due on completion or delivery. Stitch should only state deposit amounts, accepted payment methods and refund rules that the shop has provided. If the customer asks something not covered, Stitch should say a staff member will confirm. Stitch should never ask for card numbers in chat; payment should go through the shop's approved channels."},{"id":"kb-care-020","topic":"care","title":"What if hidden damage is found?","aliases":["hidden damage","extra charges","frame damage found","unexpected repairs","quote changed","surprise costs"],"text":"Some problems cannot be seen until a piece is stripped, such as broken frame parts, rotten webbing, damaged springs, previous poor repairs or pest damage. Reputable shops will tell the customer what they found, explain the options and any extra cost, and get the customer's approval before doing additional work. This is why initial estimates, especially from photos, are described as preliminary. Stitch should set this expectation kindly upfront: the estimate covers the work as seen, and the shop will contact them if anything unexpected turns up."},{"id":"kb-care-021","topic":"care","title":"Sentimental and heirloom pieces","aliases":["sentimental furniture","heirloom","family furniture","grandmother's chair","inherited sofa","memory piece"],"text":"Many people bring in furniture that belonged to a parent or grandparent. These pieces carry memories, and customers may care as much about keeping the feel of the piece as about how it looks. Shops can photograph the piece before work, save original fabric or small items found inside, keep original frames and springs where possible, and discuss options to keep the character, such as similar fabric or the original button pattern. Stitch should be warm and attentive to these stories, ask what matters most to the customer, and pass that to staff along with the practical details."},{"id":"kb-care-022","topic":"care","title":"Bed bugs and pest policies","aliases":["bed bugs","pest policy","infested furniture","bugs in sofa","will you take furniture with bugs","pest inspection"],"text":"Bed bugs and other pests can hide in upholstered furniture and spread to a shop and other customers' pieces. Many shops have a policy: inspecting pieces on arrival, refusing pieces with signs of infestation, or requiring treatment by a licensed pest control professional first. Signs of bed bugs include small dark spots, shed skins or live insects in seams and crevices. Stitch should ask, gently and without judgment, whether there have been any pest issues, and share the shop's policy if one is provided. Stitch should not give pest control advice beyond suggesting a licensed professional."},{"id":"kb-care-023","topic":"care","title":"Smoke, odour and water-damaged furniture","aliases":["smoke damage","odour removal","smell in sofa","water damage furniture","flood damage","cigarette smell","insurance claim furniture"],"text":"Smoke, pet odours and other smells often soak into foam, padding and the frame, not just the fabric, so cleaning the surface may not fix them. Reupholstery with new padding and foam usually removes most odours; heavily smoke-affected frames may need cleaning or sealing. Water or flood damage can lead to mould, rusted springs and weakened glue joints, and a shop will need to inspect the piece. For insurance claims, shops can sometimes provide an assessment or estimate in writing, according to their own policy. Stitch should collect details and photos and have staff advise."},{"id":"kb-care-024","topic":"care","title":"Warranty and guarantees","aliases":["warranty","guarantee","workmanship guarantee","what if something breaks","after service","repair after reupholstery"],"text":"Warranty terms are set by each shop. Some shops guarantee their workmanship, such as seams and springs they installed, for a period, while fabric durability is usually covered by the fabric maker, if at all, and customer-supplied fabric is often excluded. Normal wear, pet damage, sun fading and misuse are generally not covered. Stitch should only describe warranty terms the shop has provided, and otherwise say staff will explain. If a customer has a problem after work is done, Stitch should collect photos and details and pass them to staff promptly, with an empathetic response."},{"id":"kb-care-025","topic":"care","title":"Measuring furniture at home for an estimate","aliases":["how to measure my sofa","measure for quote","measurements for estimate","customer measuring guide","sofa dimensions"],"text":"To help with a quick estimate, customers can measure a few things at home with a tape measure. Overall width, from arm to arm at the widest point. Overall depth, from the front of the arm or seat to the back. Overall height, from floor to the top of the back. Also count seat cushions and back cushions, note whether there is a skirt, tufting or nailheads, and note whether there is exposed wood. Photos from the front, side, back and close-ups of details help most. Stitch should reassure customers that rough measurements are fine for an estimate, and the shop will measure exactly before ordering fabric."},{"id":"kb-care-026","topic":"care","title":"Photos that help an estimate","aliases":["photo tips","what photos to send","pictures for quote","photograph furniture","estimate photos"],"text":"Good photos make estimates more accurate. Customers should take photos in good light showing the whole piece from the front, both sides and the back, plus close-ups of the arms, any tufting or pleating, legs or show wood, and any damage like tears, sagging or broken parts. A photo of the underside, if they can tip the piece safely, helps show springs and webbing. If there is a label with a maker's name or materials, a photo of it is useful. Stitch should ask for these politely and explain that clearer photos mean a more accurate preliminary estimate."},{"id":"kb-care-027","topic":"care","title":"Can you fix a sagging sofa?","aliases":["sagging sofa","saggy couch","sofa sinks","seat sag repair","couch dip","sunken seat"],"text":"Yes, sagging is one of the most common repairs. The cause can be worn cushion foam or fill, broken or loose springs or spring clips, stretched webbing, broken frame rails, or worn padding on the deck. Each has a different fix: new cushion inserts, re-clipping or replacing sinuous springs, re-tying coil springs, new webbing, or frame repair. Sometimes it is a combination. Stitch should ask where the sag is, whether it is the cushion or the seat under it (by removing the cushion and pressing the deck), and whether there are noises like squeaks or clunks, then pass that to staff for an assessment."},{"id":"kb-care-028","topic":"care","title":"Can you change foam firmness?","aliases":["firmer cushions","softer cushions","change foam","cushion too soft","cushion too hard","comfort upgrade"],"text":"Shops can usually change how a seat feels by changing the cushion core and wrap. Firmer foam, a firmer core with a soft top layer, or added support beneath can make a seat firmer. Softer foam, a thicker wrap or a down envelope makes it softer and plusher. Cushion thickness also changes seat height, which matters for people who find low seats hard to get out of. Customers often benefit from trying samples or demo cushions in the shop. Stitch should ask what they like and dislike about the current seat, and who uses it most, and pass that to staff."},{"id":"kb-care-029","topic":"care","title":"Do you make custom cushions?","aliases":["custom cushions","new cushions made","bench cushion","window seat cushion","outdoor cushion order","replacement cushions"],"text":"Most upholstery shops make custom cushions for sofas, chairs, window seats, benches, banquettes, boats and patios. To quote, they need the shape and measurements of each cushion, thickness, the type of edge (box, knife edge, waterfall), fill preference, whether it needs a zipper, ties or hook-and-loop fasteners, and the fabric choice. For irregular shapes, a paper template traced from the seat is very helpful. Stitch should collect these details, plus photos, and note whether the cushions are indoor or outdoor, since outdoor cushions need different foam, fabric and thread."},{"id":"kb-care-030","topic":"care","title":"DIY vs professional upholstery","aliases":["DIY upholstery","do it myself","can I do it myself","beginner upholstery","DIY dining chair","DIY or pro"],"text":"Some projects are friendly for beginners, like recovering slip seats on dining chairs, simple ottomans, padded headboards and basic bench cushions. These need a staple gun, fabric, foam and patience. Larger pieces, like sofas, wingbacks, tufted pieces, spring work and anything with complex sewing like welted box cushions, are much harder and need industrial machines and experience. A failed DIY job can waste expensive fabric. Stitch can encourage DIY on simple pieces and offer the shop's services for complex ones, without discouraging customers. Some shops sell materials or offer workshops; Stitch should check what this shop offers."},{"id":"kb-care-031","topic":"care","title":"What happens at a consultation?","aliases":["consultation","in-person visit","showroom visit","home visit","estimate appointment","what to expect"],"text":"At an upholstery consultation, staff look at the piece in person or through photos, check its construction and condition, talk about what the customer wants, and show fabric options. They may discuss changes to style or comfort, and explain any repairs needed. They then give an estimate covering labour, fabric and repairs, along with a timeline. Some shops do home visits for large or built-in jobs, sometimes for a fee. Customers should bring photos, measurements, fabric ideas and, if supplying fabric, the fabric details. Stitch should book according to the shop's availability and policies."},{"id":"kb-care-032","topic":"care","title":"Upholstery for rental and condo living","aliases":["condo furniture","small space sofa","apartment upholstery","Toronto condo","elevator delivery","small sofa"],"text":"Many Toronto customers live in condos or apartments with tight elevators, narrow hallways and building rules. Before pick-up or delivery, they should check elevator size and booking rules, and measure doorways and hallways. Some sofas have removable legs or arms, which helps. Reupholstering an existing piece that already fits can avoid the risk of a new sofa not fitting through the door. For small spaces, customers may want apartment-size pieces, armless chairs, or performance fabrics for durability. Stitch should collect building access details when arranging transport."},{"id":"kb-care-033","topic":"care","title":"How to describe a piece over the phone or chat","aliases":["describe my furniture","identify my sofa","what style is my chair","no photo description","describe upholstery"],"text":"When a customer cannot send photos, Stitch can ask simple questions to understand the piece. Is it a sofa, loveseat, sectional, armchair, dining chair, ottoman or headboard? Roughly how wide is it? Are the seat and back cushions loose or attached? How many cushions? What do the arms look like: rolled, square or none? Is there exposed wood? Are there buttons, tufting or nailheads? Is there a skirt or visible legs? What is the fabric like, and is there any damage? These answers give staff enough for a very rough idea, but Stitch should still explain that photos or a visit are needed for a proper estimate."},{"id":"kb-care-034","topic":"care","title":"Ordering only fabric or supplies","aliases":["buy fabric only","sell foam","buy foam","supplies","cut foam to size","fabric only purchase"],"text":"Some upholstery shops also sell fabric by the yard, foam cut to size, batting, trims and other supplies to DIY customers, while others focus only on their own jobs. Foam cut to size is a common request for cushions and window seats. If the shop sells supplies, Stitch should collect what the customer needs, quantities and measurements, and pass the request to staff, using only prices the shop has provided. If the shop does not sell supplies, Stitch should politely say so and offer the shop's full services instead, without naming other suppliers."},{"id":"kb-care-035","topic":"care","title":"Reupholstering a set of dining chairs","aliases":["dining chair set","recover dining chairs","six chairs","dining set upholstery","chair set quote"],"text":"Dining chair sets are a frequent job. Shops usually quote per chair, so the number of chairs, whether seats are slip seats or stuff-over, and whether backs are upholstered matter most. Fabric for the whole set should be ordered together from one dye lot. Cleanable or performance fabrics are popular for dining. Shops often check and tighten or reglue loose joints at the same time, which may be an extra. Host chairs with arms may be priced differently from side chairs. Stitch should ask for the number of chairs, a photo of one front and back, the seat type, and any wobbles or damage."},{"id":"kb-care-036","topic":"care","title":"Sofa won't fit through the door","aliases":["sofa doesn't fit","moving sofa through door","remove sofa legs","sofa disassembly","tight doorway"],"text":"If a sofa cannot fit through a doorway or stairwell, there are options. Many sofas have removable legs, and some have bolt-on arms or backs. Measuring the diagonal depth and the doorway helps decide if it can be angled through. Some upholsterers can partially take apart a frame, remove the back or arms, and reassemble and re-cover the joins on site, though this is specialist work and not all shops offer it. Stitch should collect the sofa dimensions, the doorway and stair measurements and photos, then pass the request to staff rather than promising it can be done."},{"id":"kb-care-037","topic":"care","title":"Seasonal tips for Toronto homes","aliases":["Toronto climate furniture","winter dry air leather","humidity furniture","seasonal care","Canadian winter upholstery"],"text":"Toronto's climate swings from humid summers to dry, heated winters. Dry winter air can dry out leather and make wooden frames shrink, which can loosen joints; a humidifier and keeping furniture away from heat vents help. Humid summers can make linen and some natural fabrics relax and wrinkle, and damp basements can encourage mould on stored furniture. Outdoor and boat cushions should be cleaned, dried and stored indoors for winter. Road salt and slush tracked indoors can stain fabrics near entryways. These are general tips; customers with specific concerns can ask the shop."},{"id":"kb-care-038","topic":"care","title":"What Stitch should hand to staff","aliases":["handoff to staff","escalation","when to escalate","staff follow up","lead summary","job notes"],"text":"Stitch should pass to shop staff a clear summary whenever a customer wants a quote, booking, or anything Stitch cannot confirm. Include the customer's name and preferred contact method, the piece type and photos or description, measurements if given, what they want done, fabric preferences or whether they have their own fabric, any problems noted such as sagging or odours, special concerns like pets, allergies or sentimental value, timeline, and pick-up or drop-off needs. Stitch should escalate immediately for complaints, warranty issues, commercial specification questions, fire-code questions, and anything involving safety, such as airbags or electrical components."},{"id":"kb-care-039","topic":"care","title":"Tone and boundaries for Stitch","aliases":["Stitch tone","how Stitch should speak","customer service tone","honesty policy","unknown answers","Stitch boundaries"],"text":"Stitch should be warm, patient and plain-spoken, like a knowledgeable person at the front counter. It should explain trade terms in everyday words, ask one or two questions at a time, and avoid jargon unless the customer uses it. Stitch should be honest about what it does not know, and never invent prices, timelines, policies, fabric availability, statistics or regulatory claims. When a question needs a professional judgement, such as frame condition, fire compliance or appraisal value, Stitch should say staff will confirm. Stitch should treat customers' homes, finances and sentimental attachments with respect and discretion."},{"id":"kb-care-040","topic":"care","title":"Explaining an estimate to a customer","aliases":["explain estimate","quote breakdown","line items","understanding quote","what's included in quote"],"text":"A typical upholstery estimate is broken into parts: labour for the reupholstery itself; fabric, given as yardage times the price per yard, or COM if the customer supplies it; materials such as foam, batting, down, trims, welt cord and dust cover; repairs, such as frame, spring or webbing work; extras like skirts, tufting, nailheads, refinishing or leg replacement; pick-up and delivery; and tax. Stitch can explain each line in plain words when asked, using the figures on the shop's actual quote, and should mention that items found during stripping may be added only with the customer's approval."},{"id":"kb-glossary-001","topic":"glossary","title":"Welt (piping, cording)","aliases":["welt","piping","cording","welting","single welt","cord trim","self welt","contrast welt","corded seam"],"text":"Welt is a thin tube of fabric wrapped around a filler cord and sewn into a seam so the edge looks crisp and holds its shape. In North America it is usually called welt, welting or cording; in the UK and much of Europe it is called piping. Canadian shops use all three words. Welt outlines cushions, arm fronts, inside and outside backs, and skirts. When it is made from the same fabric as the piece it is called self welt; when it uses a different colour or material it is contrast welt. Welt also protects seams from wear, because the raised cord takes the rubbing instead of the stitching. Customers who want a softer, more modern look sometimes ask to leave the welt off, which is called a knife-edge or plain seam."},{"id":"kb-glossary-002","topic":"glossary","title":"Double welt","aliases":["double welt","double piping","double cord","double welting","twin welt"],"text":"Double welt is two cords wrapped side by side in one strip of fabric, making a flat, finished trim about half an inch wide. It is glued, or sometimes stapled through the middle and then hidden, over raw fabric edges where the upholstery meets exposed wood, such as on dining chairs, show-wood frames, chair backs and the bottom edge of sofas without skirts. It does the same job as gimp but uses the cover fabric, so it blends in. UK shops may call it double piping. It is usually made in the shop with a special double-welt sewing foot, though ready-made versions exist. Hot glue is commonly used to apply it, so it can be removed later when the piece is recovered again."},{"id":"kb-glossary-003","topic":"glossary","title":"Welt cord (filler cord)","aliases":["welt cord","filler cord","piping cord","cotton cord","poly cord","paper cord","5/32 cord"],"text":"Welt cord is the filler rope inside welt. It comes in cotton, polyester, foam and twisted paper, and in several diameters. A thin cord, often around five thirty-seconds of an inch, is the common size for furniture; jumbo or large welt uses thicker cord for a bolder line. Polyester and foam cords do not shrink or rot, so they are preferred for cushions that might be cleaned and for outdoor or marine work. Cotton cord is traditional and gives a firm line but can shrink if washed. Paper cord is cheap and firm but breaks down with moisture. When quoting welt, shops count the running length needed, usually in yards or metres, which then adds to the fabric yardage."},{"id":"kb-glossary-004","topic":"glossary","title":"Deck (platform)","aliases":["deck","platform","seat deck","decking","deck cloth","deck fabric","platform cloth"],"text":"The deck, also called the platform, is the upholstered surface a loose seat cushion sits on. It is built over the springs with padding and covered in deck cloth, a cheaper, sturdy fabric, often a cotton or poly blend in a colour close to the cover fabric. The front band that shows below the cushion is usually covered in the real cover fabric and sewn to the deck cloth, so the expensive fabric is only used where it can be seen. Some customers ask for a self-deck, meaning the whole deck in the cover fabric, which uses more yardage. A sagging or lumpy deck usually points to tired springs or padding underneath rather than to the cushion alone."},{"id":"kb-glossary-005","topic":"glossary","title":"Eight-way hand-tied springs","aliases":["eight-way hand-tied","8-way hand tied","hand-tied coils","hand tied springs","tied coil springs","eight way tie"],"text":"Eight-way hand-tied is a traditional seat system where individual steel coil springs are stitched or clipped to webbing or a base, then tied to each other and to the frame with strong twine. Each coil is tied front to back, side to side and on both diagonals, so the knots pull it in eight directions and keep it upright and level. It is slow, skilled work and is considered a mark of quality in higher-end furniture, giving a supportive, resilient seat that can last for decades and can be retied. Not every seat sold as hand-tied is tied eight ways; some are four-way or have fewer ties. Shops often re-tie old systems rather than replace them if the coils themselves are sound."},{"id":"kb-glossary-006","topic":"glossary","title":"Coil springs (seat and back coils)","aliases":["coil springs","spiral springs","seat coils","back coils","double-cone springs","hourglass springs","open coils"],"text":"Coil springs are spirals of steel wire used in seats and backs. Traditional upholstery coils are usually double-cone, shaped like an hourglass, so they compress evenly. Seat coils are made from heavier gauge wire than back coils, because seats carry body weight; back coils are softer and lighter. Coils are sized by height and wire gauge, and a lower gauge number means thicker, stiffer wire. In tied systems the coils are fixed at the bottom to webbing or steel bands and tied at the top. Coils can lose height or break over time, and a seat with a few bad coils can often be fixed by replacing only those, rather than the whole system."},{"id":"kb-glossary-007","topic":"glossary","title":"Pocketed coils (Marshall units)","aliases":["pocket coils","pocketed springs","Marshall units","encased coils","innerspring units","spring units"],"text":"Pocketed coils are small springs each sewn inside its own fabric pocket and joined in rows or a mat. In upholstery they are sometimes called Marshall units, a name taken from early innerspring construction, and the term is used loosely for any pocket-spring unit. They are used inside seat cushions, wrapped in foam and down, to give a bouncy, mattress-like feel, and in some seat decks. Because each coil moves independently, they conform to the body. They add weight and cost compared with a solid foam core. If a customer says an old cushion feels lumpy or has springs inside, it may be a spring-core cushion, and the shop can rebuild it the same way or switch to foam."},{"id":"kb-glossary-008","topic":"glossary","title":"Sinuous springs (S-springs, zig-zag, no-sag)","aliases":["sinuous springs","S-springs","zig-zag springs","zigzag springs","no-sag springs","serpentine springs","flat springs","wiggle springs"],"text":"Sinuous springs are long strips of steel wire bent into a continuous S or zig-zag shape. They run from the front rail to the back rail of a seat, or from top to bottom of a back, and are fixed with metal clips. They go by many names: S-springs, zig-zag springs, no-sag springs, serpentine springs. They are joined side to side with small helical springs or wire links so they move together. Sinuous springs are lower profile and cheaper than hand-tied coils, and they are used in most modern sofas. Well-made sinuous systems with good wire gauge and correct arc are comfortable and durable. Common problems are broken clips, pulled-out nails and springs that have lost their arc."},{"id":"kb-glossary-009","topic":"glossary","title":"Spring clips and helicals","aliases":["spring clips","sinuous clips","helical springs","helicals","spring links","tie wires","stabilizer wires","clip nails"],"text":"Spring clips are metal brackets nailed or stapled to the frame that hold the ends of sinuous springs. Many have a paper or plastic lining to stop squeaks. When a clip breaks or the nails loosen, the spring pops free and the seat suddenly feels like it has a hole, which is one of the most common sofa repairs. Helical springs, often just called helicals, are small tension springs that link sinuous springs to each other or to the side rails so the whole deck acts as one surface. Some systems use bent wire links or tie wire instead. Replacing clips and helicals is a fairly quick repair compared to rebuilding a spring system."},{"id":"kb-glossary-010","topic":"glossary","title":"Spring twine (laid cord)","aliases":["spring twine","laid cord","tying twine","jute twine","hemp twine","polyester twine","spring cord"],"text":"Spring twine is the strong cord used to tie coil springs to each other and to the frame. Laid cord is the heavier type used for the main ties; lighter twine is used for stitching springs to webbing and burlap. Traditional twine is jute, hemp or flax; modern shops often use polyester or nylon twine because it does not rot and holds tension longer. Old jute twine dries out and snaps, which is a major reason hand-tied seats fail. When a shop re-ties springs it normally replaces all of the twine, not just the broken pieces. Button twine, used for tufting, is a thinner, very strong cord and is a separate product."},{"id":"kb-glossary-011","topic":"glossary","title":"Edge wire","aliases":["edge wire","spring edge wire","border wire","edge rod","spring edge","roll edge spring"],"text":"Edge wire is a heavy steel wire bent to follow the shape of the seat front, and sometimes the sides, and fastened to the tops of the outer coil springs with clips or twine. It creates a spring edge, so the front of the seat flexes and feels soft instead of sitting on a hard wooden rail. A seat built this way is called a spring-edge seat. Edge wires can bend, break or come loose from their clips, which makes the front of the seat dip or feel uneven. Replacing or re-clipping the edge wire is often part of a hand-tied spring rebuild. Many modern sofas with sinuous springs have a hard front edge instead, or a short wire across the front."},{"id":"kb-glossary-012","topic":"glossary","title":"Jute webbing","aliases":["jute webbing","webbing","upholstery webbing","black and white webbing","English webbing","herringbone webbing","cotton webbing"],"text":"Jute webbing is a strong woven strap, usually about three and a half inches wide, stretched tightly across a frame in a basket-weave pattern to support springs or padding. It is the foundation of traditional upholstery: coil springs are stitched to it, or in unsprung pieces like dining chairs and footstools the stuffing sits directly on it. Webbing is stretched with a webbing stretcher and fixed with tacks or staples, with the end folded back for strength. Quality varies; tightly woven jute or linen-blend webbing lasts longer. Old webbing that has sagged or torn is a common reason seats feel saggy. Heavier steel webbing straps are sometimes used under springs in seats instead."},{"id":"kb-glossary-013","topic":"glossary","title":"Elastic webbing (Pirelli webbing)","aliases":["elastic webbing","rubber webbing","Pirelli webbing","Pirelli straps","resilient webbing","elastomeric webbing"],"text":"Elastic webbing is a stretchy rubber-based strap used instead of springs, especially in mid-century and modern chairs with slim frames. Many people still call it Pirelli webbing, after the company that popularised rubber webbing in the mid-twentieth century, but the name is used generically. It is fixed under tension to the rails with staples or metal clips that slot into grooves. Different grades suit seats and backs. Over time the rubber dries, cracks and loses stretch, so original webbing on vintage mid-century chairs is often brittle and needs replacing. Installing it at the right stretch matters: too loose and the seat sags, too tight and it can stress the frame. Shops follow the webbing maker's stretch guidance."},{"id":"kb-glossary-014","topic":"glossary","title":"Burlap (hessian)","aliases":["burlap","hessian","jute cloth","spring burlap","tarpaulin","scrim"],"text":"Burlap is a coarse, plain-woven cloth made from jute. North Americans, including most Canadian shops, call it burlap; in the UK and Australia it is called hessian. In upholstery it is stretched over tied springs to give the padding something to sit on, and it is used over webbing, as a base for stuffing, and to cover the inside of arms and backs before padding. Heavier grades are used over springs and lighter grades for stuffing layers. Scrim is a lighter, more open-weave cloth used in traditional stitched work. Burlap can rot or dry out in old furniture; when a piece is stripped, any brittle or torn burlap is normally replaced."},{"id":"kb-glossary-015","topic":"glossary","title":"Gimp (and galloon)","aliases":["gimp","gimp braid","galloon","braid trim","decorative braid","gimp tacks","trim braid"],"text":"Gimp is a narrow decorative braid used to hide staples or tacks and raw fabric edges where upholstery meets exposed wood, for example around the seat of a French chair or the back panel of a show-wood sofa. It is usually glued on, sometimes with small gimp tacks at the ends. Galloon is a related flat, woven trim, and the two words are often used interchangeably in shops. Gimp comes in many colours and patterns, from simple scroll braids to ornate antique-style designs. Double welt made from the cover fabric is the usual alternative when a customer wants a quieter look. Gimp can be colour-matched to the fabric or chosen to contrast."},{"id":"kb-glossary-016","topic":"glossary","title":"Fringe, cord and trims","aliases":["fringe","bullion fringe","brush fringe","tassel fringe","onion fringe","decorative cord","lip cord","flange cord","trimmings","passementerie"],"text":"Trims, also called passementerie, are decorative extras added to upholstery and soft furnishings. Bullion fringe is long twisted loops, often used along the bottom of a skirt or a traditional sofa in place of a skirt. Brush fringe is short, cut, fluffy fringe. Tassel and onion fringe add small hanging ornaments. Decorative cord can be sewn into seams; lip cord or flange cord has a flat tape edge so it can be sewn into a seam like welt. Trims are usually priced by the yard or metre and are a separate line on a quote. They can be delicate, so they suit low-traffic or decorative pieces better than family sofas, pet homes or commercial seating."},{"id":"kb-glossary-017","topic":"glossary","title":"Nailhead trim (decorative nails)","aliases":["nailhead","nailheads","nail head trim","decorative nails","upholstery nails","brass nails","nail trim","close nailing","spaced nailing"],"text":"Nailhead trim is a row of decorative round-headed nails along an edge of upholstery, such as an arm front, a seat rail or the border of a headboard. The nails come in finishes like antique brass, nickel, bronze, pewter and black, and in different head sizes. They can be placed touching each other, called close nailing or French nailing, or spaced apart at even intervals. Traditional nails are individually hammered with a nylon- or leather-faced hammer to avoid denting the heads. Nailheads are both decorative and functional, holding fabric in place and replacing gimp or welt. Close nailing takes a lot of time, so it can add noticeably to labour cost on a quote."},{"id":"kb-glossary-018","topic":"glossary","title":"Nailhead strip","aliases":["nail strip","nailhead strip","nail trim strip","faux nailhead","strip nails","ready-made nailhead"],"text":"A nailhead strip is a flexible metal band stamped to look like a row of individual decorative nails touching each other. Only every fifth nail or so is a real nail that goes into the frame, so it installs much faster than placing nails one by one. It is the common choice on modern furniture and many headboards. Strips are cheaper in labour, very straight and even, but a close look shows the joined backing, and they bend less neatly around tight curves. Individual nails look more traditional and can follow any line. When a customer asks for nailhead trim, the shop should confirm whether they want individual nails or strip, since time and cost differ."},{"id":"kb-glossary-019","topic":"glossary","title":"Tack strip (cardboard stripping)","aliases":["tack strip","cardboard tack strip","cardboard stripping","back tacking strip","tack strip board","blind tacking strip","chipboard strip"],"text":"Tack strip is a narrow strip of cardboard or fibre board, usually about half an inch wide, used for back-tacking. Fabric is laid face down, the strip is placed along the seam line, stapled through, and then the fabric is flipped over the strip. The result is a straight, sharp, invisible edge with no visible staples. It is used along the top of outside backs and outside arms, the top of inside backs, and anywhere a clean straight line is wanted. Many shops just call it cardboard. It does not bend well around curves, so flexible metal versions or hand-stitching are used there. Some suppliers sell plastic versions that resist moisture."},{"id":"kb-glossary-020","topic":"glossary","title":"Flexible metal tack strip (curve-ease, flex-grip)","aliases":["curve-ease","curve ease","flex-grip","flexgrip","flexible tack strip","metal tack strip","pronged strip","curved tack strip"],"text":"Flexible metal tack strip is a thin, bendable steel strip with prongs or teeth that holds fabric invisibly along curves and edges. Shop names include curve-ease and flex-grip, which began as trade names but are now used loosely for any similar product. It bends to follow arms, wingbacks and rounded outside backs, where cardboard tack strip will not go. The fabric is pushed onto the prongs, the strip is folded closed and then tapped down so the fabric edge disappears. It saves hand blind-stitching on curves. The teeth are sharp, so staff handle it carefully, and it must be removed with care when the piece is stripped later."},{"id":"kb-glossary-021","topic":"glossary","title":"Ply-grip (metal closure strip)","aliases":["ply-grip","ply grip","plygrip","metal tack strip","pronged tack strip","closure strip","outside back strip"],"text":"Ply-grip is a rigid metal strip lined with sharp teeth, folded lengthwise, used to close the last edge of an outside back or outside arm without hand stitching. The strip is stapled to the frame along the edge, the fabric is pressed onto the teeth and tucked into the fold, and the strip is hammered shut so the edge looks like a neat folded seam. Like curve-ease, the name began as a trade name but is used generically in shops. It works best on straight or gently curved edges. Its advantage is speed and a very tight, clean finish; the downside is that it can be harder to remove, and fabrics that fray easily need care."},{"id":"kb-glossary-022","topic":"glossary","title":"Upholstery tacks","aliases":["tacks","upholstery tacks","blued tacks","cut tacks","gimp tacks","tack hammer","magnetic hammer","improved tacks"],"text":"Upholstery tacks are short, sharp, flat-headed nails that were the main way of fixing fabric, webbing and burlap to frames before staples became common in the twentieth century. They are often blued or sterilised steel and sized by number, with larger numbers being longer. A tack hammer, often magnetic on one end, lets the upholsterer pick up and set tacks one-handed. Tacks are still used in traditional and antique work, especially on old frames where repeated staple holes would weaken the wood, and some restorers prefer them because they come out more cleanly. Gimp tacks are small tacks with tiny heads used to fix trim. Old tack holes help date and trace the history of a frame."},{"id":"kb-glossary-023","topic":"glossary","title":"Upholstery staples","aliases":["staples","upholstery staples","pneumatic stapler","air stapler","staple gun","crown staples","fine wire staples"],"text":"Most modern upholstery is fixed with fine-wire staples shot from a pneumatic stapler driven by compressed air. Staples are sized by crown width and leg length; longer legs are used for webbing and heavy layers, shorter ones for fabric near show wood. Staples are fast and hold well, but every recovering adds more holes, and a frame that has been recovered many times can become chewed up along the tacking rails. Removing old staples carefully is a big part of stripping time. For outdoor and marine work, stainless steel or Monel staples are used because ordinary steel rusts and stains fabric. Hand staplers are fine for small DIY jobs but not production work."},{"id":"kb-glossary-024","topic":"glossary","title":"Skirt (valance)","aliases":["skirt","valance","sofa skirt","chair skirt","flounce","bottom skirt"],"text":"A skirt is a fabric panel that hangs from the bottom of a sofa or chair toward the floor, hiding the legs and the underside. Some people call it a valance or flounce. Skirts are usually lined and sewn with welt at the top, and they sit a little above the floor so they do not drag. Common styles are kick-pleat, box-pleat, gathered, and dressmaker. A skirt softens a piece and makes it look more traditional; removing a skirt and showing legs makes the same piece look lighter and more modern. Adding or removing a skirt is a common request during reupholstery, and adding one uses extra fabric and labour."},{"id":"kb-glossary-025","topic":"glossary","title":"Kick pleat","aliases":["kick pleat","kick-pleat skirt","corner pleat skirt","inverted corner pleat","tailored skirt"],"text":"A kick pleat skirt is a tailored, straight skirt with an inverted pleat at each corner, so the corners open slightly when bumped and then fall back flat. It is one of the most common skirt styles on traditional and transitional sofas and chairs. Long pieces like sofas may also have pleats along the front at the cushion joins to break up the length. The pleat is usually backed with a separate underlay piece of fabric. Kick pleat skirts use more fabric than a plain skirt because of the folded-in pleats. They need careful measuring so the pleats land exactly at the corners, and patterned fabric must be matched across each pleat."},{"id":"kb-glossary-026","topic":"glossary","title":"Dressmaker skirt","aliases":["dressmaker skirt","dressmaker details","waterfall skirt","straight skirt with underlay","tailored dressmaker"],"text":"A dressmaker skirt is a straight, tailored skirt with the corners left open over a hidden underlay, rather than folded into a pleat, giving a clean couture look. The term dressmaker is also used more broadly for fine tailoring details borrowed from clothing, such as button tabs, contrast welt, decorative stitching or ties. A dressmaker skirt usually starts a few inches below the seat rather than at the bottom, and is lined and weighted to hang straight. Because the openings show the underlay, the underlay must be cut and matched carefully. It is mostly seen on slipper chairs, settees and designer pieces, and it tends to take more labour than a simple kick pleat."},{"id":"kb-glossary-027","topic":"glossary","title":"Box pleat and gathered skirts","aliases":["box pleat","box-pleated skirt","gathered skirt","ruffled skirt","shirred skirt","bunched skirt","ruched skirt"],"text":"A box pleat skirt has regular folded pleats all the way around, each folded so the front face looks like a flat box. It uses a lot of fabric, often around two to three times the finished length, and gives a tailored but generous look. A gathered or ruffled skirt is fabric drawn in loosely along the top for a soft, cottage or romantic look; shirring and ruching are related gathering techniques used on panels and arms. Both styles need careful spacing and, for patterns, planning so motifs fall nicely across the pleats. When quoting, the yardage for these skirts should be calculated from the actual pleat or gather ratio rather than a standard skirt allowance."},{"id":"kb-glossary-028","topic":"glossary","title":"Waterfall (waterfall edge)","aliases":["waterfall","waterfall edge","waterfall front","waterfall cushion","waterfall back","bullnose","wrap-around front"],"text":"Waterfall describes fabric that flows in one continuous piece over an edge, with no seam or welt at the front. A waterfall seat or cushion has the top fabric running over the front edge and down, so it looks smooth and rounded. A waterfall back has the inside back fabric rolling over the top. The look is soft, modern and casual. It is sometimes called a bullnose edge, especially when the edge is noticeably rounded. Because there is no seam to hold the shape, the foam and wrap need to be shaped well and the fabric must have the right grain direction. Plains, small patterns and fabrics without a strong nap work best."},{"id":"kb-glossary-029","topic":"glossary","title":"Boxing (box strip)","aliases":["boxing","box strip","box border","band","boxing strip","gusset","cushion border","side band"],"text":"Boxing is the strip of fabric that forms the sides of a cushion, seat or back, joining the top and bottom panels so the shape has depth, like the sides of a box. On a box cushion the boxing runs all the way around, usually with welt at the top and bottom seams. On tight seats and backs, a boxing band can run along the front or top edge. Sewers also call it a gusset or border, especially in the UK. The zipper for a cushion is usually set into a back section of the boxing. Boxing height equals the finished cushion thickness, and its fabric must be cut so patterns and stripes line up with the top panel."},{"id":"kb-glossary-030","topic":"glossary","title":"Facing (arm facing, arm panel)","aliases":["facing","arm facing","arm panel","arm front","front panel","scroll panel","show panel","front post panel"],"text":"A facing is the piece that covers the front of an arm, or sometimes the front of a back post or wing. On many sofas it is a padded wooden panel shaped like the arm, covered in fabric and welt, then screwed or nailed onto the arm front after the arm is upholstered. On others the arm front fabric is pleated or sewn directly. Facings let the upholsterer get a crisp, outlined front even on rounded scroll arms. Shops sometimes say arm panel or front panel. During stripping, facings are removed first and kept as patterns, because their shape is hard to recreate. A damaged or missing facing board can be remade from plywood or particle board."},{"id":"kb-glossary-031","topic":"glossary","title":"Crown","aliases":["crown","crowning","dome","crowned seat","loft","cushion crown"],"text":"Crown is the gentle dome or rise in the middle of a seat, cushion or back, so it looks full and rounded instead of flat or sunken. Upholsterers create crown by adding extra padding, a slightly oversized foam or a polyester wrap, or by building up the stuffing in the centre of traditional seats. Good crown also helps the fabric stay tight and prevents wrinkles. A cushion with no crown can look tired even when new. Customers sometimes describe it as wanting cushions to look plumper. Shops create crown on seat cushions by cutting the foam a little bigger than the cover and wrapping it, so the cover is filled snugly."},{"id":"kb-glossary-032","topic":"glossary","title":"Tight seat","aliases":["tight seat","fixed seat","attached seat","no-cushion seat","upholstered seat","bench seat"],"text":"A tight seat is a seat with no loose cushion: the padding and fabric are built directly onto the frame and springs, so it cannot be removed. Many tuxedo sofas, mid-century pieces, banquettes and dining chairs have tight seats. They look tailored and stay neat, since there are no cushions to plump or slide. The tradeoff is that wear and spills cannot be fixed by flipping a cushion, and replacing the seat fabric means reupholstering that part of the frame. A tight seat can be a single bench or split into sections with welt or tufting. Converting a tight seat to a loose cushion, or the reverse, is possible but changes the frame work and fabric needs."},{"id":"kb-glossary-033","topic":"glossary","title":"Loose cushion","aliases":["loose cushion","loose seat cushion","removable cushion","seat cushion","reversible cushion","zippered cushion"],"text":"A loose cushion is a separate seat or back cushion that sits on the deck or against the back and can be lifted out. Most sofas and many chairs have loose seat cushions. They are usually reversible, with fabric on both sides, so they can be flipped and rotated to spread wear. They normally have a zipper in the back boxing so the insert can be replaced. Loose cushions are the easiest part of a sofa to refresh: new foam or new fill can transform how a piece sits without touching the frame. When customers say their sofa sags, the shop checks whether the problem is the cushion, the deck padding, or the springs underneath."},{"id":"kb-glossary-034","topic":"glossary","title":"Tight back","aliases":["tight back","fixed back","upholstered back","attached back upholstery","no-pillow back"],"text":"A tight back is a back that is upholstered directly onto the frame with no loose pillows. The padding and fabric are fixed in place, often with tufting, channelling or a smooth, pulled-tight finish. Chesterfields, tuxedo sofas, camelbacks and many mid-century designs have tight backs. They look crisp and tailored, need no plumping and do not slide, but they cannot be flipped or replaced separately. Comfort depends heavily on how the back is built: springs, foam layers and angle all matter. A customer who finds a tight back too hard can sometimes have it softened with more padding during reupholstery, or add a few loose scatter pillows instead."},{"id":"kb-glossary-035","topic":"glossary","title":"Loose pillow back","aliases":["loose pillow back","loose back","loose back cushions","pillow back","scatter back","throw pillow back","cushion back"],"text":"A loose pillow back sofa has separate back cushions that rest against the frame and can be lifted off. They are often filled with feathers, down, fibre or a blend, so they are soft and casual. They need regular plumping and can slump or slide, but they are easy to replace, refill or re-cover. A scatter back is a looser version with many smaller pillows instead of full-width back cushions. When quoting, each loose back pillow counts as a cushion, with its own fabric and fill cost. Customers who dislike constant plumping sometimes switch to attached pillow backs or a tight back during reupholstery."},{"id":"kb-glossary-036","topic":"glossary","title":"Attached pillow back","aliases":["attached pillow back","attached back","sewn-in back cushions","semi-attached back","fixed pillow back","pillow-top back"],"text":"An attached pillow back looks like loose back cushions but is sewn or fastened to the frame so it cannot be removed. It gives the soft, cushioned look of a pillow back with less slumping and no cushions sliding around, which suits families and people who prefer less maintenance. The fill is often fibre or a fibre and foam mix in internal pockets, which can flatten over time; refilling it usually means opening a seam or zipper on the back. Some styles are semi-attached, held by a strip of fabric at the bottom. When a customer asks about converting to an attached back, the frame shape, fill and fabric yardage all need checking."},{"id":"kb-glossary-037","topic":"glossary","title":"Box cushion","aliases":["box cushion","boxed cushion","square cushion","rectangular cushion","boxed seat cushion"],"text":"A box cushion is a cushion with a top panel, a bottom panel and a boxing strip around the sides, giving it straight sides and a defined thickness, like a shallow box. It is the most common seat cushion shape. The seams are usually finished with welt, top and bottom. Box cushions can be rectangular or follow a slight taper to fit between arms. Fill choices include foam wrapped in polyester, foam with a down envelope, or spring cores. When measuring, the shop records the width at front and back, depth and thickness, plus any curves, because arms rarely leave a perfect rectangle. A box cushion is reversible if both faces are in show fabric."},{"id":"kb-glossary-038","topic":"glossary","title":"T-cushion","aliases":["T-cushion","T cushion","tee cushion","T-shaped cushion","T-back cushion","extended front cushion"],"text":"A T-cushion is a seat cushion whose front corners extend forward beyond the arms, so from above it looks like a wide letter T. It is used on sofas and chairs where the seat projects past the inside of the arms, which is common on traditional and roll-arm styles. A box cushion, by contrast, is a simple rectangle that sits entirely between the arms. T-cushions take more care to cut and sew, because the boxing has to turn inside corners neatly and the pattern must stay centred on the front. When ordering new cushions or foam, it is important to know whether the cushion is T-shaped, since a box-shaped replacement will not fill the seat properly."},{"id":"kb-glossary-039","topic":"glossary","title":"Knife-edge cushion","aliases":["knife edge","knife-edge cushion","knife edge pillow","pillow edge","no-boxing cushion","throw pillow seam"],"text":"A knife-edge cushion has two panels sewn together around the edge with no boxing, so it is thickest in the middle and thins to a sharp edge, like a typical throw pillow. It may have welt in the seam or not. Knife-edge styling is used for scatter pillows, some back cushions, thin seat pads on dining or outdoor chairs, and some mid-century and modern seats. It gives a softer, more casual look than a box cushion. When a customer wants thin seat pads, the shop should explain the difference between knife-edge and a thin box cushion, because a box cushion keeps its thickness to the edge and sits flatter."},{"id":"kb-glossary-040","topic":"glossary","title":"Channelling (channel tufting, fluting)","aliases":["channelling","channeling","channel tufting","channel back","fluting","vertical channels","channel stitching","piped channels"],"text":"Channelling is upholstery divided into padded vertical or horizontal tubes, like ribs or pipes, often on chair and sofa backs, barrel chairs, headboards and restaurant booths. Each channel is filled separately with foam or stuffing, so the surface stays even and the lines stay straight. Narrow channels are sometimes called fluting. American spelling uses channeling with one l; Canadian and British writing often uses channelling. Channel backs on curved chairs need tapered channels, narrower at the bottom and wider at the top. The work is labour-intensive and needs careful layout, and striped or patterned fabrics need extra thought, so channelling usually adds to the labour portion of a quote."},{"id":"kb-glossary-041","topic":"glossary","title":"Diamond tufting","aliases":["diamond tufting","diamond button tufting","deep diamond tufting","tufted diamonds","button tufting","Chesterfield tufting"],"text":"Diamond tufting is a pattern where buttons are pulled deep into the padding in a grid of offset rows, creating diamond shapes, with folded pleats running between the buttons. It is the classic look of a Chesterfield back and arms, tufted headboards and many ottomans. The pleats are folded in a consistent direction, usually facing downward so they do not collect dust. Diamond tufting needs extra fabric, because each fold takes up material, and careful layout on the foam and fabric before work starts. It is one of the more labour-intensive jobs in upholstery. Velvet, leather and solid colours show tufting best; large patterns get broken up by the folds."},{"id":"kb-glossary-042","topic":"glossary","title":"Biscuit tufting","aliases":["biscuit tufting","biscuit tufts","square tufting","grid tufting","box tufting","mattress tufting","biscuits"],"text":"Biscuit tufting is a grid of squares or rectangles, sitting in straight rows and columns rather than offset diamonds. Each square puffs up slightly, like a biscuit or a cracker. It can be made with buttons at the corners, or stitched without buttons, which gives a sleek modern look. Unlike deep diamond tufting, the squares usually have no folded pleats between them, or only very shallow ones. It is popular on headboards, benches, mid-century pieces and modern sofas. The look is calmer and more geometric than diamond tufting. Biscuit tufting can be made by sewing the cover in squares, or by pulling the fabric down with twine at each corner."},{"id":"kb-glossary-043","topic":"glossary","title":"Blind tufting","aliases":["blind tufting","buttonless tufting","tufting without buttons","hidden tufting","pull-through tufting","stitched tufting"],"text":"Blind tufting is tufting with no visible buttons. The fabric is pulled down into the padding at each tuft point with twine or stitches that are hidden in the fold, or the cover is sewn so the dimples form naturally. It gives a clean, modern version of a tufted look and avoids the risk of buttons popping off, which suits family homes, pets, children's furniture and commercial seating. Blind tufting can be done in diamond or biscuit layouts. Because there is no button to hide the anchor point, the pull has to be very neat. Customers who like tufting but worry about lost buttons often find blind tufting a good compromise."},{"id":"kb-glossary-044","topic":"glossary","title":"Deep buttoning (button tufting)","aliases":["deep buttoning","deep button tufting","button tufting","tufting","buttoning","Victorian buttoning","deep tufting","shallow buttoning"],"text":"Deep buttoning is the traditional British term for deep tufting, where covered buttons are pulled far into thick padding with strong twine, creating deep pleats between them. Tufting is the common North American term for the same family of techniques. Deep buttoning became very popular in Victorian furniture. Shallow buttoning or float buttoning uses buttons on a smoother surface with little or no pleating, mainly for decoration or to hold padding. In shops, people say tufting, buttoning and button tufting interchangeably, then specify the type: diamond, biscuit, blind or channel. When quoting, the number of buttons and the depth matter, because deeper tufts take more fabric and much more time."},{"id":"kb-glossary-045","topic":"glossary","title":"Covered buttons and tufting tools","aliases":["covered buttons","upholstery buttons","button press","button machine","tufting needle","button twine","tufting twine","button backs"],"text":"Upholstery buttons are usually covered in the same fabric as the piece, made in the shop with a button press and dies in several sizes. Some have a wire loop on the back; others use prongs or a tuft. Strong button twine, often nylon or waxed linen, is threaded through the button and pulled through the padding with a long double-pointed tufting needle, then tied off at the back over a small wad of fabric or a washer so it cannot pull through. Leather buttons need thinner leather to wrap neatly. In homes with children or pets, buttons can come off, so shops often use stronger anchors or suggest blind tufting instead."},{"id":"kb-glossary-046","topic":"glossary","title":"Stuffing (filling)","aliases":["stuffing","filling","padding","stuffing layers","first stuffing","second stuffing","loose stuffing","upholstery filling"],"text":"Stuffing, or filling, is the padding material that shapes the upholstered surface. In traditional work, loose fibres such as curled horsehair, coir or tow are worked into shape over burlap in a first stuffing, stitched to build firm edges, then covered with a second stuffing and a cotton or wool layer before the muslin and top cover. Modern upholstery mostly replaces loose stuffing with sheets of foam topped with polyester or cotton batting. Both can be comfortable; the traditional method is slower and more skilled and can last a very long time. On antiques, the existing stuffing is often worth keeping and reusing, which is an ethical as well as practical choice."},{"id":"kb-glossary-047","topic":"glossary","title":"Edge roll (fox edge)","aliases":["edge roll","fox edge","roll edge","prebuilt edge","paper edge roll","fibre edge","edging roll","welt edge roll"],"text":"Edge roll is a ready-made padded roll, usually a core of compressed paper, fibre or foam wrapped in burlap with a flat flange for stapling. It is fixed along the front of a seat rail, the edge of a frame or the top of springs to create a firm, rounded edge that stops the fabric and padding wearing over a sharp wooden or wire edge. Fox edge is a trade term often used for this product. It comes in several diameters. Edge roll is the modern shortcut for a hand-stitched edge built from loose stuffing. It is used widely in both production and custom work, and on dining chairs and seat fronts it gives a neat, crisp line."},{"id":"kb-glossary-048","topic":"glossary","title":"Stitched edge (hard edge, roll edge)","aliases":["stitched edge","hand stitched edge","hard edge","roll edge","French edge","blind stitch edge","top stitch edge","built edge"],"text":"A stitched edge is a firm edge built by hand in traditional upholstery. The upholsterer packs loose stuffing under burlap or scrim, then stitches through it with a long needle and twine in rows. First come blind stitches, which pull stuffing forward without showing on top, then one or more rows of top stitches, which form a crisp, firm roll. The result holds its shape for decades and gives antique seats their square, sharp profile. It is slower than using prefabricated edge roll and is a sign of skilled traditional work. On antiques, a good original stitched edge is often preserved and only re-covered, rather than torn out."},{"id":"kb-glossary-049","topic":"glossary","title":"Blind stitch and slip stitch (ladder stitch)","aliases":["blind stitch","slip stitch","ladder stitch","hand closing stitch","invisible stitch","curved needle stitching","hand sewing"],"text":"In upholstery, closing the last seams by hand is done with a slip stitch, also called a ladder stitch or blind stitch, using a curved needle and strong thread. The needle passes alternately along the folded edge of each piece, so when the thread is pulled tight the seam closes and the stitches disappear. It is used to close outside arm and back panels on curves, finish cushions that have no zipper, and attach trims. Note that blind stitch also refers to a specific stuffing stitch used in building traditional edges, so context matters. Ladder stitching takes time and skill, which is why many shops use metal tack strip for faster closures where it suits."},{"id":"kb-glossary-050","topic":"glossary","title":"Muslin (calico)","aliases":["muslin","calico","muslin cover","undercover","first cover","calico lining","test cover","toile"],"text":"Muslin is a plain, unbleached woven cotton fabric. In North America upholsterers call it muslin; in the UK it is called calico, and British muslin means a much finer sheer cloth. In traditional upholstery a muslin undercover is fitted tightly over the stuffing before the final fabric, to compress and shape the padding so the top cover only has to look good, not hold everything together. This is often a sign of quality work. Muslin is also used to make test patterns or mock-ups, sometimes called a toile, before cutting expensive fabric, and for interior panels of cushions and bases that do not show."},{"id":"kb-glossary-051","topic":"glossary","title":"Dust cover (cambric)","aliases":["dust cover","cambric","bottom cloth","dust cloth","cambric cover","black cambric","bottom cover","dust panel"],"text":"The dust cover, also called cambric, is the thin black fabric stapled to the underside of a sofa or chair. It keeps dust from falling out of the springs and padding, hides the inside workings, and gives a tidy finish. Older cambric was a glazed cotton, but most modern cambric is a non-woven polypropylene. It is usually the last thing installed after reupholstering. When a dust cover is torn or sagging, it can usually be replaced on its own as a small job. Because it is the first thing removed when inspecting a piece, staff often look under it to check the springs, webbing and frame before giving an estimate."},{"id":"kb-glossary-052","topic":"glossary","title":"Polyester wrap (Dacron)","aliases":["Dacron","dacron wrap","polyester wrap","poly wrap","bonded polyester batting","polyester batting","fibre wrap","batting","polywrap"],"text":"Polyester wrap, often called Dacron in shops even though Dacron is a brand name, is a sheet of bonded polyester fibre wrapped around foam. It softens the hard edges of foam, gives cushions a rounder, plumper look, fills the cover snugly so fabric does not wrinkle, and lets fabric slide over the foam instead of grabbing it. It comes in different thicknesses and weights. It is also used on frames to soften inside arms and backs, and as a layer under the cover on tight seats. Over years the wrap compresses and the cushion looks flatter, so replacing wrap is often part of refreshing tired cushions even when the foam is still good."},{"id":"kb-glossary-053","topic":"glossary","title":"Down-proof ticking","aliases":["down-proof ticking","downproof ticking","feather-proof ticking","ticking","down-proof fabric","cushion ticking","inner casing fabric"],"text":"Down-proof ticking is a very tightly woven fabric, usually cotton, used to make inner casings for down and feather cushions. The tight weave stops small feather quills and down from working through, so they do not poke out of the show fabric. Feather-proof ticking is slightly less tight and suits feather fills but not pure down. Striped ticking is the traditional mattress fabric and is now also used as a decorative fabric. Down cushion inserts are usually sewn with internal walls, called baffles or channels, so the fill does not all slide to one end. A quality down cushion should always have a proper ticking inner, never just the show fabric holding the fill."},{"id":"kb-glossary-054","topic":"glossary","title":"Down-wrapped cushion (spring-down, down blend)","aliases":["down wrapped","down wrap","down envelope","feather wrap","spring-down cushion","foam core down cushion","blend-down","down-wrapped foam"],"text":"A down-wrapped cushion has a foam core, sometimes a spring core, surrounded by an envelope of down and feathers in a ticking casing. It combines the support and shape retention of foam with the soft, sink-in feel of down on top. It is a popular upgrade for sofa seats. A spring-down cushion has a pocketed coil core wrapped in foam and down. Compared with pure down, a down-wrapped cushion needs much less plumping. Compared with foam and polyester, it feels softer and more luxurious but costs more and still needs occasional fluffing. Shops can make these to order, and customers allergic to feathers can choose a synthetic down-alternative wrap."},{"id":"kb-glossary-055","topic":"glossary","title":"Panel names (inside arm, outside back, etc.)","aliases":["inside arm","outside arm","inside back","outside back","IA","OA","IB","OB","seat front","panel names","cut list names"],"text":"Upholsterers name the parts of a piece by position, and these names appear on cut lists and work orders. Inside arm is the part of the arm facing the seat; outside arm faces outward. Inside back faces the sitter; outside back is the back of the piece facing the room or wall. Seat or deck is where you sit; the front band, nose or seat front is the strip below the cushion. Arm fronts, facings or panels cover the arm ends. Boxing, skirt, welt and cushions are listed separately. Shops often abbreviate these as IA, OA, IB and OB. Knowing these names helps Stitch understand staff and explain quotes to customers."},{"id":"kb-glossary-056","topic":"glossary","title":"Stretcher fabric (pull strips, flies)","aliases":["stretcher","stretchers","pull strips","flies","fly","fly pieces","hidden extensions","tuck-away fabric","economy strips"],"text":"Stretchers are pieces of plain, cheaper fabric sewn onto the edges of the show fabric in places that will not be seen, such as where the inside back tucks down behind the deck or where the inside arm tucks under the seat. In the UK they are called flies. The upholsterer pulls on the stretcher to tighten the cover and staples it to the frame, saving expensive fabric and reducing yardage. Pull strips is another shop name. Customers bringing their own fabric sometimes ask why their yardage seems low; stretchers are part of the reason. Some high-end pieces use full show fabric throughout, which uses more yardage."},{"id":"kb-glossary-057","topic":"glossary","title":"Tuck-away (crevice allowance)","aliases":["tuck-away","tuck away","crevice","crevice allowance","tuck-in","tuck allowance","pull-through","stuff-through"],"text":"The tuck-away is the extra fabric that pushes down into the gap, or crevice, where the inside back and inside arms meet the deck. It lets the fabric move when people sit without pulling tight or tearing at the seams. The cover or its stretcher is pushed through the gap and fixed to the frame underneath. Upholsterers add a tuck allowance when measuring these panels. The crevice is also where coins, crumbs and pet hair collect, so customers should vacuum it regularly. On tight seats, the tuck-away has to be generous so the seat can compress without stressing the fabric or the welt seams along the arms."},{"id":"kb-glossary-058","topic":"glossary","title":"Railroading (term)","aliases":["railroading","railroaded","railroad fabric","turned fabric","fabric turned","run horizontally","up the bolt sideways"],"text":"Railroading means the fabric is turned so its length runs horizontally across the piece, from arm to arm, instead of vertically from top to bottom. The pattern ends up sideways relative to how it looks on the roll. Fabrics designed for railroading have patterns that look correct when turned; fabrics marked up the roll must not be turned. Railroading lets a long sofa be covered with no seams across the inside back and seat, and often saves yardage on long pieces. Plain fabrics and some stripes can usually be railroaded, but velvets and naps will change their shading. Shops check the fabric label or the supplier's specifications before deciding."},{"id":"kb-glossary-059","topic":"glossary","title":"Up the roll (non-railroaded)","aliases":["up the roll","up-the-roll","non-railroaded","vertical repeat","up the bolt","standard orientation"],"text":"Up the roll means the pattern runs vertically along the length of the fabric, so on furniture the top of the design must point up toward the top of the piece. Most patterned fabrics are up the roll. On a long sofa, an up-the-roll fabric narrower than the inside back has to be seamed, with panels joined and the pattern matched at each seam, which uses more fabric and labour. Pieces cut up the roll are laid out down the length of the roll in order, which is why the cut list must be planned carefully. The fabric specification sheet or selvage usually indicates whether a fabric is railroaded or up the roll."},{"id":"kb-glossary-060","topic":"glossary","title":"Pattern repeat","aliases":["pattern repeat","repeat","vertical repeat","horizontal repeat","half-drop","half drop repeat","straight match","motif"],"text":"A pattern repeat is the distance before a fabric design starts over. The vertical repeat is measured along the length of the roll and the horizontal repeat across its width. A straight match repeats directly across; a half-drop shifts by half the repeat across the width. Large repeats mean more waste, because each cushion, arm and back piece needs the motif centred and matched to its neighbours. Upholsterers add extra yardage for patterned fabric, and the amount depends on the size of the repeat and how many pieces need centring. When a customer picks a large floral or a bold geometric, the quote should account for the repeat rather than using the plain-fabric yardage."},{"id":"kb-glossary-061","topic":"glossary","title":"Selvage (selvedge)","aliases":["selvage","selvedge","self edge","fabric edge","bolt edge","selvage printing"],"text":"The selvage, also spelled selvedge, is the finished, woven edge along both long sides of a roll of fabric that stops it fraying. It is often printed with the maker's name, the pattern, colour information and sometimes registration marks for the pattern. Upholsterers do not use the selvage in visible areas because it can be tighter or shrink differently from the body of the fabric. The usable width is measured inside the selvages. When a customer asks about their own fabric, the selvage printing can help identify whether it is railroaded, its content and cleaning code, and the repeat. Customers bringing their own fabric should keep the selvage and any label."},{"id":"kb-glossary-062","topic":"glossary","title":"Bias (bias cut)","aliases":["bias","bias cut","bias strip","true bias","cross grain","straight grain","grain line"],"text":"Bias is the diagonal direction across a woven fabric, at forty-five degrees to the straight grain. Fabric cut on the bias stretches and bends more easily, which is why welt strips are often cut on the bias so they curve smoothly around cushion corners and curved arms without puckering. Straight grain runs along the length of the fabric; cross grain runs across its width. Many shops cut welt on the straight grain for straight runs to save fabric, and switch to bias for curves or stripes, where bias welt creates a candy-stripe effect. Choosing the grain affects how much fabric welt uses, which can change the total yardage needed."},{"id":"kb-glossary-063","topic":"glossary","title":"Nap and pile direction","aliases":["nap","pile","pile direction","nap direction","shading","one-way fabric","brush direction","directional fabric"],"text":"Nap or pile is the raised surface of fabrics like velvet, mohair, chenille and corduroy. The fibres lean in one direction, so the colour looks lighter or darker depending on which way you view or brush it. Upholsterers keep the pile running in a consistent direction on every panel, usually smoothing down on seats and inside backs, so the piece does not look patchy. On seats the pile normally runs from back to front so it does not catch clothing and wear oddly. One-way or directional fabrics cannot be turned to save fabric, and railroading may change their shading, so velvets often need a little more yardage than plain flat-woven fabrics."},{"id":"kb-glossary-064","topic":"glossary","title":"Show cover (top cover)","aliases":["show cover","top cover","face fabric","cover fabric","show fabric","outer fabric","final cover","decorative fabric"],"text":"The show cover, or top cover, is the final decorative fabric or leather that people see on the finished piece. Everything beneath it, such as muslin, padding, burlap and springs, is the inside work. In shop conversation the show cover is usually just called the fabric or the cover, and the hidden fabrics like deck cloth, stretchers and cambric are named separately. Yardage estimates usually refer to show cover only. Customer's own material, often written as COM, means the customer supplies the show cover themselves. The show cover's weight, durability rating and cleaning code all affect whether it is suitable for the piece being upholstered."},{"id":"kb-glossary-065","topic":"glossary","title":"Cotton felt and batting","aliases":["cotton felt","cotton batting","cotton linters","cotton padding","batting","wadding","skin wadding","felt padding"],"text":"Cotton felt, or cotton batting, is a layer of matted cotton fibres used as a top padding over stuffing or foam, just under the muslin or show cover. In the UK the soft top layer is often called wadding. Cotton gives a smooth, slightly firm surface and stops loose hair or fibres poking through the cover. It was standard in traditional and early twentieth-century upholstery and is still used in quality work, especially antiques and tight seats. Polyester batting is a lighter, springier modern alternative that resists compressing but can feel slippery. Old cotton felt can become lumpy, dusty or hard; during reupholstery it is usually replaced unless it is part of an antique being conserved."},{"id":"kb-glossary-066","topic":"glossary","title":"Horsehair (curled hair)","aliases":["horsehair","horse hair","curled hair","curled horsehair","hair stuffing","animal hair","hogs hair","rubberised hair"],"text":"Curled horsehair is a traditional luxury stuffing made from horse mane and tail hair, twisted into ropes, curled and then teased apart so it is springy. It is resilient, breathable, long-lasting and can be cleaned and re-teased for reuse, which is why restorers value original horsehair in antique pieces. Cheaper traditional mixes blended horsehair with hog or cattle hair. Rubberised hair is curled hair bonded with latex into sheets, used in some period and high-end work. Horsehair is also a woven fabric, made from tail hair on a cotton or silk warp, used on some period chairs. Horsehair can harbour dust or old pests, so shops inspect it carefully before deciding to reuse it."},{"id":"kb-glossary-067","topic":"glossary","title":"Coir, tow and other traditional stuffings","aliases":["coir","coconut fibre","coir fibre","tow","flax tow","Algerian grass","Spanish moss","excelsior","wood wool","straw","seaweed","alva"],"text":"Before foam, upholsterers used many plant fibres. Coir is springy coconut husk fibre, used for first stuffings and still sold, sometimes rubberised into pads. Tow is coarse flax or hemp fibre. Algerian grass, also called crin végétal, is a palm fibre that was common in European pieces. Spanish moss, cured and dried, was widely used in North American furniture. Excelsior, or wood wool, is fine wood shavings found in cheaper older pieces. Straw, seaweed and other grasses also appear in older work. Identifying the stuffing helps date a piece. Most of these break down into dust over time, so they are usually replaced unless the piece is a valuable antique where original material should be conserved."},{"id":"kb-glossary-068","topic":"glossary","title":"Frame","aliases":["frame","furniture frame","hardwood frame","kiln-dried hardwood","plywood frame","particle board frame","engineered wood frame"],"text":"The frame is the wooden skeleton of upholstered furniture. Quality frames are kiln-dried hardwood, such as maple, ash, oak, birch or beech, joined with dowels, mortise and tenon or screws and reinforced with glued corner blocks. Kiln drying reduces later shrinking and cracking. Many modern frames use plywood or engineered wood, which can be sound when well built, while cheap particle board frames may not hold staples or stand up to reupholstering. Before quoting a reupholstery job, staff check the frame for loose joints, cracks, broken rails and previous repairs. A solid frame is the main reason reupholstering a good older piece is often worth more than buying a cheap new one."},{"id":"kb-glossary-069","topic":"glossary","title":"Corner blocks","aliases":["corner blocks","glue blocks","corner braces","corner brackets","triangular blocks","frame blocks"],"text":"Corner blocks are triangular or square blocks of wood glued and screwed into the inside corners of a frame, where rails meet legs or posts. They brace the joint against racking, the side-to-side wobble that loosens chairs and sofas. Good frames have corner blocks at every seat corner. When a chair wobbles, the blocks have often come loose, split, or lost their glue. Part of frame repair is removing loose blocks, cleaning old glue, regluing and screwing them back, or replacing them. Some cheap furniture uses staples or metal brackets instead. Staff checking a piece for reupholstery should flip it and look at the corner blocks first."},{"id":"kb-glossary-070","topic":"glossary","title":"Frame joints (dowel, mortise and tenon)","aliases":["dowel joint","dowels","mortise and tenon","mortise","tenon","glued joint","butt joint","screw joint","frame joinery"],"text":"Frame joints are where the wooden parts of a frame connect. A dowel joint uses round wooden pins glued into matching holes in both parts. A mortise and tenon joint has a tongue on one part fitting into a slot in the other, a very strong traditional joint. Cheaper frames use butt joints held by staples, nails or screws alone. Over time, glue dries out and joints loosen, especially on chairs that are leaned back on. Repair usually means taking the joint apart, cleaning the old glue, and regluing under clamps, sometimes with new dowels. Simply squirting glue into a loose joint rarely lasts, because new glue does not bond well over old glue."},{"id":"kb-glossary-071","topic":"glossary","title":"Show wood (exposed wood)","aliases":["show wood","showwood","exposed wood","exposed frame","wood trim","carved frame","finished wood","wood rails"],"text":"Show wood is any part of an upholstered frame that is finished and left visible, such as carved crest rails, arm fronts, legs and seat rails on French chairs, Victorian sofas and mid-century pieces. Upholsterers must work carefully around show wood: staples and fabric edges are stopped neatly at a rabbet or edge and covered with gimp, double welt or nailheads, and tools must not scratch the finish. Refinishing show wood is usually a separate trade, either done by a refinisher or quoted as a separate line. Customers should be told that touch-ups and refinishing are not automatically included in a reupholstery quote unless the shop says so."},{"id":"kb-glossary-072","topic":"glossary","title":"Rails (seat rail, tack rail, back rail)","aliases":["rails","seat rail","front rail","back rail","side rail","tack rail","tacking rail","stretcher rail","top rail","crest rail","arm rail"],"text":"Rails are the horizontal pieces of a frame. Seat rails form the box that the seat is built on: front, back and side rails. Tack rails, or tacking rails, are extra rails where fabric is stapled, such as along the bottom of the inside back or the inside arms. The top rail of a back is called the crest rail on chairs. Stretchers are rails between legs. Over many reupholsterings, tack rails can become riddled with old staple holes and split; a shop may need to plug, patch or replace them before new fabric will hold. Customers will not see this, but it can affect the labour estimate."},{"id":"kb-glossary-073","topic":"glossary","title":"Slip seat (drop-in seat)","aliases":["slip seat","drop-in seat","drop seat","loose seat","lift-out seat","dining chair seat","seat pan","seat board"],"text":"A slip seat, also called a drop-in seat, is a separate seat panel that sits inside the chair frame and lifts out, usually after removing a few screws from underneath. Most dining chairs have them. The seat is a plywood board, or a wooden frame with webbing, padded with foam or traditional stuffing and covered in fabric wrapped under and stapled. Recovering slip seats is one of the simplest and most common upholstery jobs, and a popular DIY project. Each seat typically needs a modest piece of fabric, around half a yard, depending on size and pattern. Thicker new fabric or padding can make a seat too snug to drop back in, so allowances matter."},{"id":"kb-glossary-074","topic":"glossary","title":"Stuff-over seat (pad seat)","aliases":["stuff-over seat","stuffover","over-stuffed seat","pad seat","overupholstered seat","fixed upholstered seat","top-stuffed seat"],"text":"A stuff-over seat is upholstered directly over the chair's seat rails, with the fabric wrapping over the edges and fixed to the outside of the rails or under the frame, rather than being a lift-out panel. It may have springs or just webbing and padding. A pad seat is a thinner version, often on show-wood chairs, where a shallow pad sits on top of the frame and the fabric is finished with gimp or nails at the edge of the rail. Stuff-over seats take more work to recover than slip seats because the chair cannot be taken apart, and the fabric edges must be finished neatly around legs and show wood."},{"id":"kb-glossary-075","topic":"glossary","title":"Arm styles (roll, scroll, track, pleated)","aliases":["arm styles","roll arm","rolled arm","English arm","scroll arm","track arm","square arm","flared arm","pleated arm","slope arm","recessed arm","panel arm"],"text":"Arm style is one of the biggest parts of a sofa's look. A track arm is square and straight-sided, modern and tailored. A roll arm curves outward at the top; a low, tight English roll arm sits back from the seat front. A scroll arm, or panel arm, rolls out and has a flat shaped facing on the front. A pleated arm has fabric gathered in fan-shaped pleats on the front. Flared arms curve outward; slope arms angle down toward the front. Rounded arms take more fabric and labour to pleat or panel than track arms. When customers want to change an arm style, the frame itself usually has to be rebuilt, which shops quote separately."},{"id":"kb-glossary-076","topic":"glossary","title":"Arm caps (arm covers)","aliases":["arm caps","arm covers","armrest covers","arm protectors","arm guards","chair caps"],"text":"Arm caps are separate fabric covers that fit over the tops of sofa or chair arms to protect them from wear, body oils and spills. They are usually made from extra cover fabric at the same time as the reupholstery, so they match perfectly. Arms are often the first area to show wear and dirt, so caps help the piece last longer, and they can be removed for cleaning or replaced. Some customers find them old-fashioned and decline them. When fabric is ordered, it is worth asking whether the customer wants arm caps, because adding them later may mean finding fabric from a different dye lot."},{"id":"kb-glossary-077","topic":"glossary","title":"Cushion zipper","aliases":["zipper","cushion zipper","zipper tape","zip","continuous zipper","zipper pull","zip closure","boxing zipper"],"text":"Most loose cushions have a zipper sewn into the back boxing so the insert can be put in or replaced. Shops usually use continuous zipper tape cut to length with separate pulls. The zipper typically runs across the back and wraps a short way around each back corner, making it easier to insert the foam. On reversible cushions, the zipper sits along the back so it stays hidden. Customers often ask whether they can wash cushion covers by unzipping them; the answer depends on the fabric and its cleaning code, and machine washing upholstery covers can shrink them or damage backing, so shops generally advise against it unless the fabric is labelled for it."},{"id":"kb-glossary-078","topic":"glossary","title":"Hog rings","aliases":["hog rings","hog ring pliers","hog ringing","C-rings","seat rings","clinch rings","listing wire rings"],"text":"Hog rings are small C-shaped metal rings closed with special pliers. In upholstery they are used mainly in automotive seats, where the seat cover is fastened to wires in the foam or frame by rings through listing strips sewn into the cover. They are also used to attach burlap and springs, fix sinuous springs, and in some furniture and mattresses. Replacing a car seat cover means opening all the old rings and closing new ones in the same places. Stainless or galvanised rings resist rust. Some modern seats use plastic clips instead. Hog rings explain why car seat covers fit so tightly around shaped seats."},{"id":"kb-glossary-079","topic":"glossary","title":"Bolster","aliases":["bolster","bolster pillow","neck roll","round bolster","tubular cushion","roll pillow","lumbar bolster"],"text":"A bolster is a long, firm, often cylindrical cushion. Round bolsters are used on daybeds, chaises, Recamier sofas and some arms; flat or rectangular bolsters are used as back supports on daybeds, window seats and banquettes. In automotive work, bolsters are the raised side supports on seats. A bolster can have shirred ends, flat round ends with welt, or button ends. It is filled with firm foam or fibre so it keeps its shape. When customers order window seat or daybed cushions, shops often suggest bolsters to make the seat comfortable for sitting as well as lounging."},{"id":"kb-glossary-080","topic":"glossary","title":"Ottoman, footstool, pouf","aliases":["ottoman","footstool","footrest","pouf","pouffe","hassock","cocktail ottoman","storage ottoman","tuffet"],"text":"An ottoman is an upholstered seat or footrest without a back or arms. Footstools are smaller versions for resting feet; a hassock is a traditional term for a padded footstool, also used for church kneelers. A pouf, or pouffe, is a soft, often round ottoman with no visible legs. Cocktail ottomans are large and low, used like a coffee table, sometimes with a tray. Storage ottomans have a hinged or lift-off lid. Ottomans are popular projects because they need little fabric and show off tufting, nailheads or a statement fabric. When recovering one, check the lid hinges, legs and whether it has springs or just foam."},{"id":"kb-glossary-081","topic":"glossary","title":"Banquette","aliases":["banquette","booth","built-in seating","bench seating","window seat","dining banquette","booth seating","settle"],"text":"A banquette is an upholstered bench, often built into a wall or corner, used for dining in homes and restaurants. Restaurant versions are usually called booths. Banquettes may have a tight seat and back, a channelled or tufted back, or loose seat cushions on a wooden bench base. Window seats are a similar idea set into a bay or alcove. Home banquettes are often custom-built to fit a space, so they need careful on-site measuring, including wall angles and heating vents. Commercial booths need durable, cleanable fabric or vinyl, and removable seat panels help with cleaning and future recovering."},{"id":"kb-glossary-082","topic":"glossary","title":"Slipcover","aliases":["slipcover","slip cover","loose cover","loose covers","fitted cover","removable cover","tailored slipcover","sofa cover"],"text":"A slipcover is a removable fitted fabric cover made to go over an existing sofa or chair. In the UK and parts of Canada it is called a loose cover. A custom tailored slipcover is cut and sewn to the exact shape of the piece, with welt, zippers or ties, and can look very close to upholstery. Generic stretch covers are a different, ready-made product. Slipcovers suit households with children or pets and people who like to change looks seasonally. Whether a slipcover can be washed depends on the fabric; it should be pre-shrunk or dry cleaned. Slipcovers usually use somewhat more fabric than reupholstery and do not fix underlying springs or padding."},{"id":"kb-glossary-083","topic":"glossary","title":"COM and COL (customer's own material)","aliases":["COM","COL","customer's own material","customer's own leather","customer supplied fabric","own fabric","bring your own fabric","CFM"],"text":"COM means customer's own material: the customer buys and supplies the fabric instead of choosing from the shop's stock or sample books. COL is the same for leather. Most shops accept COM, but they usually ask to see the fabric first to check it is suitable for upholstery, durable enough for the piece, and that there is enough of it. Shops may not guarantee results on fabric they did not supply, such as fabrics that stretch, fray, or show needle marks. When a customer asks about bringing their own fabric, Stitch should explain that the shop will need the yardage, and ideally the fabric's width, repeat and content information, before confirming a quote."},{"id":"kb-glossary-084","topic":"glossary","title":"Yard and metre (fabric measurement)","aliases":["yard","yards","yardage","metre","meter","metres","linear yard","running yard","fabric measurement","yd","m"],"text":"Upholstery fabric is sold by length off the roll, in linear yards in the United States and often in metres in Canada and Europe. Many Canadian shops and suppliers still quote yards, and customers mix both, so it helps to convert carefully: one yard equals 0.9144 metres, and one metre is about 1.094 yards. Yardage always refers to length at the fabric's full width, so a yard of 54-inch fabric is not the same amount of material as a yard of narrower fabric. When a customer says they have a certain amount of fabric, Stitch should ask for both the length and the width, and note whether the figure is in yards or metres."},{"id":"kb-glossary-085","topic":"glossary","title":"Fabric width","aliases":["fabric width","54 inch","140 cm","standard width","usable width","wide fabric","narrow fabric","bolt width"],"text":"Most upholstery fabric is about 54 inches wide, which is roughly 137 to 140 centimetres. Some are wider, around 110 to 120 inches, especially sheers and some railroaded fabrics, and some specialty or vintage fabrics are narrower. Usable width is the width inside the selvages. Yardage rules of thumb assume roughly 54-inch fabric; narrower fabric needs more length, and wide fabric can be railroaded to avoid seams. Leather is sold by the hide or square foot instead of by width and length. When a customer's own fabric is unusually narrow or wide, the shop needs to recalculate the cut plan rather than use a standard estimate."},{"id":"kb-glossary-086","topic":"glossary","title":"Regulator","aliases":["regulator","upholstery regulator","stuffing regulator","stuffing tool","bodkin","awl"],"text":"A regulator is a long, slender steel tool, pointed at one end and often flattened at the other, used in traditional upholstery to move and even out stuffing through the burlap or muslin without opening it up. The upholsterer pushes it in and levers the fibres into place to fill hollows, sharpen corners and shape edges. The flat end is used to tuck and fold fabric into tight corners and pleats. It is one of the classic tools of hand upholstery, along with curved needles, double-pointed needles, a webbing stretcher and a tack hammer. Modern foam-based work uses it less, but it remains useful for tucking and pleating."},{"id":"kb-glossary-087","topic":"glossary","title":"Stripping tools","aliases":["ripping chisel","tack lifter","staple remover","staple puller","stripping tools","mallet","ripper","claw tool"],"text":"Stripping old upholstery uses a few specialist tools. A ripping chisel is a blunt chisel tapped with a mallet, used along the grain to drive out tacks and staples. A tack lifter has a forked tip to lever tacks out. Staple removers and staple pullers lift and grip staples, and pliers pull out any legs that stay behind. Careful stripping protects the frame and show wood; rushing it splits rails. Stripping is dusty work and old materials can contain mould, pests or loose fibres, so shops use dust masks and good ventilation. Stripping time is a real part of the labour on any quote, especially on pieces with several previous layers."},{"id":"kb-glossary-088","topic":"glossary","title":"Upholstery needles","aliases":["upholstery needles","curved needle","straight needle","double-pointed needle","mattress needle","tufting needle","button needle","packing needle"],"text":"Upholstery uses several needle types. Curved needles, in various sizes, are used for hand closing seams and stitching where you cannot reach behind the fabric, such as outside backs and cushions without zippers. Long straight double-pointed needles, sometimes called mattress or bayonet needles, pass right through stuffing for stitched edges and are used for tufting. Heavy packing needles stitch burlap and springs. Leather work uses needles with a cutting point. Picking the right needle and thread is part of the craft; using a household needle on upholstery usually bends or breaks it."},{"id":"kb-glossary-089","topic":"glossary","title":"Upholstery thread","aliases":["upholstery thread","bonded nylon thread","bonded polyester thread","heavy duty thread","UV thread","PTFE thread","button thread","thread weight"],"text":"Upholstery is sewn with heavy-duty thread, usually bonded nylon or bonded polyester. Bonding coats the thread so it runs smoothly through industrial machines and does not fray. Nylon is very strong but degrades in sunlight, so outdoor and marine work uses UV-resistant polyester or PTFE thread, which is designed to outlast the fabric outdoors. Thread is sized by numbers that vary by system, with common upholstery weights being much thicker than household thread. Thread colour is matched to the fabric, or contrasted deliberately for topstitching. When an outdoor cushion's seams are failing while the fabric looks fine, sun-damaged thread is the usual cause."},{"id":"kb-glossary-090","topic":"glossary","title":"Walking foot sewing machine","aliases":["walking foot","walking foot machine","industrial sewing machine","compound feed","upholstery machine","double needle machine","cylinder bed"],"text":"Upholstery shops sew on industrial machines, usually walking-foot machines. A walking foot moves the top layer of fabric along with the bottom, so thick layers, welt, vinyl and leather feed evenly without shifting or puckering. Compound-feed machines add a moving needle for even better grip. Some shops also have double-needle machines for topstitching, cylinder-bed machines for awkward shapes, and machines for marine canvas and auto work. Domestic machines usually cannot handle several layers of upholstery fabric plus welt. This is one reason DIY cushion covers are harder than they look, and why shops can sew heavy leather and marine vinyl neatly."},{"id":"kb-glossary-091","topic":"glossary","title":"Spray adhesive and contact cement","aliases":["spray adhesive","spray glue","contact cement","foam glue","foam adhesive","headliner adhesive","high-temp adhesive","hot glue"],"text":"Adhesives are a normal part of modern upholstery. Spray adhesive holds polyester wrap to foam, joins foam layers and keeps padding from shifting on frames. Foam-specific adhesives stay flexible when dry. Contact cement is used on vinyl, leather and auto work. Headliners need high-temperature adhesive, because car roofs get very hot and ordinary spray glue lets the fabric sag. Hot glue is used for gimp, double welt and trims. Shops work with adhesives in ventilated areas because many contain solvents. Customers sometimes ask whether glue is a shortcut; when the right product is used in the right place, it is standard practice, not a sign of poor workmanship."},{"id":"kb-glossary-092","topic":"glossary","title":"Spring edge vs hard edge seats","aliases":["spring edge","hard edge","firm edge","soft edge","front edge","nose","seat nose","nosing"],"text":"A seat's front edge is either a spring edge or a hard edge. A spring edge has springs right up to the front, often with an edge wire, so the front flexes when you sit and feels soft all the way forward. A hard edge has a fixed padded wooden front rail with the springs behind it, so the front is firm. The front band of the seat is sometimes called the nose or nosing. Spring edges are common in better traditional furniture; hard edges are common in modern and budget pieces and are easier to build and keep looking crisp. When customers say the front of the seat feels hard or digs into their legs, more padding or a different edge build can help."},{"id":"kb-glossary-093","topic":"glossary","title":"Flange and Turkish corners","aliases":["flange","flanged edge","flange pillow","Turkish corner","gathered corner","harem corner","mitred corner","mitered corner"],"text":"A flange is a flat border of fabric sewn around the edge of a pillow or cushion, extending past the stuffed part like a frame. Flanged pillows look tailored and modern. A Turkish corner, sometimes called a gathered or harem corner, is made by gathering the fabric at each corner of a pillow so it looks puffy and rounded rather than pointed. Mitred corners, mitered in American spelling, are folded or sewn at forty-five degrees to create a sharp, neat corner on tight upholstery, boxing or wrapped panels. These details matter on quotes and work orders, because each changes cutting, sewing time and sometimes yardage."},{"id":"kb-glossary-094","topic":"glossary","title":"Legs, feet and glides","aliases":["legs","feet","bun feet","turned legs","tapered legs","hairpin legs","plinth","casters","glides","furniture glides","leg plates"],"text":"Legs and feet change a piece's style a lot. Turned legs suit traditional pieces; tapered legs suit mid-century; bun feet are round and squat; a plinth base is a solid upholstered or wooden base with no visible legs. Casters let heavy pieces roll and are common on traditional chairs. Glides are small pads under the legs that protect floors. Many modern legs screw into metal leg plates, so they can be swapped easily during reupholstery. Replacing or refinishing legs is a separate line on most quotes. Changing from a skirt to exposed legs, or the reverse, is a popular update that can make an older piece look current."},{"id":"kb-glossary-095","topic":"glossary","title":"Spring gauge","aliases":["spring gauge","wire gauge","gauge","spring wire thickness","heavy gauge","light gauge","8 gauge","9 gauge","11 gauge","12 gauge"],"text":"Spring wire is sized by gauge. With the gauge system used for upholstery springs, a lower number means thicker, stiffer wire and a higher number means thinner, softer wire. Sinuous springs for sofa seats are typically heavier gauge than those used in backs, and wider seats or bigger spans need heavier wire or more springs to avoid sag. Coil springs also come in different gauges for seats and backs. When replacing springs, shops match the original gauge, or go a little heavier if the customer wants a firmer seat. Mixing gauges across a seat can create soft or hard spots, so springs are normally replaced as a set."},{"id":"kb-glossary-096","topic":"glossary","title":"Tension and pull (fabric tightness)","aliases":["tension","fabric tension","pull","stretch","wrinkles","puckering","fabric tightness","cover tightness"],"text":"Upholsterers talk about pull or tension when describing how tightly the cover is stretched. Even tension in the right direction keeps fabric smooth, prevents wrinkles and makes welt lines straight. Too little tension and the fabric bags or wrinkles as soon as someone sits; too much and seams strain, pattern lines distort, or the fabric splits at stress points. Fabrics behave differently: knits and stretchy fabrics need less pull, while tightly woven fabrics can take more. Some wrinkling on loose cushions and soft backs is normal and settles in. Persistent bagging on a tight seat after use, though, can point to padding that is too soft or fabric that has stretched."},{"id":"kb-glossary-097","topic":"glossary","title":"Bench cushion (single seat cushion)","aliases":["bench cushion","bench seat cushion","single cushion","one-piece seat cushion","long seat cushion","bench seat"],"text":"A bench cushion is one long seat cushion that spans the whole width of a sofa instead of two or three separate cushions. It gives a clean, modern or tailored look with no gaps between seats, and is common on mid-century, camelback and tuxedo styles. The trade-off is that it cannot be rotated between seat positions, so the most-used spot can wear or soften faster, and the cushion must be flipped end to end instead. Long bench cushions need a long piece of foam and often railroaded fabric to avoid seams on the top panel. Converting two or three cushions into one bench cushion, or the reverse, is a common reupholstery request."},{"id":"kb-glossary-098","topic":"glossary","title":"Insulator pad","aliases":["insulator pad","spring insulator","insulator","spring cover pad","fibre pad","sisal pad","deck pad"],"text":"An insulator pad is a layer of firm fibre, felt or non-woven material placed directly over springs, especially sinuous springs, before the foam or padding goes on. It stops the foam and fabric from working down between the wires and wearing through, and spreads the load so the springs are not felt through the seat. Burlap does a similar job in traditional work. When customers complain they can feel ridges or wires through the deck, a missing or worn insulator layer is a common cause. Replacing it is a normal part of rebuilding a seat deck and is usually a modest materials cost."},{"id":"kb-glossary-099","topic":"glossary","title":"Seat height, depth and pitch","aliases":["seat height","seat depth","pitch","seat pitch","back pitch","rake","seat angle","ergonomics sofa"],"text":"Seat height is the distance from the floor to the top of the seat cushion. Seat depth is how far it is from the front edge of the seat to the inside back. Pitch, or rake, is the angle of the seat and back. These measurements decide how a piece feels: deep, low seats with a laid-back pitch feel loungy, while higher, shallower seats with an upright back are easier to get in and out of. During reupholstery, shops can change seat height and depth somewhat by changing cushion thickness, padding or back cushions, but large changes need frame work. Customers with mobility concerns often benefit from a firmer, slightly higher seat."},{"id":"kb-glossary-100","topic":"glossary","title":"Scatter pillows (throw pillows, toss cushions)","aliases":["scatter pillows","throw pillows","toss cushions","accent pillows","decorative pillows","cushions UK","pillow inserts"],"text":"Scatter pillows, also called throw pillows, toss cushions or in the UK simply cushions, are small decorative pillows placed on a sofa, chair or bed. They are usually knife-edge or boxed, with welt, flange or plain edges, and have zippered covers over inserts of feather, down, fibre or blends. Shops often make matching pillows from leftover fabric after reupholstering, or contrast pillows to tie in other colours. Insert sizes are commonly a little larger than the cover so the pillow looks full. Scatter pillows are an easy, affordable way to refresh a room or test a bold fabric without committing a whole piece to it."},{"id":"kb-glossary-101","topic":"glossary","title":"Ties, hook-and-loop and cushion fasteners","aliases":["cushion ties","chair ties","hook and loop","Velcro","non-slip backing","cushion fasteners","snaps","grip pad"],"text":"Loose seat pads and cushions often need something to stop them sliding. Fabric ties at the back corners wrap around chair spindles, common on dining and kitchen chair pads. Hook-and-loop strips, often called by a brand name, fasten cushions to bases or to each other, as on banquettes and RV dinettes. Non-slip backing fabrics or grip pads add friction without fasteners. On boats, stainless or plastic snaps hold cushions down in wind. When ordering custom cushions, Stitch should ask how they will be held in place, since adding ties or fasteners changes sewing and materials."},{"id":"kb-history-001","topic":"history","title":"Upholstery history overview","aliases":["history of upholstery","upholstery origins","origin of upholstery","how old is upholstery","upholstery timeline"],"text":"Upholstery, the craft of padding and covering seats, grew slowly over thousands of years. Early seating in many ancient cultures used loose cushions, animal skins, woven seats and textiles laid over wooden or stone frames, rather than padding fixed to the frame. In medieval Europe, comfort mostly came from portable cushions and hangings that wealthy households carried between homes. Fixed padding attached to chair frames became common in Europe during the seventeenth century, and the craft grew elaborate in eighteenth-century France and England. Coil springs arrived in the nineteenth century, foam and sinuous springs in the twentieth, and performance fabrics late in that century. Exact dates for early developments are often uncertain, so Stitch should treat them as approximate."},{"id":"kb-history-002","topic":"history","title":"Origin of the word upholsterer","aliases":["upholsterer etymology","upholder","upholdester","word upholstery","where does the word upholstery come from","upholster meaning"],"text":"The word upholsterer comes from the Middle English upholder, from uphold in the old sense of to keep up or repair. An upholder was a tradesman who dealt in and repaired soft furnishings, such as bedding, hangings, cushions and second-hand household goods. The form upholsterer appeared later, and upholstery, meaning the work or the materials, came after that. The verb upholster is a back-formation from upholsterer. Historically, upholders and upholsterers did far more than cover chairs: they supplied beds, curtains, wall hangings and sometimes arranged whole interiors, much like a decorator. The older word survives in the name of the London guild, the Worshipful Company of Upholders."},{"id":"kb-history-003","topic":"history","title":"Medieval upholders and portable furnishings","aliases":["medieval upholstery","middle ages furniture","medieval cushions","hangings","travelling furniture","upholders medieval"],"text":"In medieval Europe, most furniture was plain and wooden, and comfort came from textiles rather than built-in padding. Wealthy households owned cushions, bed hangings, wall hangings and seat covers that could be packed and moved, since nobles often travelled between residences. Upholders made and traded these textile goods and stuffed items like mattresses, bolsters and cushions, using materials such as feathers, wool, straw and hair. Fixed upholstery on chairs was rare. Much of what is known comes from inventories, guild records and surviving pieces, so detailed claims about early techniques should be treated with caution. The trade's close link with beds and bedding lasted for centuries."},{"id":"kb-history-004","topic":"history","title":"Worshipful Company of Upholders","aliases":["Worshipful Company of Upholders","Upholders Company","upholders guild","London livery company","1626 charter","guild of upholsterers"],"text":"The Worshipful Company of Upholders is one of the livery companies of the City of London, the historic trade guilds. Records of an organised body of upholders in London go back to the medieval period, and the company received a royal charter from King Charles I in 1626. Like other guilds, it historically regulated the trade, set standards, trained apprentices and checked the quality of goods such as feather beds and stuffed articles. Today it continues as a livery company with charitable and educational activity related to the trade. It is a useful example of how long upholstery has been recognised as a distinct, skilled craft."},{"id":"kb-history-005","topic":"history","title":"17th-century fixed upholstery","aliases":["17th century upholstery","seventeenth century","farthingale chair","Turkey work","early upholstered chairs","Jacobean upholstery"],"text":"During the seventeenth century, padding fixed permanently to chair frames became more common in Europe. In England, a type of early upholstered back stool is traditionally called a farthingale chair, a name said to come from its armless shape suiting the wide farthingale skirts of the time, though that explanation is traditionally said rather than proven. Seats were padded with materials like hair, wool or grass over webbing and covered in leather, velvet, needlework or Turkey work, a knotted wool pile fabric made in England to imitate Turkish carpets. Covers were often fixed with decorative brass nails and finished with fringe. Comfort was still modest by modern standards, and fine upholstery signalled wealth."},{"id":"kb-history-006","topic":"history","title":"French upholstery tradition (17th–18th century)","aliases":["French upholstery","tapissier","Louis XIV","Louis XV","Louis XVI","French chairs history","à la reine","en cabriolet","French tradition"],"text":"France developed one of the most refined upholstery traditions, especially from the reign of Louis XIV through the eighteenth century. The French upholsterer, or tapissier, worked alongside the menuisier, the frame maker. Seat forms such as the fauteuil, with open arms, and the bergère, with enclosed arms and a loose cushion, appeared and evolved. Backs might be flat, called à la reine, or curved, called en cabriolet. Upholstery used webbing, horsehair stuffing and carefully stitched edges to build crisp, squared shapes, often covered in silk, tapestry or damask. Many traditional hand techniques still taught today, including stitched edges, come from this European tradition."},{"id":"kb-history-007","topic":"history","title":"English upholstery tradition (18th century)","aliases":["English upholstery history","Georgian upholstery","Chippendale","Hepplewhite","Sheraton","18th century England","Director 1754"],"text":"In eighteenth-century England, upholsterers often held a powerful role in furnishing grand houses, supplying curtains, beds and seat furniture and sometimes coordinating whole interiors. Pattern books spread designs: Thomas Chippendale's The Gentleman and Cabinet-Maker's Director, first published in 1754, included upholstered chairs and sofas, and later books associated with George Hepplewhite and Thomas Sheraton were also very influential. Seats were stuffed with horsehair over webbing, with stitched edges, and covered in damask, leather, needlework or woollen fabrics, frequently finished with brass nails. Many styles still made today, such as the camelback sofa and wingback chair, have roots in this period."},{"id":"kb-history-008","topic":"history","title":"Wingback chair origins","aliases":["wingback history","wing chair history","easy chair history","fireside chair","grandfather chair"],"text":"The wing chair, or wingback, developed in England in the late seventeenth century. Its tall back and side wings are traditionally explained as protection from draughts and as a way to trap the warmth of a fire, which is plausible given the houses of the time, though it is usually told as tradition rather than documented fact. Early examples were often called easy chairs and were associated with comfort for the elderly or unwell. The form stayed popular through the eighteenth century in England and colonial America and has been reproduced ever since. Today wingbacks remain a staple reupholstery job and are known for needing careful work on the curved wings and arm scrolls."},{"id":"kb-history-009","topic":"history","title":"Traditional stuffing materials history","aliases":["history of stuffing","old stuffing","traditional materials","horsehair history","feathers history","straw stuffing"],"text":"For most of upholstery's history, padding came from natural materials close at hand. Feathers and down filled cushions and beds for wealthy households. Curled horsehair became the prized stuffing for seats because it is springy and durable. Wool, cotton, flax tow, coir and various dried grasses, mosses and straw were cheaper alternatives, and the mix varied by region and period. Seats were built up in layers over jute webbing and burlap, with stitched edges and a final soft layer. Identifying these materials in an old piece can give clues to its age and quality. These traditional materials gave way largely to foam and synthetic fibres in the twentieth century."},{"id":"kb-history-010","topic":"history","title":"Coil springs in 19th-century seating","aliases":["coil spring history","springs history","sprung upholstery history","Samuel Pratt","spring seats 19th century","invention of springs"],"text":"Coil springs changed upholstery in the nineteenth century. An English patent by Samuel Pratt in the late 1820s, often dated 1828, is widely cited as an early use of coiled springs in seating, though springs had appeared in carriages and other uses before. As spring wire became cheaper to make through the century, sprung seats spread from luxury pieces to ordinary furniture. Springs made seats deeper and softer, and furniture became fuller and more rounded to hold them. Tying springs by hand with twine developed into the eight-way hand-tied method still used in quality furniture. Many Victorian and early twentieth-century pieces that reach shops today still have their original coils."},{"id":"kb-history-011","topic":"history","title":"Victorian upholstery and deep buttoning","aliases":["Victorian upholstery","deep buttoning history","Victorian furniture","19th century furniture","button back","balloon back"],"text":"Victorian upholstery, roughly the mid to late nineteenth century in Britain and its empire, is known for deep, comfortable, heavily padded seating. Coil springs and plentiful stuffing let makers build plump, rounded shapes, and deep buttoning, now usually called diamond tufting, became fashionable on chairs, sofas and ottomans. Common pieces included button-back chairs, balloon-back chairs, ladies' and gentlemen's chairs, chaises and early chesterfield forms. Covers ranged from velvets and plush to leather and horsehair cloth, often with heavy fringes. Industrial production of springs, fabrics and frames made such furniture available to the growing middle class. Many antiques brought in for restoration date from this period."},{"id":"kb-history-012","topic":"history","title":"Chesterfield name origin","aliases":["Chesterfield origin","why is it called chesterfield","Earl of Chesterfield","Lord Chesterfield sofa","chesterfield history"],"text":"Nobody knows for certain where the name chesterfield comes from. A popular story says a sofa was commissioned by an Earl of Chesterfield, often named as Philip Stanhope, the fourth Earl, in the eighteenth century, so that gentlemen could sit upright without wrinkling their clothes. This story is repeated widely but is unverified legend, with no solid documentary evidence. Other explanations link the name to the town of Chesterfield in England, which is also unproven. What is reasonably clear is that the word was being used for a type of padded sofa by the late nineteenth century. Stitch should share the Earl story only as tradition."},{"id":"kb-history-013","topic":"history","title":"Chesterfield as a Canadian word for sofa","aliases":["chesterfield Canada","Canadian word for couch","chesterfield meaning Canada","couch sofa chesterfield","Canadianism"],"text":"In Canada, chesterfield has long been used as a general word for any sofa or couch, not just the tufted, rolled-arm style. This usage is a well-known Canadianism, and older Canadians in particular, including many Toronto customers, may say they want their chesterfield redone when they mean an ordinary three-seat sofa. Elsewhere, chesterfield usually means the specific style with deep-buttoned back, rolled arms the same height as the back, and often a leather cover. Younger Canadians more often say couch or sofa. When a customer mentions a chesterfield, Stitch should gently ask for a photo or description so the shop knows whether it is a classic tufted chesterfield or a regular sofa."},{"id":"kb-history-014","topic":"history","title":"Lawson sofa history","aliases":["Lawson history","Thomas W. Lawson","Lawson origin","Lawson style origin"],"text":"The Lawson sofa is traditionally said to be named after Thomas W. Lawson, a wealthy Boston businessman and financier active around the turn of the twentieth century, who is said to have commissioned a more comfortable, less formal sofa than the stiff Victorian styles of his day. The details of the commission are not well documented, so the story should be treated as tradition. The style it names is clear, though: loose back cushions, arms lower than the back, and a comfortable, simple shape. The Lawson became one of the most common American sofa forms of the twentieth century and is still a common shape for reupholstery."},{"id":"kb-history-015","topic":"history","title":"Arts and Crafts and the Morris chair","aliases":["Arts and Crafts","Morris chair","William Morris","reclining chair history","Mission furniture"],"text":"The Arts and Crafts movement, which began in Britain in the second half of the nineteenth century and was associated with William Morris and his circle, reacted against heavy, machine-made Victorian furniture and promoted honest construction and handcraft. Morris's firm also designed influential textiles still reproduced as upholstery fabrics today. The Morris chair, an early reclining armchair with an adjustable back and loose cushions, was sold by his firm and later widely copied, including in American Mission-style furniture. These pieces usually have exposed wooden frames with loose cushions, so reupholstery work is mostly new cushions and covers rather than full frame upholstery."},{"id":"kb-history-016","topic":"history","title":"Early 20th century: club chairs and Art Deco","aliases":["club chair history","Art Deco upholstery","1920s furniture","fauteuil club","1930s furniture","early 20th century"],"text":"In the early twentieth century, upholstered furniture became deeper, simpler and more geometric. The club chair, a low, deep, often leather-covered armchair, is associated with gentlemen's clubs and with French designs of the 1920s, sometimes called the fauteuil club. Art Deco furniture of the 1920s and 1930s used bold curves, channelled backs, rich velvets and exotic woods, and the barrel chair and channel-back styles were popular. Upholstery still relied on springs, hair and cotton felt. Many vintage club chairs that reach shops have original springs and hair stuffing under cracked leather, and can often be restored rather than replaced."},{"id":"kb-history-017","topic":"history","title":"Latex foam rubber","aliases":["latex foam history","foam rubber history","Dunlopillo","1920s foam","first foam cushions","natural latex foam"],"text":"Latex foam rubber was developed in the late 1920s, when a British rubber company patented a process for whipping natural rubber latex into a foam and curing it. It was soon used in car seats, theatre seats, mattresses and furniture cushions, and became more common after the Second World War. Latex foam was springy, resilient and comfortable compared with traditional stuffing, and it let manufacturers make cushions and padding faster. Many mid-century pieces were originally made with latex foam, which over decades hardens, crumbles and turns to orange or yellow dust. Shops replacing old cushions often find this crumbling latex inside."},{"id":"kb-history-018","topic":"history","title":"Polyurethane foam","aliases":["polyurethane foam history","poly foam history","urethane foam","when was foam invented","Otto Bayer","1950s foam"],"text":"Polyurethane chemistry was developed in the late 1930s in the laboratory of the German chemist Otto Bayer. Flexible polyurethane foam was developed further in the following decades and became widely used in furniture during the 1950s and 1960s. It was cheaper and more versatile than latex, could be made in many densities and firmnesses, and could be cut to any shape. It largely replaced traditional stuffing in mass-produced furniture, and it is now the most common cushioning material in upholstery. Grades have improved over time, with high-density and high-resilience foams offering better durability than the lighter foams used in budget furniture."},{"id":"kb-history-019","topic":"history","title":"Sinuous springs history","aliases":["sinuous spring history","no-sag history","zig-zag spring history","S spring invention","mid-century springs"],"text":"Sinuous springs, the zig-zag wire springs found in most modern sofas, were developed in the United States in the early-to-mid twentieth century and became widespread in furniture after the Second World War. They were cheaper and faster to install than hand-tied coil springs, needed no tying, and allowed slimmer, lower seats. That suited the clean, low lines of mid-century modern design. Early sources and brand histories differ on exact dates, so Stitch should simply say mid-twentieth century. Today sinuous springs are standard in most production sofas, while eight-way hand-tied coils are associated with higher-end and custom work."},{"id":"kb-history-020","topic":"history","title":"Staples replace tacks","aliases":["staple history","pneumatic stapler history","tacks to staples","modern upholstery tools","20th century tools"],"text":"For centuries, upholstery was fixed with hand-driven tacks. During the twentieth century, staples, and especially pneumatic air-powered staplers, largely replaced tacks in both factory and shop work, because they are much faster and do less damage to fabric when placed well. This change, together with foam and sinuous springs, made upholstery quicker and cheaper to produce. Traditional upholsterers and antique restorers still use tacks in some situations, for example on fragile or historic frames, or where they want to keep to period methods. The number of staple and tack holes in a frame can give a rough idea of how many times a piece has been reupholstered."},{"id":"kb-history-021","topic":"history","title":"Mid-century modern upholstery","aliases":["mid-century history","mid century modern history","1950s upholstery","1960s furniture","moulded plywood","foam shell chairs","Eames","Saarinen"],"text":"Mid-century modern design, roughly the late 1940s to the 1960s, used new materials and slimmer forms. Designers experimented with moulded plywood, fibreglass and metal shells covered in foam and fabric or leather. Well-known examples include Eero Saarinen's Womb chair of the late 1940s and the Eames Lounge Chair of 1956. Many production pieces used foam rubber, polyurethane foam, elastic webbing and sinuous springs instead of deep coil springs, giving thin seats, tight backs and tapered legs. Reupholstering mid-century pieces often means replacing crumbling foam and brittle rubber webbing, and some designer pieces have specific original construction that collectors want preserved."},{"id":"kb-history-022","topic":"history","title":"Synthetic fabrics and vinyl","aliases":["synthetic fabric history","nylon history","polyester upholstery history","vinyl history","olefin history","acrylic fabric history"],"text":"Synthetic fibres changed upholstery fabrics in the twentieth century. Nylon was introduced in the late 1930s, and polyester, acrylic and olefin, also called polypropylene, followed in the decades after the war. They brought durability, stain resistance, colourfastness and lower cost. Vinyl, a plastic-coated fabric, became common after the war for kitchen chairs, diners, cars and boats, because it wipes clean and resists water. Rayon and viscose, earlier semi-synthetics, appeared in velvets and blends. Today most upholstery fabrics are blends, combining natural fibres for feel with synthetics for strength. Identifying fibre content matters for cleaning and durability."},{"id":"kb-history-023","topic":"history","title":"Microfibre and synthetic suede","aliases":["microfibre history","microfiber history","faux suede history","synthetic suede","ultrasuede history"],"text":"Ultra-fine synthetic fibres, known as microfibres, were developed in the mid-to-late twentieth century, and synthetic suede fabrics made from them became commercially available in the 1970s, starting in Japan. Synthetic suedes and microfibre upholstery fabrics, usually polyester or nylon, became very popular for family furniture in the 1990s and 2000s because they are soft, durable and fairly resistant to stains and pet claws. Today microfibre covers a wide range of quality, from inexpensive fabrics to high-end performance suedes. Customers often ask for microfibre when they want a soft, practical fabric for children or pets."},{"id":"kb-history-024","topic":"history","title":"Fire safety standards history","aliases":["flammability history","TB117 history","flame retardants history","California TB117 1975","fire standard history"],"text":"Concern about furniture fires led to flammability rules in the twentieth century. California's Technical Bulletin 117, from 1975, required foam and fillings to resist a small open flame, which led many manufacturers across North America to add flame-retardant chemicals to foam. Over time, concerns grew about the health and environmental effects of some of those chemicals, and about whether they helped much in real fires. In 2013 California replaced the open-flame test with TB117-2013, which focuses on smouldering ignition, as from a cigarette, and can usually be met through fabric and barrier choices without flame-retardant chemicals in foam. Canadian rules differ, so shops should check current requirements."},{"id":"kb-history-025","topic":"history","title":"Performance and outdoor fabrics history","aliases":["performance fabric history","solution dyed acrylic history","Sunbrella history","Crypton history","outdoor fabric history"],"text":"Outdoor and performance fabrics developed from the mid-twentieth century onward. Solution-dyed acrylic fabrics, where colour is added to the fibre before it is spun, were introduced in the early 1960s, initially for awnings, and later became popular for outdoor and then indoor furniture because they resist fading and can handle cleaning. Sunbrella is the best-known brand of this type. Engineered stain-and-moisture-resistant fabrics for healthcare and hospitality followed, with Crypton developed in the 1990s. Since the 2010s, performance fabric has become a mainstream category for homes with children and pets, covering many different technologies with different strengths."},{"id":"kb-history-026","topic":"history","title":"Coach trimmers and automotive upholstery history","aliases":["coach trimmer","auto trimmer","carriage upholstery","automotive upholstery history","car interior history","trimmer"],"text":"Automotive upholstery grew out of carriage and coach work. Before cars, coach trimmers upholstered horse-drawn carriages, using leather, cloth, horsehair and springs. Early automobiles were trimmed by the same tradespeople, and auto upholsterers are still often called trimmers today. Over the twentieth century car seats moved from hand-built springs and hair to moulded foam, vinyl, cloth and factory-sewn covers fastened with hog rings or clips. Classic car restoration keeps many of the older techniques alive, and some restorers specialise in reproducing original patterns, pleats and materials for specific makes and years."},{"id":"kb-history-027","topic":"history","title":"Conservation upholstery","aliases":["conservation upholstery","museum upholstery","non-invasive upholstery","historic upholstery conservation","preservation upholstery"],"text":"In the later twentieth century, museum conservators began treating historic upholstery as evidence worth preserving, not just a cover to replace. Conservation upholstery aims to keep original materials, such as webbing, stuffing, show covers and tack patterns, wherever possible, and to make any new work reversible. Conservators developed non-invasive techniques, for example custom-shaped forms or covers fastened without adding new tacks or staples to the historic frame. Most commercial upholstery shops do not work to museum standards, but the same values can guide work on valuable or sentimental antiques: document what is there, keep what you can, and avoid damaging the frame."},{"id":"kb-history-028","topic":"history","title":"Upholstery trade today","aliases":["modern upholstery trade","reupholstery today","state of the trade","fast furniture","sustainability reupholstery","upholstery revival"],"text":"Today, much furniture is mass-produced and inexpensive, and some of it is not built to be reupholstered. At the same time, many people value well-made older pieces, sentimental family furniture and keeping usable items out of landfill, which keeps local upholstery shops busy with reupholstery, cushion replacement, custom headboards and commercial work. Skilled upholsterers remain in demand, and many learn through apprenticeships, college programs or working in shops. Stitch should not quote statistics about the size of the industry or the environmental impact of furniture waste unless the shop provides a trusted source, since numbers vary widely by study."},{"id":"kb-history-029","topic":"history","title":"Quick reference: key upholstery dates","aliases":["upholstery dates","timeline summary","key dates","important dates upholstery","history cheat sheet"],"text":"A short list of dates that are reasonably well established: the Worshipful Company of Upholders in London received its royal charter in 1626. Thomas Chippendale's Director pattern book was first published in 1754. An English patent for coiled springs in seating is commonly dated 1828. Latex foam rubber was developed in the late 1920s. Polyurethane chemistry dates to the late 1930s, with flexible polyurethane foam widely used in furniture by the 1950s and 1960s. Sinuous springs spread after the Second World War. California TB117 dates from 1975 and was replaced by TB117-2013. Chesterfield and Lawson origin stories, by contrast, are traditionally said and should be presented that way."},{"id":"kb-materials-001","topic":"materials","title":"Cotton upholstery fabric","aliases":["cotton","cotton fabric","cotton canvas","cotton duck","cotton twill","cotton blend","100% cotton"],"text":"Cotton is a natural plant fibre used in many upholstery fabrics, from canvas and twill to prints, velvets and blends. It is breathable, soft, takes dye well and comes in endless prints. On its own, cotton can wrinkle, fade in sun, stain fairly easily and wear faster than synthetics, so upholstery cottons are often heavy weaves or blended with polyester or other fibres for durability. Cotton can shrink if wet-cleaned, so cleaning codes matter. Heavy cotton duck and canvas are popular for slipcovers. For high-use family sofas, a cotton blend or performance-treated cotton often lasts better than a lightweight cotton print."},{"id":"kb-materials-002","topic":"materials","title":"Linen and linen blends","aliases":["linen","linen fabric","linen blend","flax","linen upholstery","Belgian linen","washed linen"],"text":"Linen is made from flax fibre. It has a relaxed, natural texture, is cool to the touch and very strong, and is popular for its casual, elegant look. It also wrinkles easily, can bag or relax over time on furniture, especially in humid weather, and may shrink or water-mark if cleaned the wrong way. Linen fades in strong sun. For these reasons, upholsterers often recommend linen blends, such as linen with cotton, viscose or polyester, which add stability, or heavier-weight upholstery linens. Linen is beautiful on headboards, chairs and slipcovers, but customers should know it will wrinkle and soften with use, which many people consider part of its charm."},{"id":"kb-materials-003","topic":"materials","title":"Velvet","aliases":["velvet","velour","cotton velvet","poly velvet","crushed velvet","performance velvet","silk velvet","plush"],"text":"Velvet is a woven fabric with a short, dense, cut pile that gives a soft, rich surface and deep colour. It can be made from cotton, polyester, viscose, silk, mohair or blends. Polyester velvets are often more durable and easier to clean; cotton velvets have a matte, classic look; viscose velvets have high sheen but can crush and mark easily. Velvet shows pressure marks, called bruising or crushing, and pile direction affects colour, so panels must all run the same way. Many modern velvets are quite durable and some are performance-treated. Velvet suits tufting and channelling beautifully. Plush and velour are related pile fabrics."},{"id":"kb-materials-004","topic":"materials","title":"Mohair","aliases":["mohair","mohair velvet","mohair plush","angora goat fibre","luxury velvet"],"text":"Mohair is a natural fibre from the angora goat. Mohair velvet or plush is one of the most durable and luxurious upholstery fabrics: it is very resilient, holds colour well, resists crushing and is naturally fairly flame-resistant compared with many fibres. It has been used on high-end and historic furniture, theatres and railway seating. It is expensive and usually sold through designer channels. Mohair has a distinctive pile with a slight lustre, and as with any pile fabric, all pieces must be cut in the same direction. It suits classic and mid-century designs. Care follows the manufacturer's cleaning code, and professional cleaning is often recommended."},{"id":"kb-materials-005","topic":"materials","title":"Chenille","aliases":["chenille","chenille fabric","chenille yarn","soft woven","tufted yarn fabric"],"text":"Chenille is made from a fuzzy yarn with short fibres sticking out around a core, woven into a soft, textured fabric. The name comes from the French word for caterpillar. It is soft, warm-looking and popular for comfortable family furniture. Chenille can be made from cotton, rayon, polyester, acrylic or blends. Quality varies widely: tightly woven chenilles with good backing wear well, while loose ones can lose their pile in high-wear areas, show bald spots or snag on pets' claws. Some chenilles are not suitable for water-based cleaning. Checking the durability rating and cleaning code is especially important when choosing chenille."},{"id":"kb-materials-006","topic":"materials","title":"Bouclé","aliases":["boucle","bouclé","boucle fabric","looped yarn fabric","teddy fabric","sherpa","curly fabric"],"text":"Bouclé is a fabric made from looped or curled yarns, giving a nubby, textured surface. It can be wool, cotton, polyester or a blend. It has been popular in mid-century and recently in modern furniture for its soft, cosy look. Bouclé hides minor marks well, but the loops can catch on pet claws, jewellery and Velcro, and loose weaves may pill or show wear on arms. Lighter cream and white bouclés show dirt. Cleaning depends on fibre content and code. For homes with cats or dogs, a tighter-woven or performance bouclé is a safer choice. Upholstering bouclé usually works well, but very thick loops can make welt bulky."},{"id":"kb-materials-007","topic":"materials","title":"Tweed and wool fabrics","aliases":["tweed","wool","wool fabric","woollen","wool blend","felted wool","herringbone","plaid wool","worsted"],"text":"Wool is a natural animal fibre that is durable, resilient, naturally fairly flame-resistant and good at hiding wear. Upholstery wools include tweeds, flannels, felted wools, herringbones and plaids, as well as wool blends with nylon or polyester for strength. Tweed is a rough, textured woollen fabric originally associated with Scotland, popular on mid-century and traditional chairs. Wool resists soiling well but can shrink or felt if cleaned with too much water and heat, so cleaning codes matter. Moths can attack wool, especially in storage. Wool suits both classic and modern pieces and generally ages well."},{"id":"kb-materials-008","topic":"materials","title":"Microfibre and microsuede","aliases":["microfibre","microfiber","microsuede","faux suede","ultrasuede","synthetic suede","polyester suede"],"text":"Microfibre is a fabric made from very fine synthetic fibres, usually polyester or polyester-nylon. Microsuede or faux suede has a soft, brushed surface like suede. It is popular for family and pet homes because the tight weave resists stains and claw snags better than many loose weaves, and pet hair brushes off fairly easily. Quality varies; cheap microfibres can show water marks, crush, or develop shiny spots. Many microfibres are cleaning code S, meaning solvent cleaning only, while others are W or W/S. Spot-cleaning with water on an S-code microfibre can leave rings. Customers should check the code before cleaning."},{"id":"kb-materials-009","topic":"materials","title":"Olefin (polypropylene)","aliases":["olefin","polypropylene","PP fabric","olefin fabric","solution-dyed olefin","outdoor olefin"],"text":"Olefin, also called polypropylene, is a synthetic fibre that is lightweight, strong, very resistant to moisture and stains, and quick-drying. It is often solution-dyed, meaning colour is added before spinning, so it resists fading. It is used in indoor and outdoor upholstery, performance fabrics and commercial seating. Olefin can be damaged by high heat and may feel slightly less soft than some fabrics, though modern versions are much improved. It is generally easy to clean. Because it is affordable and practical, olefin is a good choice for family rooms, basements and patio furniture. It is also used for non-woven dust covers."},{"id":"kb-materials-010","topic":"materials","title":"Acrylic and solution-dyed acrylic","aliases":["acrylic","acrylic fabric","solution-dyed acrylic","solution dyed","outdoor acrylic","acrylic upholstery"],"text":"Acrylic is a synthetic fibre that feels soft, somewhat like wool, and takes colour well. Solution-dyed acrylic has pigment added to the liquid before the fibre is formed, so the colour runs all the way through each fibre rather than sitting on the surface. This makes it very resistant to fading in sunlight and able to handle stronger cleaning, which is why it dominates quality outdoor and marine fabrics. Indoor acrylics and blends are used for durable, soft upholstery. Acrylic can pill in some weaves. Solution-dyed acrylic is a top choice for sunrooms, cottages, boats and sunny windows."},{"id":"kb-materials-011","topic":"materials","title":"Solution-dyed vs piece-dyed fabrics","aliases":["solution dyed","piece dyed","yarn dyed","printed fabric","dye methods","fade resistance"],"text":"How a fabric is coloured affects its fade resistance and cleanability. Solution-dyed fibres have colour added before the fibre is made, so colour goes through the fibre and resists sun and bleach-based cleaners well, depending on the product. Yarn-dyed fabrics have the yarn dyed before weaving; colour penetrates well and patterns are woven in. Piece-dyed fabrics are dyed after weaving. Printed fabrics have colour printed on the surface, which can fade or wear off on high-rub areas. For sunny rooms, outdoor use and households wanting easy cleaning, solution-dyed fabrics are the most fade- and stain-resistant choice. Stitch should not claim a specific fabric is bleach-safe unless its label says so."},{"id":"kb-materials-012","topic":"materials","title":"Sunbrella","aliases":["Sunbrella","Sunbrella fabric","outdoor fabric brand","Sunbrella upholstery","Sunbrella indoor"],"text":"Sunbrella is a well-known brand of solution-dyed acrylic fabrics, originally made for awnings and now widely used for outdoor cushions, boat upholstery and indoor performance upholstery. It is known for fade resistance, water and stain resistance, and mildew resistance, with the fabric itself not supporting mildew growth, though mildew can grow on dirt left on it. The manufacturer publishes cleaning guidelines that include mild soap and, for some stains, diluted bleach solutions; staff and customers should follow the current official guidance for the specific fabric. Sunbrella fabrics are not waterproof by themselves for cushions, so outdoor cushions also need suitable quick-drying foam."},{"id":"kb-materials-013","topic":"materials","title":"Crypton","aliases":["Crypton","Crypton fabric","Crypton performance","stain resistant fabric brand","Crypton Home"],"text":"Crypton is a brand of performance fabric technology, originally developed for healthcare and hospitality settings and now widely sold for homes. Crypton treats fabrics to resist stains, moisture penetration and odours, and some versions include a moisture barrier so spills do not soak into the cushion. Different Crypton fabrics vary in look, from wovens to velvets. Cleaning instructions are published by the brand and should be followed for the specific product. It is popular with families, pet owners and commercial clients. As with any performance fabric, Stitch should describe it as stain-resistant, not stain-proof, and point to the fabric's own care instructions."},{"id":"kb-materials-014","topic":"materials","title":"Performance fabrics (general)","aliases":["performance fabric","stain resistant fabric","pet friendly fabric","kid friendly fabric","easy clean fabric","durable fabric","indoor-outdoor fabric"],"text":"Performance fabric is a broad marketing term for upholstery fabrics designed to resist stains, spills, fading and wear better than ordinary fabrics. It covers several technologies: solution-dyed acrylics and olefins, polyester fabrics with stain-resistant finishes, fabrics with moisture barriers, and treated natural fibres. Because the term is not regulated, performance claims vary, and some finishes wear off over years or with harsh cleaning. Customers should look at the fabric's cleaning code, durability rating and specific claims rather than the label alone. Performance fabrics are a great choice for homes with children and pets, dining chairs and commercial use."},{"id":"kb-materials-015","topic":"materials","title":"Polyester upholstery fabric","aliases":["polyester","poly fabric","polyester blend","poly upholstery","recycled polyester"],"text":"Polyester is the most common synthetic fibre in upholstery, used alone or blended with cotton, linen, rayon and others. It is strong, holds colour well, resists wrinkles and stretching, and is usually affordable. Polyester velvets, microfibres and chenilles are common. It can pill in some weaves and may feel less breathable than natural fibres, and it can be damaged by high heat. Recycled polyester, made from recycled plastics, is increasingly available. Cleaning codes vary by fabric construction and finish rather than fibre alone. Polyester blends are a good everyday choice when customers want durability at a moderate price."},{"id":"kb-materials-016","topic":"materials","title":"Nylon upholstery fabric","aliases":["nylon","nylon fabric","nylon blend","polyamide","nylon velvet"],"text":"Nylon is a strong synthetic fibre with excellent abrasion resistance, often blended into upholstery fabrics to add durability. It is used in velvets, commercial fabrics, microfibres and some automotive fabrics. Nylon can fade in strong sunlight and may degrade with prolonged UV exposure, which also affects nylon thread outdoors, so it is less suitable for sunny or outdoor uses unless treated. It can generate static and may pill in some constructions. In blends with wool or polyester, nylon helps fabrics reach high durability ratings for high-traffic or commercial seating."},{"id":"kb-materials-017","topic":"materials","title":"Rayon and viscose","aliases":["rayon","viscose","modal","lyocell","artificial silk","viscose velvet","rayon blend"],"text":"Rayon, also called viscose, is a semi-synthetic fibre made from wood pulp cellulose. It drapes well, takes rich colour and gives fabrics like velvets and damasks a silky sheen. In upholstery, it is often blended for lustre. Its weaknesses are that it loses strength when wet, can shrink, and is prone to water spotting, so many rayon-rich fabrics are cleaning code S or X. Viscose velvets can crush and show marks. For high-use pieces, a rayon fabric should be blended with stronger fibres. Customers should be warned not to spot-clean viscose with water unless the code allows it."},{"id":"kb-materials-018","topic":"materials","title":"Silk upholstery fabric","aliases":["silk","silk fabric","silk damask","silk velvet","silk blend","dupioni"],"text":"Silk is a luxurious natural protein fibre with a beautiful sheen and rich colour. In upholstery it appears in damasks, brocades, velvets and taffetas, mainly on formal, decorative or antique pieces. Silk is relatively delicate: it fades and weakens in sunlight, can water-spot, and wears quickly on high-use seats. It usually needs professional dry cleaning. Silk fabrics are often backed to give them stability for upholstery. For pieces that are actually sat on often, silk blends or silk-look synthetics are more practical. For antiques and formal rooms out of direct sun, silk can be a beautiful, period-appropriate choice."},{"id":"kb-materials-019","topic":"materials","title":"Damask, brocade and jacquard","aliases":["damask","brocade","jacquard","tapestry","matelassé","woven pattern","figured fabric"],"text":"Jacquard fabrics have patterns woven into the cloth on a jacquard loom rather than printed. Damask is a reversible jacquard with a pattern created by contrasting satin and matte weaves, often in one colour. Brocade has raised, often multi-coloured patterns with extra threads, sometimes metallic. Tapestry fabrics have pictorial woven designs. Matelassé has a quilted-looking raised surface. These fabrics are durable when tightly woven and suit traditional and formal furniture. Patterns can be large, so repeats and centring affect yardage. Brocades with floats, loose threads on the surface, can snag in pet homes."},{"id":"kb-materials-020","topic":"materials","title":"Canvas, duck and denim","aliases":["canvas","duck canvas","cotton duck","denim","heavy cotton","sailcloth","twill"],"text":"Canvas and duck are heavy, tightly woven plain-weave cottons, sometimes blended with polyester. They are durable, casual, and popular for slipcovers, cushions, cottage furniture and children's spaces. Denim is a twill-woven cotton, with indigo-dyed warp in classic versions, that can transfer dye onto lighter fabrics and clothing at first, especially when new. Twill weaves have diagonal ribs and wear well. These fabrics often shrink if washed, so they should be pre-washed if a slipcover will be laundered. They suit casual looks, but very stiff canvas can be hard to pleat neatly on tight curves."},{"id":"kb-materials-021","topic":"materials","title":"Faux fur, sheepskin and shearling","aliases":["faux fur","sheepskin","shearling","faux sheepskin","fur upholstery","long pile fabric","teddy"],"text":"Faux fur and faux sheepskin are long-pile synthetic fabrics popular for accent chairs, ottomans and pillows. Real sheepskin and shearling are tanned hides with the wool left on. Long piles hide seams and welt but make sewing bulky, and they shed when cut, so shops trim the pile away from seam allowances. Pile direction matters. Pet claws and heavy use can mat the pile. Cleaning is usually gentle and fabric- or hide-specific; real sheepskin needs specialist care. These materials are best for decorative or lower-use pieces rather than main family seating."},{"id":"kb-materials-022","topic":"materials","title":"Fabric backing","aliases":["fabric backing","latex backing","acrylic backing","knit backing","backed fabric","fabric stabilizer","paper backing"],"text":"Many upholstery fabrics have a backing applied to the reverse to stabilise the weave, prevent seam slippage and fraying, and reduce stretching. Common backings are thin latex or acrylic coatings, or a lightweight knit fabric laminated to the back. Apparel fabrics, loose weaves and some imported decorative fabrics can be backed by a specialist service to make them suitable for upholstery. Backed fabric is stiffer, so it may pleat differently. Washing can damage backings, which is one reason upholstery covers are not generally machine washed. When a customer brings a lightweight fabric, the shop may recommend backing it."},{"id":"kb-materials-023","topic":"materials","title":"Leather: full grain","aliases":["full grain leather","full-grain","full grain","top quality leather","uncorrected leather","natural grain"],"text":"Full-grain leather is the outer layer of the hide with the natural grain surface left intact, not sanded or buffed. It shows natural markings such as scars, wrinkles and pores, which many people value as character. It is the strongest and most durable type of leather and develops a patina with age and use. Full-grain leathers can be aniline, semi-aniline or pigmented depending on the finish. Because only the best hides are suitable, it is the most expensive grade. It breathes well and becomes softer over time. Some customers mistake natural marks for flaws, so the shop should explain this when they choose it."},{"id":"kb-materials-024","topic":"materials","title":"Leather: top grain","aliases":["top grain leather","top-grain","top grain","split grain","sanded leather","buffed leather"],"text":"Top-grain is a term used in different ways. In its common furniture sense, it means the top layer of the hide, split away from the lower layers, with the surface lightly sanded or buffed to remove imperfections and then finished. It is thinner, more uniform and more stain-resistant than full-grain but less breathable, and does not develop as much patina. Some sellers use top grain loosely to include full grain, so the label alone does not settle the question. Stitch should describe these terms generally and suggest customers ask the shop or supplier for the exact leather specification."},{"id":"kb-materials-025","topic":"materials","title":"Leather: corrected grain","aliases":["corrected grain","corrected leather","embossed leather","pigmented top grain","imitation grain","printed grain"],"text":"Corrected-grain leather has had its surface sanded to remove imperfections, then an artificial grain is embossed and a pigmented finish applied. It is made from hides with more surface flaws and gives a uniform look at a lower cost. It is durable, consistent in colour and resists stains and fading better than natural-finish leathers, but it feels less natural and does not develop a patina. Corrected grain is common in mid-priced leather furniture, automotive seating and commercial use. It is easy to clean. When repairing, matching the embossed grain and pigment colour is easier than matching aniline leather."},{"id":"kb-materials-026","topic":"materials","title":"Leather: aniline","aliases":["aniline leather","aniline","full aniline","pure aniline","naked leather","unfinished leather","dyed leather"],"text":"Aniline leather is dyed through with soluble dyes and left without a pigmented surface coat, so the natural grain and markings are fully visible. It is very soft, warm and breathable and feels the most natural, but it absorbs liquids and oils easily, stains readily, fades in sunlight and scratches. It develops a rich patina. Aniline suits careful adult households and pieces out of direct sun, rather than homes with young children or pets. Cleaning should follow leather-specific guidance, often just dusting and blotting spills immediately. Some aniline leathers have a light protective finish, which shifts them toward semi-aniline."},{"id":"kb-materials-027","topic":"materials","title":"Leather: semi-aniline","aliases":["semi-aniline","semi aniline","protected aniline","lightly pigmented leather","aniline plus"],"text":"Semi-aniline leather is dyed like aniline but has a thin protective coat that includes a small amount of pigment. It keeps much of the natural look and softness of aniline while offering better resistance to stains, fading and wear. Colour is more even than aniline, and natural markings are still partly visible. It is a popular middle ground for quality residential furniture. Care is easier than aniline but still benefits from regular dusting and prompt blotting of spills, and following the manufacturer's leather care advice. It is a good recommendation for customers who want natural-looking leather in a busy household."},{"id":"kb-materials-028","topic":"materials","title":"Leather: pigmented (protected)","aliases":["pigmented leather","protected leather","finished leather","coated leather","painted leather","semi-finished"],"text":"Pigmented leather, also called protected or finished leather, has a surface coating that contains pigment, much like a durable paint, plus a protective topcoat. It is the most stain-, fade- and wear-resistant leather finish and the easiest to clean, which makes it the common choice for family homes, cars and commercial seating. The trade-off is a less natural feel and look, with uniform colour and less breathability. Most corrected-grain leathers are pigmented. Scuffs and wear can usually be repaired and recoloured by leather technicians. It does not develop the patina that aniline leathers do."},{"id":"kb-materials-029","topic":"materials","title":"Nubuck and suede","aliases":["nubuck","suede","split suede","brushed leather","napped leather","suede leather"],"text":"Nubuck is top-grain leather that has been lightly sanded or buffed on the grain side to create a soft, velvety surface. Suede is made from the inner split side of the hide, brushed to a nap, and is softer and less durable than nubuck. Both are beautiful and soft, but they mark easily, absorb liquids and oils, and are harder to clean than smooth leathers. They need specialist care, such as brushing and dedicated cleaners. In upholstery they suit low-use accent pieces. Customers wanting a similar look with easier care might consider microsuede or a performance faux suede."},{"id":"kb-materials-030","topic":"materials","title":"Bonded leather","aliases":["bonded leather","reconstituted leather","blended leather","leather match","PU bonded leather"],"text":"Bonded leather is not solid leather. It is made from shredded leather scraps and fibres mixed with binders, formed into a sheet on a backing, and coated with a polyurethane surface that is embossed to look like leather. It is inexpensive but tends to peel, flake and crack, often within a few years, especially where skin touches it. Bonded leather cannot be repaired well, so when it starts peeling, recovering in fabric, vinyl or real leather is the usual fix. Related marketing terms include leather match, where only seating surfaces are leather and other panels are vinyl. Customers are often surprised to learn their leather sofa is bonded."},{"id":"kb-materials-031","topic":"materials","title":"Faux leather (PU and vinyl)","aliases":["faux leather","PU leather","polyurethane leather","leatherette","pleather","vegan leather","synthetic leather"],"text":"Faux leather is a fabric backing coated with polyurethane or PVC vinyl and embossed to look like leather. PU leather is usually softer and more breathable; PVC vinyl is tougher and more resistant to water and stains. Quality varies widely. Better products have strong knit backings and durable coatings that resist cracking; cheaper ones can peel or crack, particularly PU in sunlight or dry heat. Vegan leather is a marketing term that can mean PU, PVC or newer plant-based materials. Faux leather is cleanable and pet-tolerant to a point, though claws can puncture it. For heavy commercial or marine use, contract or marine-grade vinyl is the reliable choice."},{"id":"kb-materials-032","topic":"materials","title":"Polyurethane foam basics","aliases":["foam","polyurethane foam","poly foam","PU foam","upholstery foam","cushion foam","seat foam","urethane foam"],"text":"Most modern cushions and padding use flexible polyurethane foam. Foam is described by two main properties: density, which relates to durability, and firmness, measured as ILD or IFD, which relates to how hard it feels. These are separate: a dense foam can be soft and a light foam can be firm. Good seat foam is usually higher density so it keeps its shape for years; backs and arms can use lighter or softer foam. Foam is cut to size, often glued in layers, and wrapped in polyester batting. Over time all foam softens and loses height; cheaper, low-density foam breaks down much faster. Replacing foam is one of the most effective comfort upgrades."},{"id":"kb-materials-033","topic":"materials","title":"Foam density","aliases":["foam density","density","pounds per cubic foot","lb/ft3","kg/m3","foam weight","foam quality"],"text":"Foam density is the weight of a block of foam per unit volume, given in North America as pounds per cubic foot, and elsewhere as kilograms per cubic metre, where one pound per cubic foot is about 16 kilograms per cubic metre. Density is the best single guide to how long foam will last: higher-density foam generally holds its shape and firmness longer. Density does not tell you firmness; that is ILD or IFD. In the trade, foams around 1.8 pounds per cubic foot and above are often called high density, and premium seat foams are usually higher still. Exact grade names vary by maker, so Stitch should describe these as typical, not fixed, figures."},{"id":"kb-materials-034","topic":"materials","title":"ILD / IFD (foam firmness)","aliases":["ILD","IFD","indentation load deflection","indentation force deflection","foam firmness","firmness rating","compression"],"text":"ILD, indentation load deflection, and IFD, indentation force deflection, describe how firm foam feels. IFD is the current name used in the standard test, and ILD is the older term still common in shops; in everyday use they mean the same thing. The number is the force, in pounds, needed to compress a standard sample of foam by 25 percent of its thickness with a standard indenter. Higher numbers mean firmer foam. Seat cushions generally use firmer foam than backs, but the right number depends on the person, the frame, the cushion thickness and the wrap. Shops often let customers feel samples, since numbers alone do not tell people how a seat will feel."},{"id":"kb-materials-035","topic":"materials","title":"High-density (HD) foam","aliases":["HD foam","high density foam","high-density","firm foam","seat foam grade","conventional foam"],"text":"High-density, or HD, foam is conventional polyurethane foam with a higher density than budget foams, making it durable and suitable for seat cushions. It comes in a range of firmnesses, from medium to very firm. HD foam is the standard choice in many upholstery shops for seats on sofas and chairs, often wrapped in polyester for a softer surface and rounder look. It balances cost and lifespan well. Lower-density foams are cheaper but flatten faster and are better reserved for backs, arms or light-use pieces. Customers who want a long-lasting seat should ask for high-density or high-resilience foam."},{"id":"kb-materials-036","topic":"materials","title":"High-resilience (HR) foam","aliases":["HR foam","high resilience foam","high-resilience","premium foam","HR seat foam","resilient foam"],"text":"High-resilience, or HR, foam is a premium polyurethane foam with an irregular cell structure that makes it springy and quick to recover, giving a lively, supportive feel somewhat like latex. It typically has a higher density than standard HD foam and a high support factor, meaning it gets firmer the more it is compressed, which supports the body well. HR foam is durable and is often used in high-quality residential and contract seating. It costs more than HD foam. In the trade, HR usually refers to foam meeting particular density and resilience thresholds set by the foam industry and individual makers, so exact specifications should come from the supplier."},{"id":"kb-materials-037","topic":"materials","title":"Memory foam","aliases":["memory foam","viscoelastic foam","visco foam","slow recovery foam","gel memory foam","temperature sensitive foam"],"text":"Memory foam, or viscoelastic foam, is a polyurethane foam that softens with body heat and pressure and recovers slowly, so it moulds to the shape of the body. It is common in mattresses and pillows. In upholstery, it is used only as a thin top layer over a supportive core, if at all, because a full memory foam seat can feel like sinking and can make standing up harder. It can feel firmer in a cold room and softer in a warm one, which matters in Canadian winters. Some people find it warm to sit on. It is not usually recommended as the main seat foam for sofas."},{"id":"kb-materials-038","topic":"materials","title":"Latex foam","aliases":["latex","latex foam","natural latex","synthetic latex","Dunlop latex","Talalay latex","foam rubber"],"text":"Latex foam is made from natural rubber latex, synthetic latex, or a blend. It is springy, very resilient, breathable and durable, and gives a buoyant, supportive feel. There are two main production processes, known as Dunlop, which tends to be denser and firmer, and Talalay, which tends to be lighter and more even. Latex is heavier and more expensive than polyurethane foam. People with latex allergies should be cautious with natural latex. Very old latex foam in vintage furniture often hardens and crumbles into dust and should be replaced. Modern latex is a premium choice for customers wanting a natural-material cushion."},{"id":"kb-materials-039","topic":"materials","title":"Closed-cell foam","aliases":["closed cell foam","closed-cell","polyethylene foam","PE foam","flotation foam","cross-linked foam","marine closed cell"],"text":"Closed-cell foam has sealed cells that do not let water in, so it does not absorb water and is buoyant. Common types are polyethylene and cross-linked foams. It is firm, and is used in marine cushions where flotation or water resistance matters, boat bolsters, kneeling pads, some outdoor seating and as a thin moisture barrier layer. It does not compress and recover like seat foam, so it can feel hard on its own; shops often combine it with a softer top layer or use it in thin sections. It is different from open-cell drain-through foam, which lets water flow through rather than keeping it out."},{"id":"kb-materials-040","topic":"materials","title":"Drain-through (reticulated) foam","aliases":["drain-through foam","reticulated foam","dry fast foam","quick dry foam","open cell outdoor foam","outdoor foam","marine foam"],"text":"Reticulated foam, often sold under names like dry-fast or quick-dry foam, has a very open cell structure, so water drains straight through and air dries it quickly. It is the standard for quality outdoor and marine cushions because it reduces trapped water and mildew. For it to work, cushions should be covered in breathable outdoor fabric, often with mesh panels on the bottom or back, and placed on bases that drain, such as slatted seats or plywood with drain holes. It is more expensive than standard foam. Regular indoor foam used outdoors soaks up water like a sponge, takes days to dry, and can grow mildew."},{"id":"kb-materials-041","topic":"materials","title":"Foam lifespan and signs of wear","aliases":["foam lifespan","how long does foam last","worn out foam","flat cushions","foam replacement signs","cushion sag"],"text":"How long foam lasts depends on its density and quality, how much the seat is used, and the weight of the people using it. Higher-density foams last much longer than low-density ones. Signs that foam needs replacing include cushions that stay flattened or dented, the sitter feeling the frame or springs, uneven sinking in the favourite seat, crumbling or yellow dust inside the cover, and cushions that no longer fill their covers. Exposure to sun and heat speeds breakdown. Stitch should avoid promising a specific number of years for any foam and instead explain that better foams generally last noticeably longer."},{"id":"kb-materials-042","topic":"materials","title":"Down fill","aliases":["down","goose down","duck down","down fill","down cushions","down clusters","fill power"],"text":"Down is the soft, fluffy undercoat of ducks and geese, without hard quills. It is very light, soft and luxurious, and gives cushions a relaxed, sink-in look. Pure down cushions need frequent plumping and lose shape quickly when sat on, so in upholstery down is more often blended with feathers or used as a wrap around a foam core. Down needs a down-proof ticking inner. Some people are allergic or sensitive to feathers and down, though cleaning processes reduce this. Responsibly sourced down certifications exist for customers who ask. Down is one of the more expensive fill options."},{"id":"kb-materials-043","topic":"materials","title":"Feather and down-feather blends","aliases":["feather fill","feathers","down feather blend","feather down mix","25/75","50/50 down","blend ratios"],"text":"Feathers have a central quill and are firmer and heavier than down. Upholstery fills are usually blends of feathers and down, described by percentage, such as a blend that is mostly feather with a smaller share of down. More down means softer, lighter and more expensive; more feather means firmer and more economical, but quills can poke through if the ticking is not feather-proof. Feather blends are common in back pillows and loose cushions. They need regular plumping and turning. Many shops recommend channelled inner casings so the fill does not migrate to one end of a long cushion."},{"id":"kb-materials-044","topic":"materials","title":"Polyester fibre fill","aliases":["fibre fill","fiberfill","polyester fill","poly fibre","cluster fibre","blown fibre","ball fibre","down alternative","fibre pillow fill"],"text":"Polyester fibre fill is a synthetic stuffing used in back pillows, attached backs, arms and scatter cushions. Basic fibrefill is loose staple fibres; cluster or ball fibre is formed into small balls that move more like down and resist clumping. Down-alternative fills aim to mimic the feel of down without feathers, which suits people with allergies. Fibre fills are affordable and hypoallergenic, but they can flatten and clump over time, so back pillows filled only with fibre may need refilling every few years. Blends of fibre and down, or fibre wrapped around a foam core, help pillows keep their shape."},{"id":"kb-materials-045","topic":"materials","title":"Double rubs: Wyzenbeek test","aliases":["double rubs","Wyzenbeek","Wyzenbeek test","abrasion test","ASTM D4157","durability rating","rub count"],"text":"Double rubs measure a fabric's resistance to abrasion. In North America, the Wyzenbeek method, ASTM D4157, rubs a fabric sample back and forth with a cotton duck or wire screen abradant; one forward-and-back motion is one double rub, and the test reports how many double rubs the fabric withstands before noticeable wear. Commonly cited guidelines treat around 15,000 double rubs as suitable for general contract or heavy residential use and 30,000 or more for heavy-duty contract use, but these are guidelines, not guarantees. Durability also depends on weave, fibre, backing and pilling resistance. Very high numbers mainly matter in commercial settings."},{"id":"kb-materials-046","topic":"materials","title":"Martindale test","aliases":["Martindale","Martindale rubs","Martindale test","ISO 12947","ASTM D4966","cycles","European abrasion test"],"text":"The Martindale test, common in Europe, the UK and on many imported fabrics, rubs a fabric sample against a standard abradant in a figure-eight pattern, Lissajous pattern, until visible wear or thread breaks. Results are given in cycles or rubs, and the method is covered by standards such as ISO 12947 and ASTM D4966. Commonly cited UK guidelines treat roughly 15,000 to 25,000 rubs as general domestic, above 25,000 as heavy domestic, and higher figures, often 30,000 to 40,000 and up, for contract use, though suppliers and organisations vary. Like Wyzenbeek, it measures abrasion only, not pilling, fading, stains or seam strength."},{"id":"kb-materials-047","topic":"materials","title":"Wyzenbeek vs Martindale conversions","aliases":["Wyzenbeek to Martindale","Martindale to Wyzenbeek","rub conversion","convert double rubs","compare durability ratings"],"text":"Customers and staff often ask how to convert Martindale results to Wyzenbeek double rubs or the reverse. There is no reliable conversion. The two tests use different machines, abradants, motions and end points, and the same fabric can rank differently on each. Rough conversion ratios circulate online, but they are not accurate enough to rely on. The best approach is to compare fabrics within the same test method, and to use the supplier's stated intended use, such as heavy-duty residential or contract, alongside the rating. Stitch should never present a converted number as if it were a tested result."},{"id":"kb-materials-048","topic":"materials","title":"Choosing durability for the use","aliases":["durability guide","how durable should fabric be","fabric for family sofa","fabric for dining chairs","high traffic fabric","light use fabric"],"text":"Match fabric durability to how the piece is used. Decorative or occasional pieces, like a bedroom accent chair, can use more delicate fabrics. Everyday family sofas, dining chairs and pieces used by children or pets need durable, tightly woven fabrics with good abrasion ratings, and ideally cleanable or performance finishes. Commercial seating needs contract-grade fabric or vinyl suitable for the setting, plus any fire requirements. Beyond rub counts, look at weave tightness, pilling resistance, colourfastness, seam slippage and cleaning code. Loose weaves, long floats and delicate fibres wear faster regardless of rub count."},{"id":"kb-materials-049","topic":"materials","title":"Cleaning codes overview (W, S, W/S, X)","aliases":["cleaning codes","cleaning code","W code","S code","WS code","SW","X code","care codes","fabric care label"],"text":"Many upholstery fabrics carry a cleaning code. W means water-based cleaners can be used, such as mild water-based upholstery shampoo or foam. S means solvent-based cleaners only, often called dry-cleaning solvents, with no water, since water can spot, shrink or stain the fabric. W/S, sometimes written S/W or WS, means either water-based or solvent-based cleaners may be used. X means vacuum or light brushing only, with no water or solvent cleaners, and professional advice for anything more. Codes apply to spot-cleaning the fabric on the furniture, not to machine washing covers. Always test any cleaner in a hidden spot first and follow the fabric maker's instructions."},{"id":"kb-materials-050","topic":"materials","title":"Cleaning codes in practice","aliases":["how to clean upholstery by code","spot cleaning","water rings","solvent cleaning","professional cleaning","cleaning advice"],"text":"In practice: for W fabrics, blot spills quickly and use a mild water-based upholstery cleaner sparingly, without soaking, working from the outside of the spot inward. For S fabrics, use a solvent-based upholstery cleaner in a ventilated room and avoid water, which can leave rings, especially on microfibres and viscose. For W/S fabrics, either can work; start mild. For X fabrics, vacuum only and call a professional for stains. With any code, test first in a hidden area, avoid rubbing hard, and never use household products not meant for upholstery. If a customer is unsure of their fabric's code, Stitch can suggest a professional upholstery cleaner."},{"id":"kb-materials-051","topic":"materials","title":"Fire safety: California TB117-2013","aliases":["TB117-2013","TB 117-2013","TB117","California fire standard","smolder test","smoulder resistance","flammability label"],"text":"California Technical Bulletin 117-2013 is a flammability standard for residential upholstered furniture sold in California. It tests resistance to smouldering ignition, the kind caused by a lit cigarette, using the cover fabric, barrier materials and filling. It replaced the older open-flame TB117 from 1975. Because it can typically be met without adding flame-retardant chemicals to foam, many manufacturers changed their materials after 2013. Furniture meeting it carries a label stating compliance. Much North American furniture and many fabrics are labelled TB117-2013 because manufacturers sell across markets. It is a California rule; whether it applies to a job in Canada is a separate question."},{"id":"kb-materials-052","topic":"materials","title":"Fire safety: Canadian context","aliases":["Canadian fire rules","Canada upholstery regulations","Health Canada furniture","Ontario fire code upholstery","flammability Canada","stuffed articles Canada"],"text":"In Canada, consumer products including upholstered furniture fall under federal consumer product safety law administered by Health Canada, and some provinces have had their own labelling rules for stuffed articles, which have changed over the years. Requirements for commercial and public spaces can also come from building and fire codes, the fire marshal, or the client's own specifications. Because these rules are detailed and change over time, Stitch should not state specific Canadian legal requirements. For any question about compliance, especially commercial, healthcare, hotel or restaurant jobs, Stitch should say that the shop will confirm current requirements and the client's specifications before quoting materials."},{"id":"kb-materials-053","topic":"materials","title":"Fire safety: contract and commercial standards","aliases":["commercial fire standards","contract flammability","NFPA 260","UFAC","TB133","CAL 133","public occupancy seating","fire rated fabric"],"text":"Commercial and contract jobs often come with a written specification that names flammability tests the fabric, foam or finished seating must pass. Specs may reference test methods such as NFPA 260 or California TB133, a full-scale test historically used for seating in some public occupancies, and others depending on the setting and jurisdiction. The status and adoption of some of these standards has changed over time. Fabric suppliers publish which tests a fabric has passed. Fire barriers and inherently flame-resistant fabrics can help meet requirements. Stitch should collect the client's spec and pass it to staff rather than confirming compliance itself."},{"id":"kb-materials-054","topic":"materials","title":"Flame retardant chemicals","aliases":["flame retardants","FR foam","chemical free foam","flame retardant free","PBDE","toxic foam","FR chemicals"],"text":"Some customers ask whether foam contains flame-retardant chemicals. In the past, many North American foams had added flame retardants, largely because of California's older TB117 open-flame standard. Some of those chemicals raised health and environmental concerns, and certain ones were phased out or restricted. After TB117-2013, many manufacturers stopped adding them to residential foam. Today many suppliers offer foam labelled as made without added flame retardants. Stitch should not make health claims, but can say the shop can tell the customer what foam they use and whether it is sold as free of added flame retardants."},{"id":"kb-materials-055","topic":"materials","title":"Pilling","aliases":["pilling","pills","fuzz balls","bobbles","lint balls","fabric shaver","pilling resistance"],"text":"Pilling is when short or loose fibres on a fabric surface tangle into small balls from rubbing. It happens most on seats and arms, and is common with some synthetics, blends, wools, bouclés and brushed fabrics. Strong synthetic fibres can hold pills on the surface instead of letting them break away. Pilling is often a surface issue rather than a sign the fabric is failing, and pills can be removed carefully with a fabric shaver or de-piller, testing in a hidden spot. Some fabrics are rated for pilling resistance. Choosing tighter weaves and longer-staple fibres reduces pilling for high-use furniture."},{"id":"kb-materials-056","topic":"materials","title":"Fabric weight","aliases":["fabric weight","ounces per yard","oz/yd","gsm","grams per square metre","heavy fabric","lightweight fabric"],"text":"Fabric weight is given in ounces per linear or square yard, or grams per square metre, often written as gsm. Upholstery fabrics are generally heavier than apparel or drapery fabrics. Heavier fabric often wears longer and hides lumps better, but weight alone does not guarantee durability: a heavy loose weave can wear faster than a lighter tight weave. Very heavy fabrics can be hard to pleat and may add bulk to welt and tufting. Very light fabrics may need a backing to be suitable for upholstery. When a customer asks if a fabric is strong enough, look at weight, weave, abrasion rating and intended use together."},{"id":"kb-materials-057","topic":"materials","title":"Colourfastness and fading","aliases":["colourfastness","colorfastness","lightfastness","fading","sun fading","UV damage","fade resistance","crocking"],"text":"Colourfastness is how well a fabric keeps its colour. Lightfastness is resistance to fading in light, which matters a lot near windows. Crocking is when dye rubs off onto other surfaces, such as new dark denim or some reds. Natural fibres like cotton, linen and silk tend to fade faster in sun than solution-dyed synthetics. To reduce fading, keep furniture out of direct sun, use blinds or UV-filtering window film, and rotate cushions. For sunrooms and south- or west-facing rooms, solution-dyed or high-lightfastness fabrics are worth recommending. Faded fabric cannot be restored, so placement matters."},{"id":"kb-materials-058","topic":"materials","title":"Seam slippage and loose weaves","aliases":["seam slippage","loose weave","seam strength","fabric pulling apart","yarn slippage","open weave"],"text":"Seam slippage is when yarns slide apart near a seam under stress, leaving gaps even though the stitching has not broken. It happens most with loose weaves, slippery yarns and fabrics with long floats. It shows up at cushion seams, welt and stress points like the front edge of seats. Fabrics intended for upholstery are often backed to prevent this. When a customer chooses a loose or open weave, especially their own fabric, the shop may suggest backing it or using it on lower-stress pieces. Stitch should mention this as a possible concern for loose weaves rather than a certainty."},{"id":"kb-materials-059","topic":"materials","title":"Stretch and knit fabrics","aliases":["stretch fabric","knit fabric","stretchy upholstery","spandex","jersey","four-way stretch"],"text":"Most upholstery fabrics are woven, which keeps them stable. Knit and stretch fabrics are used for some modern furniture, office chairs, ready-made stretch slipcovers and curved shapes, because they wrap easily. They are harder to work with in traditional upholstery: they can stretch out of shape, show wrinkles as they relax, and need less tension when fixed. Some knits are bonded to backings for stability. For customers bringing their own stretch fabric, the shop should assess whether it will hold up. Vinyl and many faux leathers also have stretch from their knit backings, which helps them conform to shapes."},{"id":"kb-materials-060","topic":"materials","title":"Fabric protector sprays and finishes","aliases":["fabric protector","stain repellent","fabric guard","protective spray","fluorochemical finish","stain treatment","fabric protection"],"text":"Many fabrics come with factory stain-repellent finishes, and fabric protector sprays can be applied after upholstery. These help liquids bead up so they can be blotted before soaking in, but they do not make fabric stain-proof, and their effect lessens with wear and cleaning. Some finishes have been reformulated in recent years because of concerns about certain chemicals. Before treating, check the fabric maker's guidance; some performance fabrics should not be retreated, and some sprays can change the feel or colour of delicate fabrics. Stitch should present protection as helpful but not a guarantee, and avoid naming specific products."},{"id":"kb-materials-061","topic":"materials","title":"Choosing fabric for pets","aliases":["pet friendly fabric","fabric for cats","fabric for dogs","claw resistant fabric","pet hair fabric","best fabric for pets"],"text":"For homes with pets, tightly woven fabrics with no loops or long floats are best, because claws catch on loose weaves, bouclé and textured chenille. Microfibre, microsuede, tight performance wovens and some durable velvets are often recommended, as is pigmented leather for dogs, though claws can still scratch it. Mid-tone colours and subtle patterns hide pet hair and marks better than very light or very dark solids. Cleanable performance fabrics help with accidents. No fabric is completely claw-proof, so Stitch should describe these as more resistant rather than pet-proof, and suggest the customer test samples with a fingernail or by brushing to see how hair releases."},{"id":"kb-materials-062","topic":"materials","title":"Choosing fabric for allergies and sensitivities","aliases":["allergy fabric","hypoallergenic upholstery","dust mite","feather allergy","latex allergy","sensitive skin fabric"],"text":"Customers with allergies can make choices that help. Avoid down and feathers if sensitive, and use polyester fibre or foam fills instead. Natural latex foam can be a concern for people with latex allergies. Tightly woven or smooth fabrics, leather and vinyl collect less dust and are easy to wipe or vacuum, while long piles and textured fabrics trap more dust and pet dander. Removable, cleanable covers can help. Some fabrics and finishes carry third-party certifications for chemical emissions, which customers can ask about. Stitch should not give medical advice and can suggest customers check with their doctor if they have serious concerns."},{"id":"kb-materials-063","topic":"materials","title":"Is the customer's fabric suitable for upholstery?","aliases":["fabric suitability","can I use this fabric","apparel fabric for upholstery","drapery fabric on chair","COM check","curtain fabric upholstery"],"text":"Not every fabric works for upholstery. Shops check a customer's own fabric for weight and weave tightness, abrasion rating if known, backing, stretch, fibre content, cleaning code and any flaws. Apparel and drapery fabrics are often too light or loosely woven for seats; they may tear, slip at seams or wear quickly, though they can be fine for headboards, decorative pillows or low-use pieces, sometimes with a backing. Vintage fabrics may be weakened by age. The shop also confirms there is enough yardage from one dye lot. Stitch should encourage customers to bring or send their fabric details before committing, so the shop can advise."},{"id":"kb-materials-064","topic":"materials","title":"Vinyl upholstery (PVC) basics","aliases":["vinyl","PVC vinyl","upholstery vinyl","contract vinyl","expanded vinyl","vinyl fabric"],"text":"Upholstery vinyl is a PVC coating on a fabric backing, often a knit for stretch. It is water-resistant, wipes clean and is durable, making it common for kitchen and dining chairs, restaurants, healthcare, offices, cars and boats. Grades vary from light residential to heavy contract and marine. Better vinyls have strong backings, abrasion ratings, and additives for UV, mildew and stain resistance. Some vinyls can be cleaned with diluted bleach solutions and some cannot, so follow the maker's guidance. Vinyl does not breathe, so it can feel warm or sticky, and cheap vinyl can crack in cold or after years of sun."},{"id":"kb-specialty-001","topic":"specialty","title":"Automotive upholstery overview","aliases":["auto upholstery","automotive upholstery","car upholstery","auto trim","car interior repair","vehicle upholstery","trimmer"],"text":"Automotive upholstery, often called auto trim, covers car, truck and van interiors: seats, headliners, door panels, carpets, consoles, convertible tops and sometimes dashboards. It uses different methods from furniture work. Seats are moulded foam over metal frames, with sewn covers fastened by hog rings or plastic clips. Materials include automotive-grade cloth, vinyl and leather designed for heat, sun and abrasion. Many furniture shops do some auto work, like seat repairs and headliners, while full classic car interiors are usually done by specialists. Modern seats can contain airbags, heaters, sensors and wiring, so care is needed. Stitch should confirm whether the shop takes auto work before booking."},{"id":"kb-specialty-002","topic":"specialty","title":"Car headliner replacement","aliases":["headliner","car headliner","sagging headliner","roof liner","headlining","ceiling fabric car","headliner repair"],"text":"A sagging headliner is one of the most common auto upholstery jobs. Most modern headliners are foam-backed fabric glued to a moulded fibreboard or fibreglass shell. Over time, heat breaks down the thin foam layer, so the fabric separates and droops. Simply re-gluing the old fabric rarely works, because the foam has turned to dust. The proper repair removes the headliner board from the vehicle, strips off the old fabric and crumbled foam, cleans the board, and glues on new foam-backed headliner fabric with high-temperature adhesive. Sunroofs, grab handles, visors, lights and trim must be removed and refitted. Older cars may have bow-supported, sewn headliners that need a different method."},{"id":"kb-specialty-003","topic":"specialty","title":"Headliner: what customers should know","aliases":["headliner FAQ","headliner time","pins in headliner","headliner sag cause","headliner glue"],"text":"Customers often try pins, staples or spray glue on a sagging headliner. These are temporary, and spray glue from a hardware store can soak through and stain or fail in summer heat. A full replacement with new foam-backed fabric and high-heat adhesive gives a lasting fix. The job usually takes part of a day to a day of shop time, depending on the vehicle, sunroof and trim, but each shop sets its own schedule. Fabric colour is matched as closely as possible to the original. Customers should mention sunroofs, overhead consoles, rear-window features or any airbag curtains, so staff can plan. Stitch should not quote a fixed time or price without staff."},{"id":"kb-specialty-004","topic":"specialty","title":"Car seat repair and covers","aliases":["car seat repair","seat cover replacement","auto seat upholstery","torn car seat","bolster repair","seat foam repair"],"text":"Common car seat jobs include repairing a torn panel, often the driver's side bolster where people slide in, replacing worn seat foam, and recovering whole seats. A single damaged panel can sometimes be replaced and sewn into the existing cover, which is cheaper than a full cover, though new material may not match faded original. Bolster foam can be rebuilt. Full seat covers are either made from patterns taken off the old cover or bought as ready-made kits for specific vehicles. Seats are removed from the vehicle, the cover is taken off by opening hog rings or clips, and the new cover is fitted. Seats with heaters, sensors or airbags need extra care."},{"id":"kb-specialty-005","topic":"specialty","title":"Seat airbags, heaters and sensors","aliases":["seat airbag","side airbag seat","seat heater","occupant sensor","seat sensor","airbag seat cover","heated seats upholstery"],"text":"Many modern vehicle seats contain side airbags, seat heaters, cooling fans and occupant sensors that tell the airbag system whether someone is seated. This affects upholstery: seats with side airbags have covers with special tear seams designed to let the airbag deploy, so replacement covers and repairs must not interfere with them. Occupant sensors and heating pads can be damaged if handled roughly. Disconnecting the battery and following safe procedures is important when removing seats. Stitch should flag any modern seat with airbags or electronics to staff, and should not promise any work that affects airbag function. Shops decide what they are equipped to do."},{"id":"kb-specialty-006","topic":"specialty","title":"Convertible tops basics","aliases":["convertible top","soft top","convertible roof","ragtop","convertible top replacement","vinyl top","canvas top"],"text":"Convertible soft tops are made from vinyl or multi-layer canvas-style fabrics over a folding metal frame, with a rear window of plastic or glass. Over time tops fade, shrink, tear or leak, and plastic rear windows cloud or crack. Replacement involves removing the old top, checking the frame, pads and weatherstripping, then fitting a new top, often a pre-made top for the specific model, and adjusting it for tension and seal. Many upholstery shops do not handle convertible tops; specialists usually do. Care includes cleaning with products made for the top material, avoiding folding it when wet or very cold, and protecting it from sun."},{"id":"kb-specialty-007","topic":"specialty","title":"Auto carpets and door panels","aliases":["car carpet","auto carpet","door panel repair","door card","trim panel","car interior panels","kick panels"],"text":"Auto carpets are usually moulded to the floor shape and replaced as kits made for the specific vehicle, or cut and bound from roll carpet. Door panels, also called door cards, are boards covered in vinyl, cloth or leather, often with padded inserts, armrests and pockets; when the covering peels or the board warps from moisture, they can be recovered or rebuilt. Classic car door panels may use specific patterns of pleats and stitching. Kick panels, rear decks and pillar trims are smaller related jobs. These jobs require removing trim carefully because plastic clips can break and some parts are hard to source."},{"id":"kb-specialty-008","topic":"specialty","title":"Classic car interiors","aliases":["classic car interior","vintage car upholstery","restoration car interior","original pattern seats","tuck and roll","pleated seats car"],"text":"Classic car interiors are a restoration specialty. Owners often want a factory-correct look, using materials, colours, grain patterns and stitching that match the original, or a custom style like tuck-and-roll pleating, a pleated, padded look popular on hot rods. Original seats may have coil springs, burlap and padding rather than moulded foam. Patterns are taken from old covers, and some restorers buy reproduction kits made for specific models. Authenticity matters for show cars and value, so documenting the original is important. Stitch should ask the year, make and model, and whether the owner wants original or custom, then pass details to staff."},{"id":"kb-specialty-009","topic":"specialty","title":"Motorcycle seats","aliases":["motorcycle seat","motorbike seat","bike seat recover","seat pan","motorcycle upholstery","gel seat"],"text":"Motorcycle seats are a moulded foam pad on a plastic or metal seat pan, covered in vinyl or leather. Common jobs are recovering a cracked or torn cover, reshaping or replacing foam for comfort, and adding gel inserts. The cover is stretched over the foam and stapled to the pan, sometimes after heating the vinyl. Because seats are exposed to sun and rain, marine or automotive-grade vinyl and UV-resistant thread are good choices. Pans can crack and may need repair. Customers often want custom stitching or a lower or higher seat profile, which changes how the foam is shaped."},{"id":"kb-specialty-010","topic":"specialty","title":"Marine upholstery overview","aliases":["marine upholstery","boat upholstery","boat seats","boat cushions","marine interiors","pontoon seats","yacht upholstery"],"text":"Marine upholstery covers boat seats, cushions, helm chairs, bolsters, cabin interiors and sometimes canvas. It must handle sun, water, humidity, salt in saltwater areas, and freezing winters in storage. Shops use marine-grade vinyl or outdoor fabrics, UV-resistant thread, stainless steel or other non-rusting staples and hardware, drain-through or closed-cell foams, and seat bases that do not rot, often marine plywood or plastic board. Seams and staple lines are planned to shed water. On Lake Ontario and Ontario cottage lakes, boat work often peaks in spring before launch, so Stitch should note timing. Many furniture shops do some marine work; complex yachts are usually specialists."},{"id":"kb-specialty-011","topic":"specialty","title":"Marine-grade vinyl","aliases":["marine vinyl","marine grade vinyl","boat vinyl","UV vinyl","mildew resistant vinyl","outdoor vinyl"],"text":"Marine-grade vinyl is a heavy PVC-coated fabric made for boats. Compared with ordinary upholstery vinyl, it usually has stronger backing, added UV inhibitors to slow fading and cracking, antimicrobial or mildew-resistant treatments, and better cold-crack resistance. It still ages: sun, heat, sunscreen and harsh cleaners shorten its life, and mildew can grow on dirt sitting on the surface. Cleaning with gentle cleaners made for marine vinyl and covering seats when not in use helps a lot. Stitch should describe marine vinyl as mildew-resistant, not mildew-proof, and refer customers to the maker's cleaning guidance."},{"id":"kb-specialty-012","topic":"specialty","title":"Stainless steel and Monel staples","aliases":["stainless staples","Monel staples","marine staples","rust proof staples","non-corrosive fasteners","stainless hardware"],"text":"Ordinary steel staples rust when wet, staining fabric and losing grip. Marine and outdoor upholstery uses stainless steel staples, or Monel staples, a nickel-copper alloy that is very corrosion-resistant and popular for marine work. Snaps, screws, zippers and other hardware should also be non-corroding: stainless steel, brass, or plastic as appropriate. Zippers for outdoor use are usually plastic coil or moulded plastic. These parts cost more than standard ones, which is one reason marine work is priced differently. Using the right fasteners prevents rust stains bleeding through light-coloured vinyl."},{"id":"kb-specialty-013","topic":"specialty","title":"Marine foam choices","aliases":["boat foam","marine foam choice","closed cell boat seat","drain-through boat cushion","flotation cushion","boat seat foam"],"text":"Boat cushions use foam suited to how wet they get. Drain-through reticulated foam lets water run out and dries quickly, so it suits open-deck cushions and sun pads, especially when covered in breathable fabric with mesh bottoms. Closed-cell foam does not absorb water and floats, so it suits bolsters, wet areas and thin seats, but it feels firm and is often layered with a softer top. Standard indoor foam soaks up water and can mildew, so it should only be used in dry cabin areas, ideally wrapped in a moisture barrier. Note that boat cushions sold as throwable flotation devices are a regulated safety item; ordinary upholstered seats are not a substitute for approved flotation."},{"id":"kb-specialty-014","topic":"specialty","title":"Marine thread (PTFE and UV-resistant)","aliases":["PTFE thread","marine thread","UV thread","outdoor thread","bonded polyester UV","lifetime thread","canvas thread"],"text":"Outdoor sun breaks down nylon thread quickly and degrades ordinary polyester over time, so seams on boat and patio work can fail while the fabric still looks fine. Marine work uses UV-resistant bonded polyester thread, which is treated to last longer outdoors, or PTFE thread, made from the same family of material as non-stick coatings, which is extremely resistant to sun, chemicals and weathering and is often expected to outlast the fabric. PTFE thread costs much more and can be trickier to sew with. Choosing the right thread is one of the most important details in outdoor and marine upholstery."},{"id":"kb-specialty-015","topic":"specialty","title":"Marine seat bases and construction","aliases":["boat seat base","marine plywood","seat board rot","polymer board","boat seat construction","drain holes"],"text":"Many boat seats and cushions are built on a base board. Ordinary plywood rots when wet, so marine work uses marine plywood sealed against moisture, or plastic and polymer boards that cannot rot. Bases often get drain holes so water does not pool under the foam. When recovering old boat seats, shops often find rotten wood bases that must be replaced, which adds to the job. Staples go into the base from below, using stainless or Monel staples. Seams on top surfaces are minimised and placed to shed water. Customers should mention soft or spongy seats, which often signal rot."},{"id":"kb-specialty-016","topic":"specialty","title":"Boat interior and cabin cushions","aliases":["cabin cushions","V-berth cushions","boat bed cushions","dinette cushions boat","cabin upholstery","berth mattress"],"text":"Cabin cushions on boats, such as V-berth beds, dinette seats and settees, are protected from rain but face humidity and condensation. Shops often use regular or high-resilience foam here for comfort, sometimes with a breathable layer or a moisture-wicking underlay beneath mattresses to reduce mildew. Covers are usually removable with zippers. Many cabin cushions have odd, angled shapes, so templates are taken from the boat, often using plastic or paper sheet. Fabrics may be marine vinyl, performance fabric or outdoor acrylic. Leaving hatches cracked for airflow and removing cushions for winter storage helps prevent mildew."},{"id":"kb-specialty-017","topic":"specialty","title":"Winterizing and storing boat and patio cushions","aliases":["winter storage cushions","storing outdoor cushions","winterize boat seats","off-season storage","cushion storage Toronto"],"text":"In Toronto and across Ontario, boat and patio cushions should come indoors or be stored dry over winter. Clean off dirt, sunscreen and food first, since mildew feeds on residue. Let cushions dry completely before storing. Store them in a dry, ventilated place off the floor, standing on edge if possible, and avoid sealed plastic bags that trap moisture. Covered boats should still be ventilated. Vinyl can crack if folded or stressed when very cold. Spring is a busy time for marine shops, so customers wanting repairs before launch should book during the off-season."},{"id":"kb-specialty-018","topic":"specialty","title":"Mildew prevention outdoors","aliases":["mildew","mould on cushions","mold boat seats","mildew prevention","mildew cleaning","black spots vinyl"],"text":"Mildew grows where moisture, warmth and organic material meet, including on dirt, pollen, sunscreen and body oils sitting on fabric or vinyl. To prevent it, keep outdoor and marine upholstery clean, let it dry fully, use drain-through foam outdoors, ventilate covered boats, and store cushions dry. Mildew-resistant materials slow growth but do not make it impossible. Once mildew stains vinyl deeply, especially from below the surface, it can be hard to remove completely. Cleaning should follow the material maker's guidance. Stitch should not recommend specific chemical mixes; staff can advise based on the material."},{"id":"kb-specialty-019","topic":"specialty","title":"Commercial and contract upholstery overview","aliases":["commercial upholstery","contract upholstery","business upholstery","hospitality upholstery","restaurant seating","office furniture reupholstery"],"text":"Commercial or contract upholstery is work for businesses: restaurants, bars, cafés, hotels, offices, healthcare, schools, churches, theatres and more. It differs from residential work in scale, durability and rules. Materials must stand heavy use and frequent cleaning, often contract-grade vinyl, performance fabrics or high-rub wovens. Clients may supply written specifications including fire requirements. Scheduling matters: businesses often need work done after hours or in phases to stay open. Shops may work on-site or remove seating in batches. Stitch should collect the business name, contact, number and type of pieces, timeline, any specs and whether on-site work is needed."},{"id":"kb-specialty-020","topic":"specialty","title":"Restaurant booths and banquettes","aliases":["restaurant booth","booth reupholstery","banquette commercial","diner booth","bar seating","booth seat replacement"],"text":"Restaurant booths take heavy daily wear, spills and cleaning. They are usually built with plywood bases, high-density foam and contract vinyl or durable performance fabric, often in channel-back or plain styles. Good booth construction uses removable seat and back panels, so they can be lifted out, recovered in the shop and reinstalled quickly, often overnight or on a closed day. Common jobs are recovering torn seats, replacing flattened foam and rebuilding loose panels. Matching existing vinyl colours can be difficult if the original is discontinued. Stitch should ask how many booths, linear length, whether seats lift out, and the restaurant's hours or closure days."},{"id":"kb-specialty-021","topic":"specialty","title":"Healthcare upholstery","aliases":["healthcare upholstery","medical seating","hospital chairs","clinic chairs","dental chair upholstery","bleach cleanable vinyl","infection control"],"text":"Healthcare seating, such as clinic chairs, exam tables, dental chairs and patient room seating, must be cleanable with disinfectants the facility uses. That usually means healthcare-grade vinyl or coated fabrics rated for those disinfectants, sometimes bleach-cleanable, plus fluid barriers and sealed seams that do not trap liquids. Some facilities specify antimicrobial materials and particular fire standards. Facilities often provide their own approved materials list. Torn exam table or chair covers can be an infection-control issue, so turnaround can be urgent. Stitch should collect the facility's material requirements, the number of pieces and access times, and pass them to staff."},{"id":"kb-specialty-022","topic":"specialty","title":"Hospitality and office seating","aliases":["hotel upholstery","hospitality furniture","office chair reupholstery","lobby seating","conference chairs","task chair recovering"],"text":"Hotels, lobbies and offices use upholstered seating heavily, but with less extreme cleaning demands than healthcare. Hotel jobs may include headboards, lounge chairs, dining chairs and banquettes, often in large quantities that need consistent fabric from one dye lot. Office jobs include task chairs, conference chairs and reception seating; many modern task chairs have shells or mesh backs where only seat pads can be recovered. Volume pricing and phased scheduling are common. Stitch should note the quantity, types, photos, whether fabric is specified, and the client's timeline, and pass these to staff for a commercial quote."},{"id":"kb-specialty-023","topic":"specialty","title":"Church pews and theatre seating","aliases":["church pews","pew cushions","theatre seats","auditorium seating","pew upholstery","kneelers"],"text":"Places of worship and theatres often have long runs of seating. Pew cushions are long, thin cushions made to the pew length, often in durable fabric or velour, sometimes with ties or non-slip backing, or fully upholstered pew seats and backs. Kneelers, also called hassocks, are padded and covered. Theatre and auditorium seats have individual seat and back pads that can be removed and recovered in batches. These jobs need careful measuring of every run, since lengths can vary, and may need fire-rated materials for public assembly spaces. Work is often scheduled around services or events."},{"id":"kb-specialty-024","topic":"specialty","title":"Commercial durability considerations","aliases":["commercial durability","contract grade","heavy duty upholstery","high traffic seating","commercial foam","commercial vinyl"],"text":"Commercial seating sees far more use than home furniture, so shops choose materials and methods accordingly. Fabric and vinyl should be contract-grade, with high abrasion ratings and cleanability suited to the setting. Foam is usually higher density or high resilience to resist flattening. Construction focuses on strong seams, solid bases, and removable panels for easy recovering later. Edge areas like front seat edges and booth tops get extra reinforcement. Even with good materials, expect commercial upholstery to need refreshing sooner than home pieces. Stitch should not promise lifespan figures, but can explain that contract materials are designed for heavier use."},{"id":"kb-specialty-025","topic":"specialty","title":"RV upholstery","aliases":["RV upholstery","camper upholstery","motorhome seating","trailer cushions","RV dinette","RV sofa","travel trailer upholstery"],"text":"RV and camper upholstery includes dinette cushions, jackknife and fold-out sofas, captain's chairs, bed mattresses and cushions for overhead bunks. Space and weight are tight, so cushions are often thinner and must fit exact shapes. Many dinettes convert to beds, so cushions must lie flat and fit together. Materials should handle temperature swings, humidity and sun, so vinyls, faux leathers, outdoor and performance fabrics are common. Factory RV furniture is often built lightly with staples and particle board, so frames may need reinforcement. Stitch should ask for the RV type, photos, cushion count and measurements."},{"id":"kb-specialty-026","topic":"specialty","title":"RV furniture: jackknife sofas and dinettes","aliases":["jackknife sofa","RV couch","dinette conversion","RV bed cushions","RV furniture replacement"],"text":"Jackknife sofas fold flat into a bed; dinettes have two benches and a table that drops to make a bed with the cushions. When reupholstering, cushions must allow folding or fit together flat with no gaps, so measurements and foam thickness must be precise. Many owners replace RV jackknife sofas with lighter residential-style furniture, but weight, seat belts, mounting and slide-out clearance must be considered, and changes affecting safety equipment should be checked. Removable, zippered covers are practical. Foam choice balances comfort with storage; some owners use firmer foam for dinette cushions that double as beds."},{"id":"kb-specialty-027","topic":"specialty","title":"Antique restoration ethics","aliases":["antique ethics","restoration ethics","preserving antiques","conservation principles","original materials","should I keep original"],"text":"When working on antiques, shops balance use, appearance and preservation. Key principles: assess the piece's age and value before stripping; document everything with photos and notes; keep original materials that are still sound, such as stuffing, springs, webbing or stitched edges; make new work reversible where possible; avoid unnecessary damage to the frame from new tacks or staples; and keep removed original materials, labels or fabric fragments for the owner. For valuable pieces, an appraiser or conservator should be consulted first, since stripping original upholstery can reduce value. For ordinary older family furniture, practical modern methods are fine, guided by the owner's wishes."},{"id":"kb-specialty-028","topic":"specialty","title":"Keeping original stuffing and horsehair","aliases":["reuse horsehair","keep original stuffing","wash horsehair","re-tease hair","reuse antique stuffing","original filling"],"text":"Curled horsehair and other quality traditional stuffings can often be reused. The hair is removed, cleaned of dust and debris, and teased, or carded, to restore its springiness before being worked back into the piece, sometimes topped up with new hair. This preserves original material and often gives a better result than foam on period pieces. Not all stuffing is worth keeping: moss, straw or excelsior that has turned to dust, or anything with mould or pests, is usually replaced. Reusing stuffing takes labour, so it can cost as much as new materials. Stitch should explain that the shop will assess what can be kept."},{"id":"kb-specialty-029","topic":"specialty","title":"Traditional vs modern methods on antiques","aliases":["traditional methods antiques","foam on antiques","modern materials antique","period correct upholstery","tack vs staple antique"],"text":"Owners of antiques choose between traditional methods, such as webbing, hand-tied springs, hair stuffing, stitched edges and tacks, and modern methods with foam and staples. Traditional work suits valuable or historically important pieces and gives a period-correct shape and feel, but takes more skilled labour. Modern materials can be faster, cheaper and more comfortable for daily use, and are often fine on ordinary older furniture. A middle path keeps the original spring system and stitched edges while adding a modern padding layer. Using tacks instead of staples, or minimising new fixings, helps protect fragile antique frames. The shop should explain options so the owner can decide."},{"id":"kb-specialty-030","topic":"specialty","title":"Non-invasive and reversible upholstery","aliases":["non-invasive upholstery","reversible upholstery","museum method","staple-free upholstery","conservation methods"],"text":"Non-invasive upholstery, developed by museum conservators, avoids adding new holes to historic frames. Techniques include building the upholstery on a separate removable frame or board that sits in or on the original frame, using carved foam forms, or fastening covers with stitching or Velcro-type fasteners to existing structures. Reversible means the work can later be removed without damaging the original. These methods are mainly for museum or very valuable pieces and take specialist skills. Most shops will not offer full conservation-grade work, and Stitch should refer customers with museum-quality pieces to a conservator if the shop does not."},{"id":"kb-specialty-031","topic":"specialty","title":"Identifying old materials during restoration","aliases":["identifying old upholstery","old materials clues","dating furniture upholstery","age of upholstery","history of a piece"],"text":"Stripping an old piece often reveals its history. Multiple layers of fabric show past re-coverings; tack versus staple holes hint at when work was done; jute or cotton webbing, hand-tied coils, hair, moss, excelsior, cotton felt or early foam give clues to era and quality. Labels, stamps, newspaper used as padding, or pencil notes from past upholsterers sometimes turn up. Documenting these finds can interest the owner and add to the piece's story. Upholsterers should be cautious about dating claims and avoid giving formal valuations; an appraiser should handle value questions."},{"id":"kb-specialty-032","topic":"specialty","title":"Original show covers and fragments","aliases":["original fabric antique","keep old fabric","fabric fragments","historic textiles","period fabric"],"text":"On older or valuable pieces, the original show cover, or fragments of it found under later layers, can be historically interesting. Shops can photograph, label and return fragments to the owner, and sometimes a hidden area shows the original colour before fading. If the owner wants a period-correct look, the fragment can guide fabric choice, and reproduction fabrics of historic patterns are available from specialist mills. Original covers in good condition on museum-quality pieces are sometimes conserved rather than replaced. Stitch can mention to owners that their shop can save old fabric pieces if they want them."},{"id":"kb-styles-001","topic":"styles","title":"Chesterfield sofa","aliases":["chesterfield","chesterfield sofa","tufted leather sofa","button back sofa","Chesterfield couch"],"text":"A classic chesterfield is a sofa with a deep-buttoned, diamond-tufted back and arms, where the rolled arms are the same height as the back, forming one continuous curved line. It usually has a tight back, and either a tight seat or loose seat cushions, and is often covered in leather, though velvet is also popular. Upholstering a chesterfield is demanding: the tufting runs around the back and arms in continuous diamond rows with folded pleats, the rolled arm fronts are pleated or panelled, and nailheads are common. It takes a lot of fabric or leather and labour. Note that in Canada, chesterfield is also a general word for any sofa, so confirm which the customer means."},{"id":"kb-styles-002","topic":"styles","title":"Lawson sofa","aliases":["Lawson","Lawson sofa","Lawson arm","Lawson style","loose back sofa"],"text":"A Lawson sofa has a loose cushion back, loose seat cushions, and arms set noticeably lower than the back. Arms may be rolled, square or flared, and the back cushions are usually separate pillows. It is a relaxed, versatile shape that suits traditional and casual rooms. A Lawson is fairly straightforward to upholster compared with tufted or exposed-wood styles, but the many cushions add sewing time and fill cost. The style is named, traditionally, after Thomas W. Lawson. Customers who want a comfortable, easy-to-refresh sofa often have a Lawson-style piece, and cushion refills or new foam can make a big difference without a full reupholstery."},{"id":"kb-styles-003","topic":"styles","title":"Tuxedo sofa","aliases":["tuxedo","tuxedo sofa","tuxedo arm","tuxedo chair","high arm sofa","box sofa"],"text":"A tuxedo sofa has arms the same height as the back, forming a clean, boxy outline. The arms are usually straight or slightly flared, and the back is often tight, sometimes tufted, with a tight seat or loose cushions. The name is traditionally linked to Tuxedo Park, New York, in the late nineteenth century, though that origin is traditionally said. Tuxedo sofas look tailored and formal or very modern depending on fabric and legs. Upholstering one needs crisp, straight lines and careful attention to the corners where arms meet the back. Because inside arms and back form a continuous surface, matching patterns across them takes planning."},{"id":"kb-styles-004","topic":"styles","title":"Camelback sofa","aliases":["camelback","camel back","camelback sofa","humpback sofa","serpentine back","Chippendale sofa"],"text":"A camelback sofa has a back with one or two raised humps, usually rising highest in the centre and sloping down toward the arms. It is an eighteenth-century English form often associated with Chippendale-period designs. Camelbacks typically have rolled arms, exposed carved or turned legs, and a tight back, often with a single long bench cushion or a tight seat. Upholstering the curved back needs careful shaping of padding and fabric so the curve is smooth, and patterned fabric must be centred on the hump. Welt along the top curve shows any wobbles, so this edge is done with care. Camelbacks often reach shops as family heirlooms."},{"id":"kb-styles-005","topic":"styles","title":"English roll arm sofa","aliases":["English roll arm","English arm sofa","English sofa","Howard sofa","low roll arm","country house sofa"],"text":"An English roll arm sofa has low, tightly rolled arms set back from the front of the seat, a tight back that is often fairly high, and deep, loose seat cushions, usually T-shaped. Legs are often turned and may have casters. Traditionally such sofas were deeply sprung and upholstered with hair, giving a soft, comfortable, relaxed country-house look. The small rolled arm is upholstered with a pleated or gathered front, rather than a flat facing. The style is a staple of traditional and transitional rooms. When reupholstering, the low tight arms and back need even padding so they do not look lumpy."},{"id":"kb-styles-006","topic":"styles","title":"Bridgewater sofa","aliases":["Bridgewater","Bridgewater sofa","Bridgewater arm","set-back roll arm","low arm sofa"],"text":"A Bridgewater sofa has low, rolled arms set back from the front edge of the seat, loose seat cushions that extend past the arms, and usually loose back pillows. It often has a skirt, though versions with legs exist. The look is relaxed and comfortable, close to an English roll arm but generally softer and more casual. Because the seat extends past the arms, cushions are typically T-shaped. Bridgewater sofas are very popular in family rooms. They are moderately complex to upholster, with the arm rolls and skirt adding work. When reupholstering, plump, well-filled back pillows are key to the look."},{"id":"kb-styles-007","topic":"styles","title":"Cabriole sofa","aliases":["cabriole sofa","cabriole","cabriole leg","exposed wood sofa","French sofa","show wood sofa"],"text":"A cabriole sofa has an exposed, often carved, wooden frame, a continuous curved back, and cabriole legs, which curve outward at the knee and inward toward a shaped foot. Some definitions use cabriole for the leg shape rather than the whole sofa. The upholstery fills the frame and is finished against the show wood with gimp, double welt or nailheads. It has a tight back and often a tight seat. Upholstering a cabriole sofa means working neatly up to the wood without scratching the finish, which takes care. Refinishing the wood frame, if needed, is usually quoted separately by the shop or a refinisher."},{"id":"kb-styles-008","topic":"styles","title":"Chaise longue","aliases":["chaise","chaise longue","chaise lounge","daybed chaise","fainting couch","recamier","meridienne","lounge chair"],"text":"A chaise longue, French for long chair, is an upholstered seat long enough to support the legs, with a back at one end. North Americans often say chaise lounge. Related forms include the Recamier, with raised ends of different heights, the meridienne, with an asymmetric back, and the Victorian fainting couch, which is a name traditionally given to day sofas with a raised head. Modern chaises may be part of a sectional. Upholstery depends on the frame: tight seats, tufted or channelled backs, and bolsters are common. Chaises need long single pieces of fabric for the seat, so railroaded fabric or careful seam placement may be needed."},{"id":"kb-styles-009","topic":"styles","title":"Sectional sofa","aliases":["sectional","sectional sofa","L-shaped sofa","U-shaped sofa","modular sofa","corner sofa","chaise sectional","LAF","RAF"],"text":"A sectional is a sofa made of two or more pieces that join, usually into an L or U shape, often with a corner unit, an armless middle piece, or a chaise end. Pieces are described as left-arm-facing or right-arm-facing, often shortened to LAF and RAF, meaning the arm is on that side when you face the piece. Sectionals are reupholstered piece by piece, but fabric must match across every section, so it is all ordered together from one dye lot. They use a lot of fabric and many cushions. Connectors that hold the sections together should be checked during the work. Measuring each section separately is important for an accurate quote."},{"id":"kb-styles-010","topic":"styles","title":"Tufted sofa (general)","aliases":["tufted sofa","tufted chair","tufted furniture","button tufted sofa","diamond tufted sofa"],"text":"Tufted is a general description for furniture whose upholstery is pulled down at regular points, usually with buttons, to create a pattern. A tufted piece can be a chesterfield, a tuxedo, a modern sofa, a headboard or an ottoman. The tufting type matters a lot for the job: deep diamond tufting with pleats is the most labour-intensive; biscuit tufting forms a square grid; blind tufting has no visible buttons; and channel tufting forms vertical tubes. When a customer says they have a tufted sofa, the shop should ask for photos to see the tufting style, count roughly how many buttons there are, and check whether the tufting is on the back only or on the arms and seat too."},{"id":"kb-styles-011","topic":"styles","title":"Mid-century modern sofa","aliases":["mid-century sofa","mid century modern sofa","MCM sofa","Danish sofa","1950s sofa","retro sofa"],"text":"Mid-century modern sofas, inspired by design of the 1950s and 1960s, have clean lines, low profiles, slim track or slanted arms, and tapered wooden legs. Backs are often tight or have simple loose cushions, and seats may be a single long bench cushion. Original pieces often used elastic webbing or sinuous springs, foam rubber and wool or tweed fabrics. Reupholstering them usually means replacing crumbled foam and webbing, and keeping lines crisp, since the style depends on precise edges and thin welt or no welt. Collectors may want original details preserved on designer pieces. Tweeds, bouclés, leathers and solid wovens suit the style well."},{"id":"kb-styles-012","topic":"styles","title":"Wingback chair","aliases":["wingback","wing back","wing chair","wingback chair","fireside chair","grandfather chair","Queen Anne wing chair"],"text":"A wingback chair has a tall back with side panels, the wings, projecting forward from the top of the back down to the arms. It often has rolled arms, a loose seat cushion or tight seat, and exposed legs, sometimes cabriole or straight. Wingbacks are one of the most commonly reupholstered chairs. They are more work than they look: the wings, arm rolls and inside surfaces all need shaping and careful patterning, the outside wings and back need clean closing, and welt follows many curves. They need more fabric than a simple armchair. Wingback headboards borrow the same shape for beds."},{"id":"kb-styles-013","topic":"styles","title":"Club chair","aliases":["club chair","club armchair","leather club chair","French club chair","fauteuil club","lounge armchair"],"text":"A club chair is a low, deep, comfortable armchair with thick, often rounded arms and a fairly low back. The classic version is leather, associated with gentlemen's clubs and with 1920s French designs. Modern club chairs come in many fabrics. Many have a loose seat cushion, sometimes a tight back, and short legs or a skirt. Old leather club chairs often have springs and hair stuffing under the leather, and reupholstering in leather requires working with hides rather than yardage. Rounded arms and backs need careful leather piecing and pleating. Club chairs are a popular reupholstery item because the frames are often sturdy and worth keeping."},{"id":"kb-styles-014","topic":"styles","title":"Slipper chair","aliases":["slipper chair","armless chair","bedroom chair","boudoir chair","low side chair","dressing chair"],"text":"A slipper chair is an armless upholstered chair with a low seat and usually a fairly tall back. It is traditionally said to have been designed for bedrooms, low enough to sit while putting on shoes or stockings. Slipper chairs suit small spaces and are often used in pairs. They may have a skirt, often dressmaker style, or exposed legs, and the back can be tight, tufted or channelled. Because they have no arms, they are relatively quick and economical to reupholster, and they need less fabric than an armchair, making them a good choice for showing off a bold or expensive fabric."},{"id":"kb-styles-015","topic":"styles","title":"Barrel chair (tub chair)","aliases":["barrel chair","barrel back","tub chair","barrel back chair","round back chair","swivel barrel chair"],"text":"A barrel chair has a rounded back that curves continuously into the arms, like a section of a barrel. The British often call similar chairs tub chairs. The back may be smooth, tufted or channelled; channelling suits the curve because the vertical channels can taper. Many barrel chairs swivel. Upholstering a barrel chair is tricky because fabric has to wrap a continuous curve: the inside back may need tapered seams or channels, and the outside back is often one large curved panel finished with curved tack strip or hand stitching. Patterns and stripes need careful placement around the curve."},{"id":"kb-styles-016","topic":"styles","title":"Bergère","aliases":["bergere","bergère","bergere chair","French armchair closed","enclosed arm chair","Louis bergere"],"text":"A bergère is a French armchair with upholstered, enclosed sides beneath the arms, an exposed wooden frame, and usually a loose seat cushion. The form developed in eighteenth-century France. Backs may be curved or flat, often with carved show wood around the edges. Upholstering a bergère means working the enclosed sides, back and seat neatly up to the wood, with gimp, double welt or nails to finish. The loose cushion is often down-filled in traditional versions. Antique bergères may have original stitched edges and horsehair worth preserving. They are more work than a fauteuil because of the extra upholstered side panels."},{"id":"kb-styles-017","topic":"styles","title":"Fauteuil","aliases":["fauteuil","French open arm chair","Louis chair","open arm chair","French armchair open","Louis XV chair","Louis XVI chair"],"text":"A fauteuil is a French armchair with open arms, meaning there is a gap between the arm and the seat, and an exposed wooden frame. The seat and back are upholstered, and the arms often have small padded arm pads, called manchettes. Louis XV-style fauteuils have curved, carved frames and cabriole legs; Louis XVI-style ones are straighter with fluted legs and oval or square backs. Many reproductions exist. Fauteuils are popular reupholstery jobs: they need relatively little fabric, but the finish against show wood with gimp or nails must be neat. Customers often refinish or paint the frames at the same time."},{"id":"kb-styles-018","topic":"styles","title":"Parsons chair","aliases":["Parsons chair","Parsons dining chair","fully upholstered dining chair","upholstered side chair","skirted dining chair"],"text":"A Parsons chair is a simple, fully upholstered armless dining chair with straight lines, a straight or slightly angled back, and straight legs that are often left exposed below the seat. The name links it to the Parsons School of Design's Paris branch, the same source as the Parsons table, from around the 1930s. Parsons chairs may be fully covered down to the legs, wear a slipcover, or have a skirt. Because they are fully covered, recovering them means doing the back and the outside back, not just the seat, so they need more fabric and time than slip-seat dining chairs. Durable, cleanable fabric suits dining use."},{"id":"kb-styles-019","topic":"styles","title":"Klismos chair","aliases":["klismos","klismos chair","Greek chair","Regency chair","saber leg chair","sabre leg"],"text":"The klismos is an ancient Greek chair form, known from vase paintings and sculpture, with a curved concave backrest and splayed, curved legs often called saber or sabre legs. It was revived in Europe and America around 1800 in the Neoclassical and Regency periods, and modern designers have reinterpreted it ever since. Upholstery on a klismos is usually limited to a seat, either a slip seat or a pad seat, and sometimes a padded back panel. Recovering it is fairly simple, but the elegant frame must be protected. It is a good piece for a statement fabric, since only a small amount is needed."},{"id":"kb-styles-020","topic":"styles","title":"Knole sofa","aliases":["Knole sofa","Knole settee","drop arm sofa","tied arm sofa","Knole"],"text":"A Knole sofa has a high back and side arms of equal height, with the arms hinged so they can drop down and held up by decorative cords or tassels tied to finials on the back posts. It is named after Knole, a historic house in Kent, England, where an early seventeenth-century example survives. Reproductions are made with fixed arms too. Knole sofas are usually fully upholstered with a tight back, thick loose seat cushions and plenty of trim. They are dramatic and formal, and use a large amount of fabric because both sides of the arms and back are upholstered. Trims and cords add to the materials list."},{"id":"kb-styles-021","topic":"styles","title":"Settee and loveseat","aliases":["settee","loveseat","love seat","two-seater","small sofa","courting chair","apartment sofa"],"text":"A loveseat is a small sofa for two people. A settee is an older word for a small upholstered seat for two or three, often with exposed wood, and in British English it can also mean a sofa in general. Apartment-size sofas fall between a loveseat and a standard sofa. Upholstering a loveseat is similar to a sofa but needs less fabric and fewer cushions, so quotes are usually lower. Settees with show wood need gimp or nails at the edges. Loveseats are a common part of matching sets; when reupholstering a set, ordering all the fabric at once avoids dye-lot differences."},{"id":"kb-styles-022","topic":"styles","title":"Daybed","aliases":["daybed","day bed","divan","studio couch","trundle daybed","upholstered daybed","backless sofa"],"text":"A daybed is a bed-sized seat that works as a sofa by day and a bed by night, often with a back and sides like a sofa, or with no back at all. Divan is an older word for a backless low couch, and in British English also means a bed base. Upholstered daybeds may have a tufted or channelled frame, a fitted mattress cover, bolsters and scatter cushions. Custom daybed work often includes a fitted mattress cover in box or knife-edge style, bolsters and back cushions. Measurements must match the mattress exactly. Durable, cleanable fabrics work well, since daybeds get both sitting and sleeping use."},{"id":"kb-styles-023","topic":"styles","title":"Recliner","aliases":["recliner","reclining chair","power recliner","lift chair","rocker recliner","wall hugger","motion furniture"],"text":"A recliner is a chair, or part of a sofa, with a mechanism that lets the back tilt and a footrest rise. Some are manual, some powered, and some lift to help the sitter stand. Reupholstering recliners is possible but more complicated than a regular chair: the mechanism has to be removed or worked around, covers are often sewn as fitted sections that attach with clips or strips, and fabric must allow for movement. Many mass-market recliners are built in ways that make reupholstery costly compared with replacement. Shops often recommend inspecting the mechanism and frame first, and checking that power components are working before investing in new fabric."},{"id":"kb-styles-024","topic":"styles","title":"Sleeper sofa (sofa bed)","aliases":["sleeper sofa","sofa bed","pull-out sofa","hide-a-bed","sleeper","convertible sofa","futon sofa"],"text":"A sleeper sofa hides a folding metal bed mechanism and thin mattress under its seat cushions. Reupholstering a sleeper is similar to a regular sofa, but the deck is not sprung in the usual way, since the mechanism takes up the space. The arms, back and outside panels are done as normal. Shops often recommend checking the mechanism and mattress at the same time: a worn mechanism or poor mattress may be worth replacing while the sofa is apart. Sleepers are heavy, which can affect pick-up and delivery. Cushions sit on the folded bed rather than a deck, so firmer seat foam is often chosen to stop the sitter feeling the bar."},{"id":"kb-styles-025","topic":"styles","title":"Swivel and glider chairs","aliases":["swivel chair","glider","swivel glider","nursery glider","rocking chair upholstered","swivel rocker"],"text":"Swivel chairs turn on a base mechanism; gliders move back and forth on a track; swivel gliders do both, and are common nursery chairs. Reupholstering them follows the same steps as a regular armchair, but the mechanism has to be detached first and the fabric finished cleanly around the base. Shops also check the mechanism for wear, squeaks or loose bolts. Barrel and club styles are often made as swivel chairs. Because these chairs move, fabric at the bottom edges and skirt sees extra rubbing, so durable fabric and proper clearance are important. Nursery gliders benefit from cleanable fabrics."},{"id":"kb-styles-026","topic":"styles","title":"Track arm sofa","aliases":["track arm","track arm sofa","square arm sofa","modern sofa","straight arm sofa","box arm"],"text":"A track arm sofa has straight, squared arms, usually with flat tops and vertical sides, giving a clean modern look. Track arms often have welt along their edges or are made without welt for a minimalist style. These sofas typically have loose seat and back cushions and exposed legs. They are among the simpler sofas to upholster, because the arms have no rolls, pleats or facings, so labour is usually less than a roll arm of similar size. However, track arms show any lumps in padding and any wavy welt clearly, so crisp, even padding and straight seams matter."},{"id":"kb-styles-027","topic":"styles","title":"Modern sofa styles (low-profile, slope arm, cloud)","aliases":["modern sofa","contemporary sofa","low profile sofa","slope arm","cloud sofa","deep seat sofa","modular cloud"],"text":"Contemporary sofas include many styles. Low-profile sofas sit close to the floor with slim arms. Slope-arm sofas have arms that angle downward toward the front. Deep-seat or cloud-style sofas have very deep, oversized cushions and loose, plump backs with large amounts of down or fibre fill. Modular sofas join cube-like pieces in any arrangement. Many modern pieces are made with mass-production methods, such as stapled plywood or engineered frames and fitted covers. Before quoting, shops should check whether a modern sofa's frame and construction are sound enough to be worth reupholstering, since some are not built for it."},{"id":"kb-styles-028","topic":"styles","title":"Tufted headboard","aliases":["tufted headboard","button headboard","diamond tufted headboard","tufted bed","buttoned headboard"],"text":"A tufted headboard is a padded panel with buttons pulled into it in a diamond or biscuit pattern. It is one of the most common custom upholstery jobs. A plywood or MDF board is cut to shape and drilled at each button location, covered in foam and batting, and the fabric is pulled down through the holes with twine or with buttons. The edges are wrapped and stapled behind or finished with welt or nailheads. Headboards can be wall-mounted or attached to a bed frame with legs or brackets. Velvet and linen are popular choices. Width is set by the mattress size, and height by the customer's preference."},{"id":"kb-styles-029","topic":"styles","title":"Channel headboard","aliases":["channel headboard","channelled headboard","channel tufted headboard","vertical channel headboard","fluted headboard"],"text":"A channel headboard has padded vertical channels, like wide pleats or pipes, running from top to bottom. It is a popular modern and glam look. Channels can be built by sewing the cover in tubes and stuffing each one, or by attaching foam strips to a board and pressing fabric into the gaps between them. Channel widths are planned so they divide the headboard evenly, with full channels at each end. Velvet shows channels beautifully but highlights uneven work. Curved channel headboards take extra labour. As with other headboards, the board size depends on the mattress width and the desired height."},{"id":"kb-styles-030","topic":"styles","title":"Wingback and shaped headboards","aliases":["wingback headboard","shaped headboard","arched headboard","camelback headboard","scalloped headboard","winged headboard"],"text":"Shaped headboards have a silhouette other than a rectangle: arched, camelback, scalloped, or with projecting side wings like a wingback chair. A wingback headboard wraps slightly around the pillows and adds a cosy, hotel-like look. The base board is cut to the shape and, for wings, joined at angles. Curves are covered with fabric that must be pleated or eased neatly, and welt following the outline needs bias-cut strips. Shaped headboards often combine with tufting, channelling or nailheads. They take more time and fabric than a plain panel. When quoting, staff should confirm the bed size, total height, and how it will mount."},{"id":"kb-styles-031","topic":"styles","title":"Panel and nailhead headboards","aliases":["panel headboard","upholstered headboard","plain headboard","nailhead headboard","border headboard","slipcovered headboard"],"text":"The simplest upholstered headboard is a flat panel: a board padded with foam and batting and covered in fabric, finished with welt or nailheads, or with a border inset. A nailhead headboard adds a row of decorative nails around the edge or as a border inset a few inches from the edge. A slipcovered headboard has a sewn cover that slides over a padded board, so the fabric can be removed for cleaning or swapped. Panel headboards are economical and good for showing off a patterned fabric. Mounting options include legs bolted to the bed frame or a French cleat on the wall."},{"id":"kb-styles-032","topic":"styles","title":"Upholstered beds and bed frames","aliases":["upholstered bed","upholstered bed frame","bed surround","footboard","side rails upholstered","platform bed upholstered"],"text":"Some customers want an entire bed upholstered, including headboard, footboard and side rails. Upholstered side rails are wrapped boards that attach to the headboard and footboard. Platform beds may have upholstered surrounds. Because these are large, they use substantial yardage and are often built to order to suit a specific mattress. Durable, cleanable fabrics work best near the floor where feet, vacuum cleaners and pets rub. When quoting, staff should confirm mattress size and height, how the frame will be assembled and moved through doorways and stairs, and whether the customer wants to keep any existing hardware."},{"id":"kb-styles-033","topic":"styles","title":"Dining chairs (upholstered)","aliases":["dining chairs","upholstered dining chair","dining room chairs","kitchen chairs","side chairs","host chairs","captain chairs"],"text":"Dining chairs range from simple wooden chairs with a slip seat to fully upholstered Parsons chairs and host or captain chairs with arms. Slip seats are the quickest and least expensive to recover. Stuff-over seats take longer. Chairs with upholstered backs need the back done inside and out. Fully upholstered chairs need the most fabric. Because dining chairs see spills, cleanable or performance fabric is a popular choice. Shops usually quote dining chairs per chair, and sets are common, so fabric for the whole set should be ordered at once. Loose joints are common in old dining chairs, and regluing is often done during reupholstery."},{"id":"kb-styles-034","topic":"styles","title":"Ottomans and benches (styles)","aliases":["ottoman styles","bench","upholstered bench","storage bench","end of bed bench","vanity stool","piano bench","cocktail ottoman"],"text":"Upholstered ottomans and benches come in many forms: square or round ottomans, large cocktail ottomans, storage ottomans with lift-up lids, end-of-bed benches, vanity stools, and padded piano bench tops. They can have tight tops, tufted tops, loose cushions, skirts or exposed legs. Benches are often quick jobs that need a small amount of fabric, and they are a good way to use a remnant or match a headboard. Storage lids need hinges and lid stays checked. Tufted tops require planning button spacing. Customers often ask for a matching ottoman when reupholstering a chair; ordering the fabric at the same time keeps the dye lot consistent."},{"id":"kb-styles-035","topic":"styles","title":"Victorian ladies' and gentlemen's chairs","aliases":["Victorian chair","ladies chair","gentleman's chair","button back chair","spoon back","balloon back chair"],"text":"Victorian parlour sets often included a gentleman's chair, a larger armchair, and a ladies' chair, smaller with lower or no arms, sometimes made to suit the full skirts of the period. Button-back chairs had deep-tufted, often spoon-shaped backs, and balloon-back chairs had a rounded, open wooden back. These pieces usually have carved show wood, coil springs, hair stuffing and stitched edges. Reupholstering them faithfully takes traditional skills, including deep buttoning and hand-stitched edges. For valuable or sentimental antiques, shops can discuss conserving original materials. Customers often choose velvet, damask or brocade to suit the period look."},{"id":"kb-styles-036","topic":"styles","title":"Morris and Mission chairs","aliases":["Morris chair","Mission chair","Arts and Crafts chair","craftsman chair","loose cushion wood chair","reclining Morris chair"],"text":"Morris chairs and Mission or Arts and Crafts chairs have solid wooden frames with exposed slats or panels and loose seat and back cushions. Morris chairs recline with an adjustable back bar. The upholstery work is almost entirely cushions: making new foam or spring cushions, covering them, and sometimes replacing the platform underneath, which may be a slatted base, springs or a webbed frame. Leather, faux leather and sturdy wovens suit the style. Because the cushions are the whole comfort of the chair, choosing the right foam firmness and thickness matters. These jobs usually need modest yardage."},{"id":"kb-styles-037","topic":"styles","title":"Wing chair vs club chair vs bergère","aliases":["chair comparison","which chair style","identify chair style","armchair types","armchair style differences"],"text":"Customers often describe armchairs loosely, so it helps to know the differences. A wing chair has a tall back with wings projecting forward. A club chair is low and deep with thick arms and a lower back, often leather. A bergère is a French chair with exposed wood and enclosed upholstered sides under the arms. A fauteuil has exposed wood and open arms. A slipper chair has no arms and a low seat. A barrel chair has a back that curves continuously into the arms. Asking for a photo is the fastest way to identify a chair, since names vary between families, regions and shops."},{"id":"kb-styles-038","topic":"styles","title":"Egg, womb and shell chairs","aliases":["egg chair","womb chair","shell chair","moulded chair","fibreglass chair","designer lounge chair","modern lounge chair"],"text":"Egg, womb and shell chairs are mid-century and modern designs where the seat and back form one moulded shell of fibreglass, plastic or plywood, covered in foam and fabric or leather, often with loose cushions. Reupholstering them differs from traditional work: the old foam and cover are removed from the shell, new foam is glued and shaped to it, and the cover is fitted, sometimes sewn as one piece and stretched over. Some famous designs have licensed reproductions and many unlicensed copies. Collectors may want original-specification materials. Shops should confirm the shell is sound, without cracks, before quoting."},{"id":"kb-styles-039","topic":"styles","title":"Banquette and booth styles","aliases":["booth styles","channel back booth","tufted booth","restaurant booth styles","built-in banquette styles","curved booth"],"text":"Banquettes and booths come in several common styles. Plain backs are smooth and simple. Channel backs have vertical padded channels and are very popular in restaurants. Tufted backs use buttons. Booths may be single-sided against a wall or double-sided back to back, straight, L-shaped, U-shaped or curved. Seats are usually tight, often built as removable panels for cleaning and replacement. Home banquettes may use loose seat cushions on a wooden bench. Curved booths need tapered channels and careful patterning. Commercial booths are usually covered in contract-grade vinyl or durable fabric for easy cleaning."},{"id":"kb-styles-040","topic":"styles","title":"Window seats and built-ins","aliases":["window seat","window seat cushion","bay window cushion","built-in bench cushion","mudroom bench","reading nook cushion"],"text":"Window seats and built-in benches usually need a custom cushion rather than full upholstery. Most are box cushions made from foam wrapped in polyester, cut to the exact shape of the seat, which in bay windows is often a trapezoid or an irregular shape. A paper or cardboard template is the most reliable way to capture odd angles. Optional extras include back pillows, bolsters, and a hinged lid if the bench has storage. Foam firmness should be firm enough for sitting. Sunny windows fade many fabrics, so solution-dyed or fade-resistant fabrics are worth suggesting for south- and west-facing windows."},{"id":"kb-styles-041","topic":"styles","title":"Outdoor furniture cushions","aliases":["outdoor cushions","patio cushions","deep seating cushions","outdoor furniture upholstery","deck furniture cushions","garden cushions"],"text":"Outdoor cushions are made differently from indoor ones. They use outdoor fabrics such as solution-dyed acrylic or olefin, UV-resistant polyester or PTFE thread, rust-proof zippers and hardware, and quick-drying foam that lets water run through, often wrapped in polyester or a mesh-backed fabric. Deep seating cushions are thick seat and back cushions for outdoor sofas and chairs; dining cushions are thinner pads, often knife-edge or thin box style with ties. In Toronto, customers usually store cushions indoors over winter, which helps them last much longer. Measuring each cushion and noting ties or hook-and-loop fasteners is important for quotes."},{"id":"kb-styles-042","topic":"styles","title":"Antique vs reproduction pieces","aliases":["antique or reproduction","is it antique","identify antique","reproduction furniture","period furniture","vintage vs antique"],"text":"Customers often wonder if their piece is a valuable antique. Signs of older construction include hand-cut joinery, irregular tool marks, many old tack holes, traditional stuffing like hair or moss, hand-tied springs and old cotton webbing or jute. Reproductions may use the same style but with machine joinery, plywood, foam and staples. Antique generally means around one hundred years old or more, and vintage is a looser term for older but more recent pieces. Upholstery shops are not appraisers; if a piece may be valuable, the customer should consult a qualified appraiser before stripping it, since removing original upholstery can affect value."},{"id":"kb-techniques-001","topic":"techniques","title":"Assessing a piece before reupholstery","aliases":["assessment","inspection","evaluating furniture","is it worth reupholstering","pre-quote inspection","condition check"],"text":"Before quoting, an upholsterer assesses the whole piece, not just the fabric. They check the frame for wobble, loose joints, cracks and broken rails, often by rocking it and looking under the dust cover. They press the seat to judge springs, listening for squeaks and feeling for broken or sagging springs. They check cushions for flattened foam or broken-down fill, look at padding on arms and backs, and note show wood that may need refinishing. They also look for signs of pests, mould, pet odour or smoke. A full assessment avoids surprises later. From photos alone, a shop can give only a rough estimate, and the final price may change after the piece is opened."},{"id":"kb-techniques-002","topic":"techniques","title":"What Stitch should ask for an estimate","aliases":["estimate intake","quote questions","what info for quote","photos for estimate","intake checklist","lead intake"],"text":"To help the shop give a useful estimate, Stitch should collect: clear photos of the piece from the front, side and back, plus close-ups of any damage; rough measurements of width, depth and height; the number of loose seat and back cushions; whether there is tufting, a skirt, nailheads or exposed wood; whether the customer wants the same look or changes; and whether they will supply their own fabric or choose from the shop. Also ask about any problems like sagging, wobbling or odours, and the customer's timeline and location for pick-up or delivery. Stitch should explain that the estimate is preliminary until staff inspect the piece in person."},{"id":"kb-techniques-003","topic":"techniques","title":"Stripping old upholstery","aliases":["stripping","strip down","tear down","removing old fabric","reverse order","teardown"],"text":"Stripping means removing the old upholstery, usually in reverse order of how it went on. The dust cover comes off first, then the outside back, outside arms, facings and skirt, followed by the inside back, inside arms and seat. Each staple or tack is removed carefully to avoid splitting the frame. Old fabric pieces are kept and labelled because they serve as patterns and show how things were attached. Padding and springs are removed or kept depending on their condition. Stripping can reveal hidden damage, so it is the point where a quote may need updating. It is dusty work that should be done with a dust mask and good ventilation."},{"id":"kb-techniques-004","topic":"techniques","title":"Documenting before and during stripping","aliases":["documenting","photos during strip","labelling pieces","pattern pieces","teardown notes","record keeping"],"text":"Good shops document a piece as they strip it. Photos are taken before work begins and at each stage, especially of how pleats, corners, welt and trims were finished, and the order of layers. Old fabric panels are labelled with their position, such as inside arm left or outside back, and marked with the top direction. Notes record things like spring layout, the number and spacing of buttons, and where stretchers were used. This helps the new upholstery match the original look and helps if staff change partway through the job. For antiques, documentation also preserves a record of original materials and methods, which can matter for value."},{"id":"kb-techniques-005","topic":"techniques","title":"Safety: pests, mould and hazardous materials","aliases":["bed bugs","pests","mould","mold","moths","safety stripping","hazardous materials","old furniture hazards"],"text":"Old furniture can carry hazards. Bed bugs, carpet beetles and moths can hide in seams, stuffing and frames, so shops inspect pieces on arrival, and many have a policy about refusing or treating infested items. Mould can grow in pieces stored in damp basements or garages. Old stuffing and foam produce fine dust, and crumbling latex foam is messy. Some older materials and finishes may contain substances that are best handled with care. Shops use dust masks, gloves and ventilation, and keep suspect pieces away from other customers' furniture. Stitch should ask customers whether a piece has been stored in a damp place or has had any pest issues, and pass that to staff."},{"id":"kb-techniques-006","topic":"techniques","title":"Frame repair: regluing loose joints","aliases":["regluing","loose joints","wobbly chair repair","frame repair glue","hide glue","PVA glue","clamping"],"text":"Loose joints are the most common frame problem. A proper repair means taking the joint apart, cleaning off all the old glue from both surfaces, because new glue does not bond well to old glue, then regluing and clamping until the glue cures. Traditional hide glue is often used on antiques because it is reversible with heat and moisture, which helps future repairs. Modern wood glues, often PVA types, are strong and common on newer frames. Broken or worn dowels are drilled out and replaced. Corner blocks are reglued and screwed. Injecting glue into a loose joint without disassembly is a quick fix but rarely lasts, so shops usually recommend the full repair when the piece is stripped."},{"id":"kb-techniques-007","topic":"techniques","title":"Frame repair: broken rails, cracks and tack rails","aliases":["broken rail","cracked frame","split rail","frame reinforcement","new tack rail","sistering","frame rebuild"],"text":"Broken or cracked frame parts are repaired by gluing and clamping the break, reinforcing it with screws, dowels or a glued wooden splint along its side, sometimes called sistering, or replacing the part entirely with new hardwood. Tack rails that are riddled with old staple holes may be filled, capped with a thin strip of new wood, or replaced so new staples will hold. Arm and back posts that have split are glued and reinforced. Frame repairs happen after stripping and before springs and padding go back in. Because damage is often hidden until stripping, shops usually describe frame repair as a possible extra in their estimate."},{"id":"kb-techniques-008","topic":"techniques","title":"Re-tying eight-way hand-tied springs","aliases":["spring re-tying","retie springs","eight way tie steps","re-tie coils","spring tying steps","hand tie springs"],"text":"Re-tying springs restores a sagging hand-tied seat. The old twine is cut away and the coils checked; bent or weak coils are replaced. The coils are fixed at the bottom to new webbing or steel straps and set in even rows. The upholsterer then ties them with laid cord, front to back first, then side to side, then on both diagonals, so each coil is tied in eight directions. Each row's cord is anchored to the frame with tacks or staples, and the coils are compressed to an even height with a slight crown in the middle. Edge coils are tied to lean slightly outward or to the edge wire. Burlap then goes over the springs, stitched to them."},{"id":"kb-techniques-009","topic":"techniques","title":"Replacing individual coil springs","aliases":["replace coil spring","broken coil","single spring replacement","coil repair","spring patch"],"text":"When only a few coils are broken or have lost height, they can sometimes be replaced individually without re-tying the whole seat. The upholsterer opens the area, cuts the ties around the bad coil, fixes a matching coil of the same height and gauge in its place, and reties it into the surrounding system. This works best when the twine and other coils are still in good condition. If the twine is old and brittle, replacing one coil often leads to more failures nearby, so a full re-tie is usually better value. Shops will explain which option makes sense after opening the seat."},{"id":"kb-techniques-010","topic":"techniques","title":"Sinuous spring repair and replacement","aliases":["sinuous spring repair","S spring replacement","zig zag spring repair","spring clip replacement","sagging sofa repair","re-arc springs"],"text":"Common sinuous spring repairs include replacing broken clips, re-nailing or re-screwing loose clips, replacing broken or missing helical links, and replacing springs that have lost their arc or snapped. Clips are fixed to the front and back rails, the springs are hooked in with the correct arc, and helicals or wire links join them across. Springs should be spaced evenly and matched in gauge. If a rail has split where clips were nailed, the rail is repaired first. A sofa that sags in one spot often has a single broken clip or spring. When most springs have lost their arc, replacing the full set gives a more even seat than patching a few."},{"id":"kb-techniques-011","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing jute webbing","aliases":["webbing installation","how to web a seat","interlaced webbing","webbing pattern","webbing tension"],"text":"Jute webbing is installed in a woven grid across the seat or back. The first strip is tacked or stapled to the back rail with the end folded over, stretched across to the front rail with a webbing stretcher until firm, fixed, then cut and the end folded back and fixed again. Strips are spaced evenly, typically with gaps a bit narrower than the webbing itself on seats. The side-to-side strips are then woven over and under the first set before stretching and fixing. On sprung seats the coils sit on the crossing points of the webbing. Webbing must be tight, but not so tight that it pulls rails out of square."},{"id":"kb-techniques-012","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing elastic webbing","aliases":["elastic webbing install","Pirelli install","rubber webbing installation","webbing clips","stretch percentage"],"text":"Elastic webbing is installed by fixing one end to the rail with staples or a metal clip, stretching it a set percentage of its length and fixing the other end. Many shops mark the unstretched webbing at a measured length and stretch it until the marks line up with the rails, to get even tension on every strap. Stretch recommendations vary by product and by whether it is a seat or back, so shops follow the webbing maker's guidance. Straps may be laid in one direction or woven. On mid-century chairs with grooved rails, clips slot into the grooves. The webbing should be covered with a layer such as burlap or a fabric before foam, so foam does not wear on it."},{"id":"kb-techniques-013","topic":"techniques","title":"Burlap over springs","aliases":["burlap installation","burlap over springs","spring cover burlap","stitching burlap to springs","spring burlap"],"text":"After springs are tied, a layer of heavy burlap is stretched over them to give the padding a stable surface and keep it from falling into the springs. The burlap is fixed to the rails, pulled snugly but without compressing the springs, and then stitched to the top of each coil with a curved needle and twine so it moves with the springs. Over sinuous springs, burlap or an insulator pad is also used so foam does not wear through on the wire. Burlap is also fixed over the inside arms and back as a base for padding. The edges are folded over for strength. Torn or brittle old burlap is always replaced during a rebuild."},{"id":"kb-techniques-014","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing edge roll","aliases":["edge roll installation","how to install edge roll","fox edge install","seat front edge","edge roll placement"],"text":"Edge roll is fitted along edges where padding or fabric would otherwise wear against wood or wire. On a seat, it is stapled along the front rail, or attached to the edge wire, so the roll sits slightly proud of the edge. On arms and backs it can define the outline. The flat burlap flange is stapled or sewn in place, and corners are cut and shaped so the roll turns smoothly. On dining chairs, a small edge roll along the front of the seat board gives a crisp front line and helps prevent the fabric wearing through. Choosing the diameter depends on how pronounced the edge should be."},{"id":"kb-techniques-015","topic":"techniques","title":"Traditional stuffing and stitched edges","aliases":["traditional stuffing steps","building a stitched edge","first stuffing","second stuffing","bridle ties","stuffing ties"],"text":"In traditional upholstery, loose stuffing such as curled hair is held in place with loops of twine called bridle ties, sewn into the burlap. Stuffing is teased into the ties and built up evenly. For a firm edge, scrim or burlap is laid over the first stuffing and tacked down, then the edge is stitched: rows of blind stitches pull stuffing toward the edge, then top stitches form a firm roll. A second, thinner stuffing goes over the top, then cotton or wool padding, then a muslin cover, and finally the show cover. This method takes much longer than foam but builds very durable shapes, and is the standard for quality antique restoration."},{"id":"kb-techniques-016","topic":"techniques","title":"Muslin undercover","aliases":["muslin cover steps","applying muslin","calico cover","undercover installation","first cover"],"text":"A muslin undercover is applied over the stuffing before the final fabric. The upholsterer cuts muslin a little larger than the area, temporarily tacks or staples it at the centres of each side, then works outward, pulling evenly to compress and shape the padding into the final form. Wrinkles are worked out and corners pleated neatly. Once it is smooth and the shape is right, it is fixed permanently. Because the muslin holds the padding, the top fabric can be applied with less strain, which helps delicate or expensive fabrics last and keeps the shape crisp. Muslin is common on quality traditional work and optional on modern foam builds."},{"id":"kb-techniques-017","topic":"techniques","title":"Foam cutting and sizing","aliases":["foam cutting","cutting foam","electric knife foam","foam saw","foam sizing","oversizing foam","foam measurements"],"text":"Foam is cut with an electric carving knife or a foam saw for straight, clean edges, or a bandsaw in larger shops. It is marked with a marker and straightedge. For cushions, foam is usually cut slightly larger than the finished cover, often by around half an inch in width and depth, so the cover is filled tightly and has crown, though the right amount depends on the foam, wrap and shop preference. Bevels and tapers are cut to match cushion shapes. Layers can be glued together to combine firmnesses, such as a firm core with a softer top. Accurate measuring and square cuts make cushions look professional and wear evenly."},{"id":"kb-techniques-018","topic":"techniques","title":"Wrapping foam with polyester","aliases":["wrapping foam","dacron wrap steps","foam wrap","polyester wrap install","wrap cushions","stockinette"],"text":"Most foam cushions are wrapped in bonded polyester, often called Dacron. The wrap is cut to go around the foam, usually over the top, front and bottom, sometimes all four sides, and lightly glued with spray adhesive. This rounds the edges, adds loft and helps the cushion fill its cover. Some shops then slip a thin knit stockinette or a light cloth over the wrapped foam so it slides easily into the cover and the wrap does not shift. For a firmer, crisper look, a thinner wrap is used; for a soft, plump look, a thicker wrap. Wrap compresses over years, so replacing it is part of refreshing older cushions."},{"id":"kb-techniques-019","topic":"techniques","title":"Measuring furniture for fabric","aliases":["measuring for fabric","how to measure a sofa","measurement for upholstery","cut list measuring","panel measurement"],"text":"To work out fabric needs, the upholsterer measures each panel of the piece at its widest and longest points: inside back, outside back, inside arms, outside arms, arm fronts, seat or deck front, cushions, boxing, skirt and welt. Allowances are added for pulling and stapling, tuck-aways and seams, usually a few inches per side. Measurements are listed in a cut list, then laid out on paper or software to the fabric's width to see how many yards are needed. For a quick estimate, shops use rules of thumb based on the type and size of piece. Customers measuring at home should measure overall width, depth and height and count cushions."},{"id":"kb-techniques-020","topic":"techniques","title":"Building a cut list and layout","aliases":["cut list","cutting plan","fabric layout","cutting layout","marker making","layout plan"],"text":"A cut list names each piece to cut, its size and any notes, such as centre the motif, pile direction, or railroad. The pieces are then planned on the fabric width, usually starting with the largest and most visible parts, like the inside back and seat cushions, so they get the best placement. Smaller pieces fill the gaps. Welt is planned as strips from leftover areas. For patterned fabric, each piece's centre line is marked so motifs are aligned. The total length of the layout gives the yardage. A careful layout can save significant fabric, and it is the basis for an accurate quote when the customer supplies their own fabric."},{"id":"kb-techniques-021","topic":"techniques","title":"Pattern matching and centering","aliases":["pattern matching","centering pattern","motif placement","matching stripes","plaid matching","fabric matching"],"text":"With patterned fabric, the main motif is centred on key surfaces, especially the inside back, seat cushions and arm fronts, and patterns should flow continuously down from the inside back, across the seat and down the front. Stripes must line up across cushions and boxing, and plaids must match both ways. Arms are usually cut as mirror images. Each panel is marked with a centre line and cut in order. Matching uses more fabric and more time, which is why patterned fabrics need extra yardage. Very large repeats may not fit well on small surfaces, which can affect fabric choice."},{"id":"kb-techniques-022","topic":"techniques","title":"Railroading vs up the roll in practice","aliases":["railroad vs up the roll","when to railroad","fabric direction decision","turning fabric","seamless sofa back"],"text":"Deciding fabric direction is part of planning. Up-the-roll fabric runs top to bottom, so a long sofa back wider than the fabric must be pieced, usually with seams placed under cushions or hidden behind welt, or centred panels with side panels. Railroaded fabric runs sideways, so the inside back, seat and front can each be one long piece with no seams, which looks cleaner and often uses less fabric on long sofas. Railroading is only possible if the pattern looks right sideways and the pile or texture still reads well. Shops check the fabric's specification before deciding, and the choice affects the yardage estimate."},{"id":"kb-techniques-023","topic":"techniques","title":"Sewing welt","aliases":["sewing welt","making welt","welt strips","cording foot","piping foot","bias welt","welt construction"],"text":"To make welt, fabric is cut into strips, usually on the straight grain for straight runs and on the bias for curves or stripes. Strip width depends on the cord size and seam allowance; shops commonly cut around one and a half inches for standard furniture cord, though they adjust for fabric thickness. Strips are joined end to end with angled seams to reduce bulk, then wrapped around the cord and sewn close to it with a welt or cording foot. Welt is then sewn into seams or stapled directly to frames. Patterned welt should be cut so the pattern looks consistent. The total welt length is added to the cut list."},{"id":"kb-techniques-024","topic":"techniques","title":"Joining and finishing welt ends","aliases":["welt joins","joining welt","welt ends","welt splice","finishing welt","welt overlap"],"text":"Where a run of welt ends or meets itself, such as around a cushion, the join should be neat and placed where it will be least visible, usually at the back. The common method is to open the end of the welt, trim the cord so the two ends butt together, fold the fabric end under and wrap it over the other end, then sew through. On furniture, welt ends are tucked under or into crevices and stapled. Leaving welt ends loose or bulky is a common amateur mistake. On long runs, joins in the strip itself are sewn on an angle so they are less noticeable and less bulky."},{"id":"kb-techniques-025","topic":"techniques","title":"Making a box cushion with zipper","aliases":["box cushion sewing","cushion construction","sewing cushions","zipper boxing","cushion steps","making cushions"],"text":"To make a box cushion, the top and bottom panels are cut to the cushion shape with seam allowances, and the boxing strip is cut to the cushion thickness plus allowances. The zipper is sewn into a separate back boxing section, usually running across the back and around each back corner by a few inches, so the insert slides in easily. Welt is sewn around the top and bottom panels, then the boxing is attached, starting at the back so any joins hide there, with corners clipped and turned carefully. The cover is turned right side out, and the wrapped foam or fill insert is folded and pushed in, then corners are filled and the zipper closed."},{"id":"kb-techniques-026","topic":"techniques","title":"Boxing corners and shaped cushions","aliases":["cushion corners","boxing corners","T-cushion sewing","shaped cushion sewing","inside corners","corner clipping"],"text":"Good cushion corners are square and sharp. On a box cushion, the seam allowance of the boxing is clipped at each corner so it can turn without puckering, and the welt is clipped slightly. On T-cushions, the inside corners where the T steps in are the hardest: the boxing must turn in, and the allowances are clipped right to the stitching line, with extra care so they do not fray. Patterned boxing should match the top panel at the front. Sewing slowly around corners and checking the panel stays square avoids twisted cushions. If corners look empty after the insert goes in, a little extra polyester can be tucked into them."},{"id":"kb-techniques-027","topic":"techniques","title":"Channelling technique","aliases":["channelling steps","how to channel","channel back construction","channel tufting technique","making channels"],"text":"There are two common ways to make channels. In the sewn method, the cover is stitched to a backing in parallel lines to form tubes, which are filled with foam strips or stuffing from one end, then fixed to the frame. In the board method, foam strips are glued to a backing board or the frame with gaps, and fabric is pressed and stapled into each gap, or pulled through slots. On curved chairs, channels are tapered so they fan out evenly. Channels must be equal width and perfectly straight to look right, so layout is marked carefully. Ends are finished with welt or tucked into the frame."},{"id":"kb-techniques-028","topic":"techniques","title":"Diamond tufting layout","aliases":["tufting layout","diamond tufting steps","button spacing","tufting grid","marking tufting","how to tuft"],"text":"Diamond tufting begins with a layout. The upholsterer draws a grid of offset rows on the foam or board, setting horizontal and vertical spacing to suit the piece, so the diamonds fit evenly with partial diamonds at the edges. Holes are cut or drilled at each button point. The fabric gets the same grid but with extra spacing added between points, to allow for the depth of each tuft and the folds. Working from the centre outward, buttons are pulled through with twine and tied off. The excess fabric between buttons is folded into neat pleats, all facing the same way, usually downward. The edges are then pleated out to the frame."},{"id":"kb-techniques-029","topic":"techniques","title":"Biscuit and blind tufting technique","aliases":["biscuit tufting steps","blind tufting steps","square tufting method","buttonless tufting technique"],"text":"Biscuit tufting uses a square grid, with rows directly aligned instead of offset. The cover can be sewn into squares on a backing and filled, or pulled down at each grid point with twine and buttons, with little or no pleating between them. Each square should puff up evenly. Blind tufting uses the same anchoring methods without a visible button: the twine is passed through a small hidden tuft of fabric or stitched so it disappears into the dimple, or the fabric is sewn in a way that forms the dimple. Both need accurate layout and even pull, because unevenness is very visible on these clean, geometric styles."},{"id":"kb-techniques-030","topic":"techniques","title":"Pleating corners and arm fronts","aliases":["pleating","corner pleats","arm front pleats","fan pleats","mitred corner","knife pleat","pleat folding"],"text":"Where fabric has to wrap a rounded or squared corner, the excess is folded into pleats. On rounded roll arms, the fabric is gathered at the arm front into evenly spaced fan pleats radiating from a centre point, or the arm is finished with a separate panel or facing. On square corners of seats, ottomans and headboards, the fabric is usually folded into a single clean pleat or a mitre, with the fold placed so it faces down or to the back. Pairs of corners, such as the two front corners of a seat, should be pleated as mirror images. Neat, consistent pleats are one of the clearest signs of skilled upholstery."},{"id":"kb-techniques-031","topic":"techniques","title":"Applying nailhead trim","aliases":["nailhead application","how to apply nailheads","installing decorative nails","nail spacing","nailhead line"],"text":"For individual nailheads, the upholsterer first marks a straight or evenly curved line, often with a tape guide or a spacing tool. Nails are placed either touching each other or spaced at regular intervals, then hammered in with a nylon or leather-faced hammer so the heads are not dented. Some start each nail with a small pilot or set tool to keep it straight. On curves, spacing is adjusted so the line flows. Nailhead strip is quicker: it is laid along the line and nailed through the real nail holes every few heads, with separate matching nails used to finish ends and corners. Frames must have solid wood under the line for nails to hold."},{"id":"kb-techniques-032","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing inside arms","aliases":["inside arm installation","upholstering arms","inside arm steps","arm upholstery","arm padding"],"text":"After the deck and seat are prepared, inside arms are padded with foam and batting over burlap or a solid panel, then covered. The fabric is fixed at the top of the arm or over the roll, pulled down and tucked through the crevice between arm and deck, and stapled to the rail below. At the back, it is cut and tucked around the back post. At the front, it is pleated or pulled around to the arm front for a facing or panel. Both arms are done as mirror images, with patterns matching. The outside arms are added later, after the inside back, closing the piece."},{"id":"kb-techniques-033","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing inside back","aliases":["inside back installation","upholstering the back","inside back steps","back padding","back cover"],"text":"The inside back is padded according to its style: smooth foam and batting, tufting, channelling, or a traditional stuffed back. The cover is centred, fixed at the top first, then pulled down and through the crevice at the deck, and stapled to the back rail. Sides are cut and tucked around the arms, with clips made so the fabric turns cleanly. For loose pillow backs, the inside back is a simpler panel with less padding, since the pillows provide comfort. Patterns are centred and aligned with the seat. The top edge may be finished with welt, ready for the outside back to be attached."},{"id":"kb-techniques-034","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing outside arms and outside back","aliases":["outside back installation","outside arm installation","closing a sofa","back tacking","outside panels","finishing outside back"],"text":"Outside arms and the outside back are among the last panels installed. The top edge is usually back-tacked with cardboard tack strip so it looks crisp and has no visible staples. A layer of cardboard, burlap or padding is often stapled to the frame first so the fabric does not sink into hollows. The fabric is pulled down and stapled under the bottom rail. Sides are closed with flexible metal tack strip, ply-grip, or hand slip-stitching, often with welt along the edge. Patterns on outside panels are aligned with the inside panels where they show. A dust cover is then fixed under the base."},{"id":"kb-techniques-035","topic":"techniques","title":"Making and attaching a skirt","aliases":["skirt installation","sewing a skirt","attaching skirt","skirt lining","skirt construction"],"text":"A skirt is sewn as a separate piece: the face fabric is cut to the finished height plus allowances, lined, and pleated according to style, with pleat underlays at corners. Welt is usually sewn along the top edge. The skirt is attached to the piece at a marked line, often by back-tacking with cardboard strip so the top edge looks clean, or by sewing it to the body fabric. It should hang straight, with an even gap to the floor, usually around half an inch, so it does not drag. Patterns must match across the skirt, especially at pleats. Accurate measuring around the base is essential so pleats land on corners."},{"id":"kb-techniques-036","topic":"techniques","title":"Installing a dust cover","aliases":["dust cover install","cambric installation","bottom cover","finishing underside","cambric steps"],"text":"The dust cover is the last step. Cambric is cut slightly larger than the base, folded under at the edges, and stapled to the bottom of the frame, working from the centre of each side outward so it is taut and smooth. It is cut neatly around legs and corner blocks. Any legs or glides that were removed are refitted after. On recliners and sleepers, the cover is installed so it does not interfere with the mechanism. A neat dust cover is a small sign of care; customers sometimes look under a finished piece. Stitch can explain that dust covers can be replaced on their own if torn."},{"id":"kb-techniques-037","topic":"techniques","title":"Cushion fill options","aliases":["cushion fill options","cushion filling choices","what to fill cushions with","foam vs down","cushion types","seat cushion options"],"text":"Common seat cushion fills are: high-density foam wrapped in polyester, firm and low-maintenance; high-resilience foam, more lively and durable; foam with a down-and-feather envelope, softer top with structure; spring-core cushions, bouncy with foam and down; and all-down or feather-blend cushions, very soft but needing frequent plumping. Back cushions are often blown polyester fibre, down blends or fibre-and-foam combinations. Fill choice affects comfort, look, maintenance and price, and customers with feather allergies should avoid down. Shops can usually show samples or demo cushions so customers can feel the difference before choosing."},{"id":"kb-techniques-038","topic":"techniques","title":"Refreshing cushions without reupholstering","aliases":["cushion refresh","new foam only","re-stuffing cushions","refill cushions","cushion re-filling","replace cushion inserts"],"text":"Many sofas feel tired because of the cushions, not the frame or fabric. If the cover fabric is in good shape, a shop can replace just the inserts: new foam, new polyester wrap, or new down fill for loose cushions. Back pillows filled with fibre can be refilled or have fibre replaced. This is quicker and less expensive than full reupholstery. The shop measures the existing covers, makes new inserts to fit, and stuffs them back in through the zippers. If covers have shrunk or stretched, inserts may need adjusting. Customers often notice a big difference in comfort and look from new inserts alone."},{"id":"kb-techniques-039","topic":"techniques","title":"Reupholstering a dining chair slip seat","aliases":["slip seat recovering","recover dining chair seat","dining seat steps","drop-in seat recovering","DIY dining chair"],"text":"To recover a slip seat, unscrew it from under the chair, remove old fabric and staples, and check the seat board and padding. Replace flattened foam with new foam cut to the board, often with a polyester wrap. Lay the new fabric face down, place the seat on top, centring any pattern, and staple the centre of each side first, pulling evenly, then work outward toward the corners. Fold the corners neatly into pleats, trim excess and staple a dust cover underneath. Check the seat still fits back in the frame, since thicker fabric or padding can make it too tight. Screw it back in place."},{"id":"kb-techniques-040","topic":"techniques","title":"Building an upholstered headboard","aliases":["headboard steps","making a headboard","DIY headboard","headboard construction","upholstered headboard method"],"text":"A headboard starts with a board cut to the bed width and the desired height, from plywood or MDF, shaped if needed. For tufting, holes are drilled on the layout. Foam is glued to the face, sometimes wrapped around the edges, then covered with batting. Fabric is laid over, centred and stapled to the back, starting at the centre of each side and pulling evenly, with corners pleated or mitred. Tufts are pulled through and tied. The back may be covered with a cambric or fabric panel. Legs, brackets or a wall cleat are added. Welt or nailheads can be applied around the edge."},{"id":"kb-techniques-041","topic":"techniques","title":"Recovering an ottoman","aliases":["ottoman recovering","reupholster ottoman","ottoman steps","footstool recovering","storage ottoman recover"],"text":"Recovering an ottoman starts by removing legs and the old dust cover, stripping fabric, and checking padding and any springs. The top is padded with foam and batting, often with extra crown. A sewn cover with boxing and welt can be made for a crisp tailored look, or the fabric can be pulled over and pleated at corners for a simpler finish. Tufted tops follow the tufting layout steps. Skirts or nailheads may be added. For storage ottomans, the lid and box are upholstered separately and the hinges reattached, with lid stays to stop it slamming. Ottomans are good beginner projects and economical jobs for a shop."},{"id":"kb-techniques-042","topic":"techniques","title":"Making a tailored slipcover","aliases":["slipcover making","tailored slipcover steps","loose cover making","slipcover construction","fitted slipcover"],"text":"A custom slipcover is made by fitting fabric on the piece itself. Panels are pinned inside out directly on the sofa or chair, following each panel of the frame, with seams placed where the original upholstery seams are. The pinned panels are trimmed with allowances, marked, removed and sewn, usually with welt, and finished with zippers or ties at the back or side so it can be removed. A separate cover is made for each cushion. The skirt is added last. Fabrics should be pre-washed if the cover will be laundered. A good slipcover looks tailored rather than baggy, and fits tightly around arms and the back."},{"id":"kb-techniques-043","topic":"techniques","title":"Upholstering in leather","aliases":["leather upholstery technique","working with leather","leather piecing","leather cutting","hide layout"],"text":"Leather is cut from whole or half hides, not from a roll, so pieces are planned around each hide's shape, natural marks, scars and thinner areas. The best, most even leather goes on the most visible surfaces like seats and inside backs; the flanks and edges are used for outside panels or hidden parts. Large panels may need seams because a single hide is not big enough. Leather is sewn with leather needles, does not forgive mistakes because needle holes are permanent, and stretches differently in different directions. Staples go only where they will be hidden. Leather jobs usually take more labour than fabric, and waste from irregular shapes is higher."},{"id":"kb-techniques-044","topic":"techniques","title":"Working with vinyl","aliases":["vinyl upholstery technique","working with vinyl","vinyl stretching","heat gun vinyl","vinyl seams"],"text":"Vinyl is popular for kitchen chairs, restaurant booths, boats, cars and medical seating. It stretches, especially when warm, which helps it wrap curves; a heat gun can soften it for tight corners, carefully, so it does not scorch or go shiny. Because needle and staple holes are permanent, layouts must be right the first time. Seam allowances are smaller, and long stitch lengths reduce perforation that can tear. Welt in vinyl gives crisp edges. Vinyl does not breathe, so on seats some shops add breathing vents or use perforated vinyl for comfort. Cold vinyl can crack when folded, so shops work in a warm space."},{"id":"kb-techniques-045","topic":"techniques","title":"Upholstering curved and round pieces","aliases":["curved upholstery","round chair","curved sofa","barrel back technique","wrapping curves","curved panels"],"text":"Curved pieces need fabric to bend smoothly without wrinkles. Techniques include cutting panels on the bias where suitable, easing fabric by stretching it slightly as it is fixed, cutting relief clips in the seam allowance, and splitting large curves into several tapered panels or channels. Curved tack strip or hand stitching closes the outside. Stripes and patterns must follow the curve gracefully, which may mean seams in the middle of a back. Stretchy fabrics are easier on curves than stiff ones. Curved sofas and round chairs usually take more fabric and labour than straight-sided pieces of a similar size."},{"id":"kb-techniques-046","topic":"techniques","title":"Reupholstery job sequence overview","aliases":["reupholstery steps","reupholstery process","order of upholstery","job sequence","how reupholstery works","upholstery workflow"],"text":"A typical full reupholstery job runs in this order: assess and quote; order fabric; strip the old upholstery while documenting it; repair and reglue the frame; repair or replace springs and webbing; replace burlap and padding; then cover the deck and seat front, inside arms, inside back, outside arms, outside back, and arm facings. Then come the skirt, trims and nailheads; the dust cover; legs and glides; and cushions are made alongside. A final inspection checks lines, pattern matching and finish. Not every job uses every step: a cushion refresh or slip seat only uses a few, and a tight-back modern piece may skip some."},{"id":"kb-techniques-047","topic":"techniques","title":"Yardage rules of thumb: overview","aliases":["yardage estimate","how much fabric","fabric estimate","yardage chart","rule of thumb yardage","fabric needed"],"text":"Upholsterers use rules of thumb to estimate fabric before measuring a piece properly. These are rough guides only, assume plain fabric about 54 inches wide, and must be verified by the shop with real measurements. Actual yardage depends on the piece's size and style, number of cushions, welt and trims, skirt, tufting, whether the fabric can be railroaded, and the pattern repeat. Patterned fabric, velvets and other directional fabrics need more. It is normal for a shop to add some extra for safety, since running short can mean a different dye lot. Stitch should give these ranges only as approximate guidance and always say the shop will confirm the exact amount."},{"id":"kb-techniques-048","topic":"techniques","title":"Yardage rule of thumb: sofas and loveseats","aliases":["sofa yardage","how much fabric for a sofa","loveseat yardage","sectional yardage","couch fabric amount"],"text":"As a rough guide for plain, 54-inch fabric, a standard three-cushion sofa often needs somewhere around 14 to 18 yards, and a loveseat around 10 to 13 yards. Sofas with many loose back pillows, skirts, tufting, or very large sizes can need more, while a simple tight-back sofa without a skirt may need less. Sectionals vary enormously and are best measured piece by piece. These are approximate shop rules of thumb, not exact amounts, and they assume a plain fabric. For patterns, velvets or large repeats, add more. Stitch should always say the shop will confirm the exact yardage after measuring or seeing the piece."},{"id":"kb-techniques-049","topic":"techniques","title":"Yardage rule of thumb: chairs","aliases":["chair yardage","armchair yardage","wingback yardage","club chair fabric","accent chair fabric","how much fabric for a chair"],"text":"For plain 54-inch fabric, rough shop rules of thumb are: a typical armchair or club chair with one cushion often needs around 6 to 9 yards; a wingback around 7 to 9 yards; a slipper chair or small accent chair around 4 to 6 yards; and an open-arm French chair, where only seat and back are upholstered, often around 2 to 4 yards. Larger chairs, skirts, tufting and patterned fabrics add more. These are only approximate starting points that vary with the individual chair, and the shop must confirm actual yardage. Customers buying their own fabric should wait for the shop's figure before ordering."},{"id":"kb-techniques-050","topic":"techniques","title":"Yardage rule of thumb: dining chairs, ottomans, cushions","aliases":["dining chair yardage","slip seat fabric","ottoman yardage","cushion yardage","bench fabric","headboard yardage"],"text":"Rough rules of thumb for plain 54-inch fabric: a typical slip seat needs about half a yard, though two small seats can sometimes be cut from one yard, depending on size. A dining chair with an upholstered back often needs around one to two yards. Ottomans range from about one to four yards depending on size, skirt and tufting. A single box seat cushion often needs about one and a half to two yards; a queen headboard often around two to four yards depending on height and tufting. These are approximate, assume plain fabric and must be confirmed by the shop. Patterns and tufting increase the amount."},{"id":"kb-techniques-051","topic":"techniques","title":"Yardage adjustments for patterns, nap and repeats","aliases":["pattern yardage","extra yardage pattern","repeat allowance","velvet yardage","stripe yardage","plaid yardage"],"text":"Patterned and directional fabrics need more yardage than plains because each piece must be centred and matched, and fabric cannot be turned to fit. Shops commonly add roughly 10 to 25 percent for patterns, sometimes more for large repeats, but the right figure comes from the actual repeat size and a layout of the pieces. Velvets and other pile fabrics usually need extra because every piece must run in the same direction. Plaids matched both ways need the most. Railroadable plains can sometimes reduce yardage on long sofas. These adjustments are rules of thumb only; Stitch should tell customers the shop will calculate the exact amount for their chosen fabric."},{"id":"kb-techniques-052","topic":"techniques","title":"Estimating leather","aliases":["leather estimate","how much leather","square feet leather","hides needed","leather yardage conversion","hide size"],"text":"Leather is sold by the hide or half hide and priced by the square foot, not by the yard. Hide sizes vary, but a full cow hide is commonly somewhere in the range of about 45 to 55 square feet, and a half hide, or side, around half that. Because hides are irregular and have marks, shops add significant waste allowance, often 20 to 30 percent or more. A rough conversion some shops use is that one yard of 54-inch fabric equals about 13.5 square feet, then add waste. These are approximate rules of thumb; the shop should confirm how many hides a piece needs, especially for large or tufted leather furniture."},{"id":"kb-techniques-053","topic":"techniques","title":"Fabric for cushions only","aliases":["cushion cover fabric","recover cushions only","new cushion covers","cushion only yardage","replace cushion covers"],"text":"Some customers want only their cushions recovered, for example to replace worn seat cushion covers. This is possible, but the new fabric will differ from the rest of the piece unless it is the same fabric from the same dye lot, which is rarely available after a few years. Even the same fabric can look different because the old fabric has faded. Shops often suggest recovering the whole piece, choosing a deliberate contrast for the cushions, or making new covers for all cushions plus arm caps. For outdoor and window seat cushions, which are standalone, recovering cushions only is common and straightforward."},{"id":"kb-techniques-054","topic":"techniques","title":"Changing the style during reupholstery","aliases":["change style","modify sofa","add tufting","remove skirt","change arms","convert cushions","update look"],"text":"Reupholstery is a chance to change a piece's look. Easier changes include new fabric, adding or removing welt, adding or removing a skirt, adding nailheads, changing legs, adding or removing tufting on a tight back, and changing cushion fill. Moderate changes include converting a loose pillow back to a tight back or the reverse, or changing a T-cushion to a box cushion. Bigger changes, like altering arm shape or seat depth, mean frame carpentry. Each change affects labour and fabric needs. Stitch should note the customer's ideas and let the shop advise what is practical for that frame, rather than promising any change."},{"id":"kb-techniques-055","topic":"techniques","title":"Making seats firmer or softer","aliases":["firmer seat","softer seat","seat comfort","cushion firmness","too soft sofa","too hard sofa","comfort adjustment"],"text":"Comfort can usually be adjusted during reupholstery. To make a seat firmer, a shop can use higher-firmness foam, add a firmer core, replace weak springs, or add support over the springs. To make it softer, they can use a softer foam or a softer top layer, add a down wrap, or use a thicker polyester wrap. Seat height and depth change slightly with cushion thickness, which matters for people who find it hard to stand from a low sofa. Shops can often show foam samples so customers can feel the difference. Stitch should ask how the customer finds the piece now and what they want, then pass that to the shop."},{"id":"kb-techniques-056","topic":"techniques","title":"Quality checks before delivery","aliases":["quality check","final inspection","finishing check","QC upholstery","delivery check"],"text":"Before a piece goes back to the customer, a good shop checks it carefully: welt lines straight and even; patterns centred and matched; pleats neat and matching on both sides; no visible staples; tufts even and buttons secure; cushions full with square corners and zippers at the back; skirt hanging straight at an even height; no loose threads, marks or glue spots; show wood clean and undamaged; the piece stable and level; and the dust cover neat. Lint and dust are vacuumed off. Shops may photograph the finished piece for their records and portfolio, with the customer's permission."},{"id":"kb-techniques-057","topic":"techniques","title":"Recovering vs reupholstering","aliases":["recover vs reupholster","recovering only","full reupholstery","partial reupholstery","what's the difference","re-cover"],"text":"People use recovering and reupholstering loosely. Strictly, recovering means putting new fabric on over the existing padding and springs, with only minor repairs, while full reupholstery means stripping down to the frame, repairing it, and rebuilding springs, padding and cover. Recovering is quicker and cheaper but only makes sense when the inside is in good condition. If the piece sags or the padding has broken down, new fabric on top will not fix it and may look poor quickly. Most shops assess the inside before advising. Stitch should not promise a cheaper recover-only price until staff have confirmed the insides are sound."}];
